        <p>open market for tne trade of the world. It suggests that his declaration may be "in some respects a somewhat too absolute statement of the intentions of the Government." Sir Michael Hicks Beach is, it is true, the bald member of the Cabinet who speaks out what lois colleagues are content to think; but, after all,his words are merely the logical conse- quence of the policy outlined by Mr. Balfour and endorsed by Lord Kimberley. It is useless for Great Britain to lay down the law, and then to shrink from carrying its views into effect. There have been so many "gracetul concessions" of late that it is well for foreign Powers to understand clearly that in regard to the commercial freedom of China we do not intend to give way. If this fact is known, they will be le-.s likely to put our patience to the test.</p>
        <p>--- The vital statistics for the Society of Friends have been compiled for the denominational year. In 1897 there were 276 deaths of members of the Society in Great Britain and Ireland. This is a slightly higher death-rate than in the previous year, but is much below the average death-rate. A re- markable feature is the large number of deaths at advanced ages. Of the 276 there were not fewer than 46 between 80 and 90 years of age at death, and seven over 90 year, so that nearly one-fifth of the deaths were of persons over 80 years of age.</p>
        <p>It is not a little curious and interesting that, des- pite the distance between them. India continues to ba the only reliable recruiting ground for the agri- cultural labour required in British Guiana. It seems that daring last year 2,380 nien,women,and children were brought as immigrants into the colony, and this notwithstanding the fact that within 400 miles the island of Barbadoes is actually facing the pro- blem of over-population. Despite all efforts by in- dividual importers of labour, and by the Govern- ments of the two colonies,it has been found imprac- ticable to establish anything but a most desultory and fitful immigration from Barbadoes into Guiana. Many of the labourers seem to earn their money and return to their beloved India. for it appears that in the course of last year 2,059 of them, in- cluding children,returned to Cal eutta,carrying with them jevrellery to the value of .£1,458, as apart from a sum of .£14,473 which they deposited for remit- tance.</p>
        <p>it is uncomfortsble to learn, on the authority of an old member of the London Fire Brigade, that gangs of incendiaries are organized to make a liv- ing out of frauds on the insurance companies. This avocation, it seems, is not at all new in the history of the metropolis, since the police have succeeded within living memory in laying hands on many men who pursued it. Bat the "Daily News " has been informed by a correspondent that at the pre- sent moment tilers is a company of" lawless men whose nefarious purpose is to at fire to the pre- mises of dishonest persons, and for purpose of gain," and that the leader of this band is a " person who, Ihaving had himself several fires, was a short time ago refused payment by the insurance offices."</p>
        <p>THE DESTROYER AND THE lIEALER. The earl ivas alone. Th 6 *wiatry light streame&amp;. into the great llbrary, whicli Was bright, warrr and luxurious. Upon a. writing-desk lay- a half.. finished letter, the ink not yet dry. Lord Hawk- hurst had bee arrested in his task by a sudden spasm of weakness that had sant him staggering to his couch. t Mrs. Dominick came hito the room, followed by Beryl. "Are you worse, my Lord ? ' asked the house- keeper, approaching bun. "Shall I send for the doctor? Shall I call Mrs: Conyers ?'' "No, no,'' gasped the earl. "I shall be all right directly. It's that cursed inedieine—l burn alive eveytimei take it. The infernal do'.tor-L-" He paused, growing ghastly pale. Beryl s quick' eyes detected a c,trafe of iced water on the table. She poured some of the liquid in a goblet and brought it to the earl, placing it to his lips. He 'thank it unhesitatingly, revived, and stared at lier with returniag strength. " My.Lord,' said Mrs. 'Dominick, "this is Mrs. Park, a curate's widow., who may he able to please you mid attend upon you for a week. 'dr so till-your Lordship gets out again. I can roe omend her in the very big est terms.' Beryl retreated a little. The earl sat up and looked at her, keenly. "Copy that portion of a letter on my desk, Mrs. Park," he Said. Beryl obeyed. The handwriting, clear and dis- tinct, appeared to ple .ise him, for he uttered an approving -" "Let me hear you read," commanded the earl. Beryl took up a volume of history upon the table, and read a paragraph. "Humph" said the earl again. "You'll do. You can stay, madam. I suppose you can do what I you are told th do. lam not so well as I thought. want you to give me my medicines henceforth, and to be at hand when I want you. You can take off your bonnet and finish reading that chapter aloud which you began." "I'll take your bonnet to my room, Mrs. Park," said Mrs. Dominick, well pleased that Beryl's ser- vices had been accepted and herself consequently relieved of many additional cares that had pressed sorely upon her. "By and by I'll show you up to your room. Shall I take any word to Miss Cavanagh ?" "No," said Beryl, removing her hat, veil and shawl. "I have no word to send except that lam grateful to her and to you." Mrs. Dominick went away with Beryl's cuter garments, and the girl took her seat at a convenient distance from the earl and read aloud to him as he had requested. Her voice, low and sweet, clear and distinct, thrilled him like music heard long ago. He watched her in a species of fascination, as she read through her spectacles, the glass of which was ordinary window-glass, nothing more. There were tears in his frosty blue eyes as he listened, and he wiped them away furtively. Her voice reminded him keenly of a voice that had long ago been hushed in death. In the pause between the paragraphs Beryl now and then stole a glance at him. She believed him to be her own father. She had come here in dis- guise, hating him with a bitter, furious hatred, as one who had wronged her mother and driven that mother to her death. But she could not look upon him and hate bins still. His massive head, his grand figure, his rough hewn features, his look of conscious Power, were all full of a subtle msg. netism that drew her liking in spite of herself. She could not wonder that poor Jenny Star had abandoned her kindred for his sake. She felt that she could love him, and struggled against the out- going warmth and tenderness of her heart as if it had been a crime to love him, as if love for him implied disrespect to the memory of her dead mother. She felt an impulse to go to him, to kneel down beside his couch, to call hilt rather and implore his love and protection • but she scorned herself for the impulse, and told herself that she must be going mad. . She finished reading and lapsed iato silence. "I have to take my medicine every two hours," said Lord Hawkhurst. "Is it two o'clock ?•' Beryl looked toward the mantel-piece. The little ormolu clock was on the stroke of two. "It is two o'clock," she said, and the silvery chimes corroborated her assertion. "The medicines are on the cabinet yonder," said Lord Hawkhurst. "The tall phial with the double label contains my medicine. You can pour it into the goblet beside it." Beryl went to the cabinet. The phial indicated contained a clear, colourless liquid like water, desti- tute of sediment. "One teaspoonful," said the earl. The teaspoon lay on a tiny silver tray at hand. Beryl was about to take it up when the door opened and Octavia entered the room. Beryl's hand trembled so that the spoon rattled against the glass, and she averted her face instinc- tively. Octavio., dressed in an elaborately embroidered dark •blue cashmere costume, had nearly crossed the floor before she detected Beryl's presence. Then she stopped short, with a look of surprise on her dark, unlovely face. "It is actually two o'clock 1" she observed, in a high, affected tone. "After all, I am prompt, Lord Hawkhurst. I am iu time to give you your medicine." The earl frowned. Her voice grated harshly upon his ears. " Mrs. Conyers," he said, quietly, "the lady is my attendant, Mrs. Park. Mrs. Park, let me pre- sent you to Mrs. Conyers." Octavia bowed superciliously. That she was not a lady in grain as in outward appearance was apparent in her treatment of one she believed to be her social inferior. "Oh ! a nurse," she said, looking at Beryl in- solently. "Why did you not send for me, any lord, to negotiate with this person? Dominick is well enough in her place, but she is not competent to select a nurse for you." "I engaged her myself, Octavia," said the earl. "Mrs. Park is a lady, and I desire her to be treated with respect." Octavia flashed a look of dislike at Beryl. She could not penetrate the girl's disguise, but she dis- liked the new-comer instinctively. "So long as Mrs. Park conducts herself pro- perly, she will be treated with respect," said Octavia, carelessly. These medicines are very powerful, and the doctor has enjoined the utmost care in dealing them out. It is a task that should not be intrusted to a stranger. I have measured out all his lordship's medicines heretofore, and shall continue to do so, although you may adminis- ter them." "I think I need not trouble you furtber,Oetavia," said the earl. "Mrs. Park can measure out the medicine as exactly as you." Oetavia's features worked in seeming grief. She looked sorely wounded and hurt. "Oh," said she, "may I not feel that in one thing at least lam indispensable to you? Is there not one thing I can do better for you than any one else, my lord? I love you as if you were my own father'—" "I beg your pardon, Octavia," said the earl, as her voice broke down, and she put her hand- kerchief to her eyes. "You can measure my medicines, if doing so gives you any pleasure. I am glad you are so fond of me and solicitous of my welfare, I am sure." Mrs. Conyers recovered her equanimity very rapidly. "Be good enough to bring the water, Mrs. Park," she said. "It is necessary to dilute the medicine."</p>
        <p>Taken as a whole, the movement to induce the owners of estates in Ireland to spend part of the autumn and winter there in regarded as being suc- cessful. There have been fewer of the big houses empty during the last few months, there have been some excellent shooting and hunting parties, and guests from England who are beginning to return to town have come back delighted with everything in Ireland but the rain. The financial and political result of this voluntary attempt to break through absenteeism has been pretty satisfactory, and it will be repeated during the next Parliamentary re- cess, if it should not be sensational in its political character and possibilities.</p>
        <p>-- It is rather strange that no details are published about General Sir Henry flavelock-Allan's wounds. or the situatioa where the body was found, or whether any robbery of his money or belongings hal .taken place. That he was in plain clothes at the time is all the new information that has been permitted to cotnr nut, with the explanation that the escort was vainly trying to reach him. There may he reasons for withholding details which must be known by this time this relatives.</p>
