      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>THERE are many ways to convince a man, but the easiest is to agree with him. A NEW suit of clothes inspires some men with great confidence in themselves. COMMON sense is not in the same class as genius, but it often gets more solid comfort out of life. IT is easier for a woman to be vain without being pretty than to be pretty without being vain. THE difficulty with the theoretical reformer is that, as generally constituted, he expects a cobbler to shoe a horse.</p>
        <p>THERE are plenty of people whose virtues are like certain trees; they blossom regularly enough but bear no fruit. _</p>
        <p>- - QUESTION FOR QUESTION. —She: "Why does a woman take a man's name when she marries him?" He: Why does she take everything else he's got?" Is. you want a man to think you are smart, you have only to make him think he is smart. " SOME are born great;" and, in the majority of cases, it w4s the only chance they had. PHILOSOPHY is that which enables a rich man to say that there is no disgrace in being poor. SARCASM is a powerful weapon, but in handling it many people take hold of the blade instead of the handle. ACCOUNTED FOR. —She : "Strange what awful talkers some men are." He: "Not at all. They have only inherited their mother-tongue." A SPANISH proverb says, "The man who on his wedding-day starts as a lieutenant in the family will never get promoted." HolgEsTv UNS URPASS ED. "He seems to be absolutely truthful and honest." " Why ?" "Tie admits that he bought the cycle he has because it was cheap." HONOURS E EN. —He : "If there is anything a woman enjoys it is being a martyr."—She: "And how willing some man is to help her enjoy herself that way." THE WOMAN OF Ir.—He: "Have you any reason for doubting what I say?" She: "Yes, I have." He: "What is it?" She: "I don't</p>
        <p>believe you." ANSWERED. "Do you believe in women lecturers?" the man in the fur-cap was asking. The mau with a thin chest produced one of his apologetic coughs. " Sh—speak easy," he said nervously; "that's my wife in the seat opposite." WHAT llu Sato.—" Is it true that you said that Jobson here had stolen your purse? "I did not go Bo far as to pay that, yoiir worship. All I said was, that if Jobson had not assisted me in look- ing for the purse, I might have found it." "Osn of the hardest things in making a speech," said the old campaigner, "is to say just what you mean." " Yes,' was the reply, "that's pretty hard. But once in a while it's a good deal harder to mean just what you say." GAVE IT Up.—Youngun : "You have written some stories in which the people stutter, haven't you ?" Oldun " Yes, lots." " Youngun : "Well, how would you make a stutterer say pneumatic— P-P-P or N-N-N ?" Too WILL.—" What ! you refuse me, half-a- sovereign—me, your intimate friend, whom you once called your second ego !" " Ah, my dear boy, I know myself too well—you would never return the money." GRATIFIED.—" I came across one of your old letters to-day, George, in which you said you would rather be in endless torment with me than in bliss by yourself," said Mrs. Naggin to her husband.—" Well, my dear, and haven't I had my wish!" said the brute. A Sovr PROPOSAL.—EtheI: What did George say when he proposed?" Maud "He said nothing, gasped, turned deathly pale, and fainted quite away. Of course I knew what that meant, so when he came to, I told him he might ask ' papa." Ethel: "And then?" Maud: "Poor 1 George fainted away again " "MY friend, excuse me, but is that whiskey you're drinking?" "It is that." " And how much, may I ask, did you pay for that bottle of it "Two shillings." "Two shillings ! Inever spent two shillings in my life for liquor." "You ain't the only one by a good many, my man, that sponges for his drinks, but you ain't going to get any of this, so I tell you." ACCORDING TO ORDETS.—New servant: "There's a man below to see you, sir." Master of the house: "What did you tell Mm?" New servant: "I told him you told me if it was a lady, to say you were in; and if it was a man, to say you were out." Master of the house; "Well, and what did he say then?" New servant: "He said I was to tell you that he was a lady." UNDOUBTED.—" Don't talk to me about culture and refinement," said a city magnate who had been 4putting on a great deal of style because he had made a little money in stocks. "What is your objection to a man of culture?" asked the gentle- man with whom he was conversing. "They are so stuck up, and are always trying to make you feel their superiority. Now, I never make people feel my culture and refinement. You can be with me 'for 20 years and never know that I have any," "Quite so," replied his listener. His NATURAL BENT.—Fond Father: "If that 'boy of mine has any particular bent, I can't find it." Philosopher: "What experiments have you made to find out?" "Very thorough ones. I gave him a toy printing press, a steam engine, a box of paints, a chest of tools, and a lot of other things, carefully selected to find out whether his tastes Were literary, mechanical, artistic, com- mercial, or what, and I know no more than I did 'before." "What did he do with them?" "Smashed them all up." " Ah, I see. He is evidently born to be a furnitnre</p>
        <p>N Ofi.EDIC&amp;Is rust iiOutsF. vi Art LIMA- L'o"UMPTIuN AND COUGHS, was ever attended with such ape, dy and unfail- ing sueoesa as DR. LOCOCY'S PULMOZZIC WaYERe. In every newspaper and periodical may 134 seen testimonials of their wonderful &amp;Beal. Nothing else gives such l. sound. Is- freshtng night's rest. In byaterie,al, nervous, and heazt 001 u. plaints, they are unfailing, while in rheumatic and nervous pains they act like a charm. To singers bud public spesk,rs they are invaluable for clonzing and strengthening the voice. They have a pleasant taste. Is. ltd. 24. 9d., 46. bd.. and Us. -..abos. Sold by all modlolne vendOrs.—eldlot. ()</p>
