      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>-OAt'ITAL:" CITA`i%</head>
        <p>-OAt'ITAL:" CITA`i%</p>
        <p>The news that England is willing to assist China to liquidate the Japanese indemnity, by providing' a loan of £12,000,000 on certain conditions,has been generally well received, and is certainly popular in the City, where people do not know what on earth to do with their money. Cautious politicians will naturally await the details of the scheme before signifying their entire approval of it, and the sub- ject must be fully debated in Parliament on the Bill to give the Government the necessary powers to raise the money and make the advance. Should the proposition receive parliamentary sanction, it will be by far the biggest thing of the kind the nation has attempted. We have joined with other countries in guaranteeing one or more foreign loans We ourselves purchased a considerable holding of Suez Canal shares, and we made same time in the fifties, during the Crimean War, a loan of two mil- lions to the King of Sardinia, of which more than three parts has been repaid. The last is, I conceive, the nearest modern precedent to what is now pro- posed. Economi,ts will however, ask one question. It is—lf we make this great advance In toe interest of China,where are we oo stop? We have been pay- ing off debt steadily during the last few years, but what will that a ,ail if, with tile ther hand, we pilatp ntw liabilities ?</p>
        <p>It is known that Lord Wolseley emphatically disapproved of the frequent issue of medals in con- nection with campaigns that in other days would certainly not have had such recognition. The Com- mander-in-Chief bas, however,readily expressed his concurrence in the proposal to issue to the men en- gaged in the Tint campaign the new Indian frontier medal, which is the same which was is.ued in con- nection with the Cnitral operations.</p>
        <p>-- I hear from Englishm-n having concessions in Madagascar that they are experiencing great diffi- culties in all their business affairs with the French Republic. Some people who have claims against the French Governmene—claims which are more or less admitted to be good—have for many months! past been unable to obtain even an acknowledg- ment of their letters. In despair they are now turning to toe Government of the United States to discover it it will interfere and help them to a settlement.</p>
        <p>In setting about the business of selecting a Liberal candidate, to be in readiness for any emer- gency, the Plymouth supporters of Mr. Mandl are wise.. It bite taken them some years to recover from the disasters which followed the reaction antece- dent to 1874, and they may now be regarded as about to return to their Liberalism It must be ad- mitted that Sir Edward Clarke has damaged his Position as the resnit of the recent contest. He was ' fighting his own battle all through. and it was his speeches and not Mr. guest's that the Liberals had to answer. Other things have contributed to weaken Sir Edward Clarke's hold upon the borough and if be were thinking of political preferment, which I am informed he is not, he might find the doubtful nature of the seat tell against him. Frior to 1874 the middle classes in Plymouth, with only a sprinkling of the working class, were the Liberal party. To-day there has been a slight recovery in the middle class, and the skilld workmen are all Liberals.</p>
        <p>--- The method of coping with the plagle in Bom- bay as described in the Indian papers is stringent enough in the view of persona who know Bombay very well to bring the epidemic under effective con- trol very shortly. The localities where infection prevails are visited every day. A military cordon surrounds it early in the morning, and no one is allowed to pass through it without being subjected to a medical examiation Toe houses where the plague prevails are visited to see if any fresh cases . have occurred, and they are removed to one of the tm hospitals for treatment. When these proceedings oo sstihbelesa to me are over the soldiers pass to another quarter, and go through hideay st entni disease, a sI e, n a thisnd w disinfectiona yi ti sal- most can be effectively carried out.</p>
        <p>All t --- he newspapers on Monday morning and evening, had appreciative memoirs of the late Mr. Villiers. I have not been able to afford time to read more than one or two of these, but in those I have seen I find no mention of the fact that for a number of years he was the recipient of a political pension of £1,200 per annum, to which I am quite sure his official service justly entitled him. While in the offices of Judge-Advocate-General and Pre- sident of the Poor-law Board he undoubtedly did good service,but, reviewing his career.l think there can be no doubt that he must have felt that with the repeal ofkthe Corn Laws his life work was vir- tually ended. I can look back over a Parliament- ary period of about a quarter of a century, yet I think, although he was at least during more than half of that period quite a regular attendant at the louse, he did not address the Chair more than once or twice, and then only briefly. have sometimes wondered whether parliamentary life has the evil reputation from a health paint of view that in some quarters attaches to it. The long and late hours,the excitement of party strife, and the general stre49 and strain, woula seem to be prejudicial to the vigour of the participants, and yet quite a large proportion of them make out a good old age. Even the journalists in the Gallery, a body of gentlemen who get through a vast amount of hard work under not the most favoura- ble conditions,are a fairly long lived race. One of theta, indeed, though his must be a record, entered the Gallery in 1820; and,altbough there was an in- terval of some three or four years' service elsewhere he did not cease his connection with it until 1884, and was even then spared to enjoy a few years of retirement.</p>
        <p>The "Times" is almost scared by the plain language in which the Chancellor of the Exchequer announced that "the Government are absolutely determined, at whatever cost, even, if necessarhat the cost of war," to keep the Chinese Empire an</p>
      </div>
