      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <p>By JOSEPH RYDER, General Secretary of the Land Nationalisation Society. ARTICLE XIII. A new and fair division I the goods and rights of this I world should be the main object of those who conduct human affairs. DE Tocianavmx.E. And they shall build houses and inhabit them ; and they shall plant vineyards and eat the fruit of them. They shall not build and another inhabit ; they shall not plant and an- other eat. ISAIA H. In order to estimate the full gain of the landlords from the expansion of population and general progress we rust reckon In the values of the land on which there are now railroads, mines, docks, dm. Taken altogsther. the money rental of lingland's soil is probably twice as high, and its real rental three or four times as high, as it was when the Corn Laws were repealed. ?noel:see' If annum. (" Principles of Economics.") A few years ago I got into conversation with a re- turned Australian colonist who, alter twenty-five years' absence from the mother country, had come back to his friends to spend a comfortable holiday. And the whole ex; ene of hie holiday would he met by the price of a single acre of land which he had bought originally for only 54, when he first went out to 'Victoria. He had bought SO acres for £l2 10s, He had never cultivated them. He had never built upon them. He bad paid no taxes upon them. Be had lived and worked twenty , miles away from them, and be scarcely ever raw them. ' Be had bought them, not for his own use, but as a speculation. It was a speculation which was absolutely certain to succeed ; for the colony of Victoria was growing in population and prosperity. And by-and- by a township sprang up near his fifty-acre plot. And F then a railway came; a State railway. A station was needed, and his land was the most favourably situated for such a purpose. And so the very Government which had geld that land to him a quarter of a century before had to buy an acre of it back for their station. They ' had sold it for Ave shillings, but since then its value had risen, and they had to pay him no less than £7OO for it. • PROFIT OF 280,000 PER CENT. It had increased in value 2,500-fold, and he told me that the value of the whole plot would become at least £50,000. I need not say that I did not waste Much breath in trying to convince him that the whole of that value was the result of the activity and enterprise of the township, that it was in no sense due to anything 'hat he had done, and that it was a vicious syitent which allowed it to be appropriated by one who had not' created it. As he dangled his heavy gold chain and- puffed a costly cigar, I clearly saw that his mind wee hopelessly non-receptive of any such doctrine. He , thought that the system of private property in land was ow, of the best things in this "best of all possible worlds," Self-interest blinds most men even to the most self-evident facts. But let, my readers ponder and ex- amine the above case. It is a typical one. Such things are occurring every day. They are not confined co new countries. In all our towns, great and small, they are to he discovered. And it is worth the while of land TO- formers to lay in a stock of such" powder and shot " wherewith to attack the landlord citadel. I will take a few cases at haphazard. LONDON " UNEARNED INCREMENT." lietween 1866 and 1888 the annual rental of London rose from £22,142,708 to £37,027,518, or from a capital value of £330,000.000 to £555,000,000. We can approxi- mately calculate from supplementary valuations that L1,T92,130 of the increased teat i was due to building operations, and £8,092,680 was doe to the rise in the value of the land alone. At the same rats of increase the rent of London's land is now about £10,000,000 more than it was thirty years ago. If we capitalise this at 25 years' purchase we find that the growth of the Metro- polis has added £250,000,000 to the value of the ground landlords' so-called" property." Had the community valued the land thirty years ago, and given full compen- sation to the then" owners" (I use the word " o weer " that is commonly used, but I deny that any individual can ever be the rightful owner of the land),the unearned increment that has since accrued would furnish the Lon- don School Board, the London County Council, the Lou- don Vestries, and the Board of Guardians with all the money they now spend, and the ratepayers would be cased of a heavy burden under which so many of them now groan, and because of which so many of them are ranged in antagonism to Progressive proposals. If ever there was a case of" reaping withonc sowing "it is to be found here. If ever there was an injustice it is this. A little time ago a City freehold was withdrawn from public auction because the reserve price was not reached. The highest bidder went up to £45 per square foot, or at the rate of £1,960,200 per acre. But it was not enough, for only a few weeks before land in Cornhill had realised at the rate of £3,000,000 per acre. The land on which the Grand Hotel, Trafalgar-square,was built was said at the rate of £4,000,000 PER ACRE. And the same can be said of the site of Her Majesty's Theatre. Yet while the landlords are appropristine an increment of 'lt million pounds (eapit.l value) every year, public improvements are checked. slums are kept In existenee, education is starved, and paupers are sacrifioed to the Guardian "economists"—because there</p>
        <p>is not enough public money available. In March, 1888,a number of leases on the Portman *s- tain fell in,and Lord Portman'e unearned increment is snid to have been £1,250,000. One case will show bow the thing is done. One of his tenants had her rent roamed from 10 guineas to VW. She had tn pay a line of £l,OOO, and was ordered to spend £5OO upon the heuse. Then she was granted a short lease,at the end of which the house must be handed over to Lord Portman in a first-class tenantable condition. She must pay all Insurance premiums,and she had to pay £l5 to the land- lord's lawyer for drafting the agreement. Multiply this case by hundreds, and you begin to underrtand the origin of that palatial modern mansion in the Viscount's park at Blandford. And let us never forget that these enormous and ever growing land values are exempt from the attention, of the rate-collector. Yet without the expenditure of the rates they would be non-exie• tent. Let each man work out the figures for his own town. If the proportion between the population and the incre- ment applies to other towns, then it is a simply rule-of- three sum. In London for every eoul that is added to the population £lOO is added to the property of the ereund landlord. When the people gain wisdom that value will he wisely spent by public bodies for the wel- fare, of the workers, and will no longer ha diverted to buy mansions and fine equipages,yachts and racehorses, -nenservante and maidaervants,wines and costly dinners, £lO,OOO pictures, and vanish in all the thousand and one ways in which the earnings of thr, toilers are now frittered and squandered by the privileged class. a YEW PROVINCIAL CASES. The Glasgow Municipal Buildings are perhaps the no- blest of their kind In this country. The land on which they stand was Corporation property until the latter part of the last century. It was then sold to the City Fathers for 21. Bd. per yard. Silt when the Town Hall was wanted it was bought back. And it cost the Cor- poration no less than £35 to acquire each yard that had been so foolishly and unjustly (for the land was not theirs to sell) sold 100 years before for 21. Id. In all £175,000 had to be paid for the site before &amp;single crafts- man could be eat to work. And the ratepayers will have to pay an extra,and unnecessaty,2B,ooo per annum for about fifty years in consequence. In Bradford the sum total of the site values has inureased by £5,800,000 in 32 years. And the public debt of the town is £4,691,000. So that if the town had acquired the site on which it stands there would not now be a debt of £2l on each inhabitant,and there would be a handsome sur- plus in addition. The lend of Portsmouth was worth £5,206 in 11100. A few years ago it was £202,100, or £32,000 more than the whole of the local rates. fair =ALAND. I have before me as I write an important pamphlet Issued by the Ground Rent Revenue League of New Zealand (Auckland Branch). It is the explanation of their change of policy from the Georgeite plan of taxing landlords out—a plan which they say has found little favour, and which they discard (wisely, I think) for one of State purchase. They say that the total amount re- ceived by the State for the land which it has sold within the past fifty years is £13,312,994. But that land Is now worth £76,787,895, showing an unearned incre- mont of over 62 million pounds,whick,curiously enough, shows an increment of a little over £lOO for every ad- ditional inbabitant,as in the case of London land. Yet, while all the Colonial Governments have been alienating public land in this way, and literally giving away a magnificent property, they have been adding to their Indebtedness by the raising of loans which seriously weight and handicap all their enterprises. LkND SYNCULATION IN AMBRICk.</p>
        <p>The United States of Ault ries "bossed" by millionaires and syedieates, has given about 270,000,000 acres of public land to the great railway companies—that is, an area whist' is nearly five times the size of Great Britain. And it is just this land which has become the most</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <p>thickly settled. It is the land of the fertile valleys,it is the site of the chief townships, it is near the railways. Hence the wealth of the " railway kings " *f America. The Yanderbilts and the Jay Goulds are the fruits of the alienation of public land, and of the encouragement of monopoly and land speculation. And the Astor family are the typical grrund landlords of the American States who are multi-millionaires because of the unearned in- crement of Manhattan lisland, upon which New York stands. They are to New York what the Duke of West- minster is to London. In America land speculation is looked upon as the one method of making money fast. Every town has its " land boom," and land values are forced up in the most reckless manner to anticipate the growth of rising townships. Land at £lO an acre one year becomes £l6O an acre three years afterwards. And among the thou- sands who make money in speculating on the needs of the community Hettv Geene stands prominent. She ad. mite being west!' 50,000,000 dollars, and she explained to an interviewer bow she had acquired that vast for- tune. She said, " I buy land ia every rising township, and wait for the profit that is sure to come. Then I sell. It dces not need much acumen to see that a specular and values is not a creator of them. His profit is made up of the communits's loss, in the same way that the winnings in horse-race betting and in card-gambling are simply made un of the teethes of the unlucky. But of all forms of gambling land gambling is far away the worst. It means the enrichment of a class, but it equaly involves the impoverishment of whole nations.</p>
        <p>ALTER THE LAW. Not that I blame landlords more than other men. They only take advantage of the law as they find it. The res object of reformers, therefore, must be, not the condem nation of the individual, but the alteration of the law. Said Robert Lowe, "We must educate our masters'.. He meant it as a sneer. But it contained a truth. For tlas masses of the la adless are the real masters. Let us, then, educate them upon great questions such as the one lam dealing with. Let us show them the land laws in all their naked injustice. Let us teach them to see the difference between the proper rights of property, with- out which no civilisation could last a day, as contrasted with the bogus rights which masquerade as real rights, and which, so long as they are recognised, prevent national progress.</p>
        <p>WHY WOMEN ARE ATTRACTITE. Why is one woman attractive and anether not. It isn't eutirelv a question of age, or features, or intellect. The most admirable and attractive thing about an a tractive woman is bee womanliness. Everybody admires a womanly woman. She must have health, of course, be- cause without it she would lose the brightness of her eyes the fulness of her cheeks, and her vivacity. Health brings all these things, but health means more than most people think of. If pale, nervous, and weak, a woman lacks good health. Women who are pale and wan should not resort to iron, drugs and tonics, except by the advice of a properly qualified medical man. They should try instead to nourish and build up their blood by the vital nourishment imparted by Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa. And so rosy cheeks and comliness may be attained. Surely the road is pleasanter than the thorny and nasty path paved with drugs. Nurse Tillatson, Alexander Hotel, St. Leonard's-on- sea, writes: "I have tried Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and like it very much. I shall have much pleasure in re- commending it to my patients." Miss S. Percival, Post Office, Burgh, writes :—" I do not think any other can equal yours. My father has been taking ordinary cocoa, but I think Dr. Tibbles' Vi- Cocoa is better. I will tell my friends of your Vi- Cocoa." Mrs. King. Linden Cottage, Wimbledon Hill, Surrey. writes :—" I think Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is delicious,and quite fulfills all said about it." Mrs. Budden, Eradwardine, Bournemouth, writes :- "I am pleased with Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, and like it, and will certainly use it in future." Merit, and merit alone, is what we claim for Dr. Tibbles' Vi• Cocoa, and we are prepared to send to any reader who names the Warrington "Examiner" a dainty sample tin of Dr. Tibblee' Vi-Coeoa free sad post-paid. There is no magic in all this. It is a plain, honest, straightforward offer. It is done to introduce the merits of Vi-Cocoa into every home. Dr. Tibbles' Vi- Cocoa as a concentrated form of nourishment and vitality is invaluable; nay, more than this; for to all who wish to face the strife and battle of life with greater endurance and more sustained exertioo, it is absolutely indispensable. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa can be ebtained from all chemists, grocers, and stores, or from Dr. Tibbles' Vi. Cocoa, Limited, 60, el and 62, Bunhill Row, London, E.O. Write for free sample.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>A very fine badger, scaling over 40'b., was killed at dawk's Hill, Deal, on Saturday. Owing to depredations on several p mitry yards in the district lately, supposed to have been committed by a fox, some farmers ergan- ised a hunt. A terrier with the party discovered the hole and entered it, hut failing to drive out any animal, the party set to work to dig out, and eventually the badger was unearthed and killed. It is stated to be the largest ever seen in that countryside.</p>
        <p>Ina</p>
        <p>When a vessel is !attached by the Japanese they do not " christen "it as we do, but instead they hang over Ike ship's prow a large pasteboard cage full of birds. Directly the ship is afloat a man palls a string and tha cage opens and the birds fly off and make the air alive with their songs and the whirr of their wings. The idea Is that these birds welcome the vessel as she begins her career as a thing ef life.</p>
        <p>The Marquis of Lorne, when Governor General of Canada was present at some sports held on the ice of the St. Lawrence. Though wrapped in furs, he felt the cold acutely, and was astonished to see an Indian meander- ing around bare-legged and enveloped only in a blanket. He asked the Indian how he managed to bear such a temperature. " Why yell no cover your face 2" asked the Indian sharply. The Marquis replied that no one ever did so, and that his faca was hardy from exposure. " Good !" rejoined the Indian briefly. "Me all face." And then he walked away magestically.</p>
        <p>_ The Admiralty and the War Office are !Witty under various climatic conditions a reetbpd of conserving flour. One objection to rational granaries has been the diffi- culty of storing wheat for any length of time. Tim grain germinates and is ruined. Experiments are being made with a system of compression into bricks by by. draulic pressure. Flour so treated is not affected by damp, is free from mould, and is sweet and wholesome. Moreover, compression destroys all forms of larval life, and lour is thereby rendered safe from the attacks of insects. The saving in storage is enormous,as the cnbio space occupied by 100 lb of loose flour will bold mons than 3001 b. of the compressed article.</p>
        <p>The father of Sir Tattoo Sykes was a well-known oportsman, and his name Ii til a household word in Yorkshire. Till he was nearly sixty he was always ready to take a mount on the racecourse, but he did not bet. Though Mt, in his stockings be would train himself down till he scaled only lilt. It was his ambition to live to be a hundred, but he died prematurely at ninety- three, in consequence of a chill caught while sleeping on a heap of stories by the roadside, which be had been en- gaged in breaking—a favorite diversion of the old gentleman's. On cne occasion Sir Tattoo himself drone a dock of sheep which he had bought in Leicestershire back to Sledrnere. Another time the fit seized him to rids to Dundee and back. Ile was always performing some feat or other which became the talk of the countryside, and endeared him to the hearts of the horse-loving York- hiremen.</p>
        <p>There are two specimens of a bird in the insect house - at the Zoo at present which has collected round itself Various travellers' tales. The bird is the" Data "of the South American Indians and the " Campanero" of the Spaniards. It is called by us the "Bell "bird. Travel- 'ere have narrated how, in the silence of a Brazilian forot, the toll of the distant bell has startled the unpre- pared traveller, who believed that he was miles from civilisation. One writer (Mr. Waterton) has declared that the bird tolls with so sweet a note that " Aerosols would stop in mid-chase. . . . Orpheus himself would drop hisi lute to listen " ; while the clear note earl be beard at a d mice of three miles ! But this, alas, is pure fancy. If anyone will have the patience to keep quietin the insect house for a little time be will hear a note which is muehmore like the unromantic clash of as knife on a plate, and not at all suggestive of carrying far—even in a tropical noon. Still, the bird is worth lookingat„ on account of its beautiful snowy plummier, and the curious dilatable" horn" on the beak, wbicU the male swells Out when he produces his sound.</p>
        <p>LINSEED COMPOUND demulcent anodyne expeetorst4s. Coughs and Colds, nd ,or Chemists only. Ad t.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DeA IL N ELLIE. 1 aW the other day a very pretty draped toque ,of rosy gime silk, with runnings of narrow bls.cii satin ribbon all over the crumpled crown. Now you know, rose colour is not like the famotu "pink pills," specially devised for '• pale people." The rose-colour is very becoming. That is if it is of shot glace, a deep rose shot with white ; the broken colour took away the trying effect of the crimson. Moral : It behoves people to be careful in the choice of reds. There are some hideous reds going about this wintev, but I have a theory that pale people might wear many more tin Is than 'they do if they only exereised a little judgment in their choice. At the came time these matters require a great deal of self-restraint. It is extremely annoying to find that you cannot, for in9tance, wear pale green without looking sere and yellow; and that in a Medici collar wore by your tall, slender-necked neighbour you only give 'the impression of an owl looking out of an ivy bush.</p>
        <p>very pretty hat I saw a few days ago was of 'felt trimmed with violets and green and velvet and black feathers. This is very fashionable. A charmingly pretty hat is carried out in velvet with folds of velvet and glace in two colours and shaded wings, a rosette of velvet being arranged under the brim.</p>
        <p>Draped toques mads of crinkled satin arc deliciously light and graceful. One in emerald green, with white plumes and a touch of old lace and black chiffon, would be a capital confection fox smart wear in winter, for emerald green is a colout which stands winter weather very well, and it serves to brighten up a black or dark blue gown beautifully. I recommend this useful fabric to the notice of those who want to refurbish a hat. By removing a straw crown, and substituting a draped one of the satin in any colour that best suite the 'scheme, wonders may be effected.</p>
        <p>.. very pretty di .. a girl is a specie of empire dress of red corduroy mounted on red velvet yoke daintily draped with red ribbon. The scallops of the velvet are edged with ruches. Dresses for older girls. i.e., from 10 to 14 or 14 this winter lean towards trimmed skirts which 'causes them to be somewhat tiresome to make up at home. But if a little trouble is not objected to, a most attractive and not expensive frock can bt composed of white China silk, the skirt being trimmed with three very narrow gathered frills of the silk at the hem, them with three bands of lemon-yellow satin ribbon (about three-quarters of an inch wide), and above these again with two kills of silk, the skirt having a lining of yellow silkette or sateen, with a deep frill of white lace tacked round the edge. The bodice should be full, slightly overhanging the yellow satin sash, which ties at the back with long ends, with a pointed yoke made of tucked bands of silk, divided by strips of yellow ribbon, and bordered with a rather deep frill of silk, caught up on the shoulders with ribbon-bows. The sleeves are tight, with tucking' from shoulder to wrist on the outside of the arm, end frills of the silk in place of cuffs.</p>
        <p>winter coat is of seal-brown lis- .th triinings of musquash fur, and ?Per filigree, set with stones of varied due- -as model would be charming in velours du Nord and chinchilla, or in dark green or violet Cloth, to be worn with a skirt en suite. In some cases appliqué designs are merely finished With stitching or outlined with fine cord or a gold thread, but in some of the newer French gowns they are bordered by a quilling of half-inch ' ribbon. This effect, for example, may be seen on • one of the famous toilettes in the famous " Liars." It is the cerise shot silk worn by Miss Irene Van- brugh in the third act. The white silk applique is all bordered with a narrow quilling of • satin ' ribbon. Quillings of chiffon may similarly border eqaulettes or berthes of lace. On a pale pink evening bodice I saw at the pantomime last week, very delicate décolletage arrangement of tea- Coloured lace was bordered with a tiny ruching of Elttlx chiffon, and the lace on a white gown, which of "ts belted with green was adorned with a quilling thi° ' Pale Undine shade. Your loving J</p>
        <p>f cop' Thn</p>
