      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The dates for the March Drawing Rooms are not yet definitely fixed upon. but probably these functleos will take place during the first week of that month, as the Queen is anxious that the Princess of Wales should go to Copenhagen as early as may be, for the sake of the Queen of Denmark, whose health, though benefitted by her long residence at Bernatorff, is far from good. Though now advanced in years, and somewhat care- worn, Queen Louise still retains her charm of manner, which is a by-word among the sturdy Danes. Her Majesty is tall, and upright as a girl, and her silvery hair is soft and abundant. Toe King of Denmark has always been a model husband. He cannot bear to be parted from his "golden bride" for more than a few hours, and they are seldom seen in public apart. The Chatleau of Berustorff, the favourite country seat of the King and Queen of Denmark, is comparatively small, and somewhat incoaveniently arranged, yet both the Dowager Empress of Russia, and the Princess of Wales love te stay there. The life is of the simplest,and Is doubtless a great relief after the etiquette and routine of the larger European Courts. Breakfast is served at eight o'clock, luncheon at one, tea at five, and dinner at seven. The Queen of Denmark, and her two elder daughters, are aocoraplished pianists, and the evening is usually devoted to music. At eleven the royal person- ages retire to rest, and by midnight everything is still and quiet. There is considerable excitement in New York over the question of the, possible marriage of Mr. Howard Gould, a son of the late Jay Gould, whose engagement 'to Miss Clemmons, a beautiful actress, was mentioned some time age. The attraction which the stage has had for the Gould family, has been remarkable. Mr. George Gould, it will be remembered, married a very beautiful actress, Miss Edith Kingdon, and Miss Anna Gould was at one time engaged to an actor. Miss Cleminons who Is a Californian girl of twenty-five, dresses with the grace and elegance of a Frenchwoman. She can row, box, and fence, she can also ride anything in the shape of a horse, and is quite at home with a tandem, or four- in-hand. Her voice is euft and melodious, her manner quiet and reserved. A somewhat curious question has lately been opened up concerning the charitable bequests, made by mem- bers of our sex during the past year. It seems that twenty-five women, whose personalty amounted to two and a half millions, bequeathed no less than • fourth of , this sum to charities. And this state of affairs is sup- posedly owing to—family squabbles. We are net so partial as are men, to relations, and we are often more interested in a hospital for the general poor, than in the personal care of an indigent Cousin! Doubtless, in some oases, all this is perfectly true, but one caonot help feel- ing it just a little hard that such motives for charitable work, as I have quoted, should be ascribed to our sex , generally. It is easy to be fur trimmed nowadays. So little fur is needed for the purpose. Take for instance our coats. Last winter we indulged in two revers, this winter we are content with one. We are all trimmed lip the left side this season, and a cascade of frills takes the place of the second rover. A scrape of fur to match is permit- ted to decorate the muff, and yet another scrape, to adorn the toque. Coats, I must not omit to mention in passing, are still the favourite wear of the moment. Probably this is due to our having realised that no fashion can be successful which hides the line of the ' neck, and the outline of the arms. Capes, comfortable and convenient as they are, can never compete with well-cut coats so far as gracriful•ese is concerned. In the covering of one material with another,we wax more cunning every day. Two materials, one on the top of another, have already been found too simple, and It takes three skirts now, to achieve distinction. A pretty skirt I espied at the theatre a few evenings back, was of rose pink satin. Over this fell another of maize- coloured silk muslin, and on the top of these a third, of black tulle. The soft tints of the silk and muslin shone through the Wile like a winter sunset through dark trees. Such gowns, however, are only for those of us Whose pin money is of goodly proportions. " Pin-money" by the way,was,as many of my readers are doubtless aware,originally a husband's allowance to his wife, for the purchase of those articles for her own uee,or as presents,though it retained the name long after its significance had vanished. Addison disliked what he called "the doctrine of pin money," and wrote against it in 1711. He could not comprehend the attitude of a woman who wanted any kind of allowance from her husband,and proposed that if it must exist, it should at least be called" needle money,which would imply some- thing of good housewifery." How long it is since pins first came into existence,history declares not, but surely never was an ancient British or Danish barrow opened that did not contain a bone pin or two. The women of old Greece and Rome stuck round headed gold and silver pins in their hair,and the small skewers we occasionally hear of were tbe precursors of the modern pin. It is supposed that Anne of Bohemia, the second Queen of Richard IL, first brought the pin proper into these Wes of ours, in the latter half of the fourteenth century. Fins repnire a lot of making with very expensive, delicate and complicated machinery. The wire from which they are manufactured is first drawn through copper rollers. This is to get all the bends and kinks out of it. It is then wound on a very large reel, which is attached by means of a spindle to the machine that makes the pins. One of these machine' maim 8,000 pins an hour. Next the pins take a bath of sulphuric acid, after which they are mixed with a quantity of-sawdust, and placed in a "tumbling barrel." After being whirled round and round for three or four bolero, they come out quite smooth and clean. Pins and sawdust are them allowed to fall in a steady stream through a strong air blast. The sawdust, being the lighter, is blown away into a big box-like room, and the pins fall into a tank containing a solution of tin in aturiatic acid. The tin in solution is deposited on the pine,by means of electricity, and after a while they are taken out white and bright. But as yet they are pointless. In order to remedy this defect, they are first carried on an endless groved belt past a set of rapidly moving files. This points them roughly, and after beiog passed between two grinding wheels, and forced against a rapidly moving band faced with emery cloth, they are dropped Into the polishing tub. From this polishing tub they are carried to a " sticker " where they fall from a hopper.on an inclined plane, in which are a number of slits. Toe pins catch in these slits, and hanging by their heads slide down to the apparatus which inserte them in the paper. This machine is perhaps the most ingenious of all the beauti- ful contrivances that help to make and manipulate pins. It grabs a whole row of pins at once, and after crimp- ing two rows of the paper sticks them all in with the greatest ease imaginable. It does this at the rate of one hundred thousand pins an hour. According to an individual, well known in Society, every second woman rouges, and every third wears a tourp4s ! With regard to statement the first, matters are greatly exaggerated, but with reference to the second item, the communicative person referred to, is not very wide of the mark. People are beginning to find out that waving the hair with hot irons he most destruc- tive. and in consequence many women conceal good heads ef hair under what are called "Transformations," which are nothing more or less than entire wigs so deftly contrived that it is impossible to detect them. The natu- ral hair is simply twisted is as small a compass as pos- sible beneath. Here is the recipe for a dish usually much apprecia- ted by the little folks. Take a teacupful of milk, ene , ounce of butter, three ounces of flour, a'dessertepoonful ; of castor sugar, and two eggs well beaten. Put the milk into a saucepan, with thee butter and sugar, and allow it to melt,add the flour by 411egrees, stirring it all the time. When it thickens turn it into a basin, and add the eggs, mixing all well together. Have ready a pan of boiling fat and drop the mixture in, in tablespoonfuls. Keep turning each about. When a light browh color they are done. Serve them piled up with white sugar sprinkled , over them, and warm jam round them if liked.</p>
        <p>" MADGE."</p>
      </div>
