      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>MR. JOSIAH NIX AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MR. JOSIAH NIX AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL MISSION.</p>
        <p>Mission services have been conducted inßold- street Wesleyan Church each day during this week by Mr. Josiah Nix, an earnest and successful lay evangelist. The object of the mission as stated by the missioner is " to induce the unsaved to make their peace with God, by coming at once to Jesus Christ. Also to lead God's people to consecrate themselves afresh to Him and His service in this world." There have been large congregations at each service, and the number of those who have afterwards entered the enquiry room has been most H The opening services on Sunday were very successful. At the morning service, r. Nix encouraging. based his remarks on the words " Be ye ready," and gave a preparatory address to the workers engaged in the mission. He traced the defeat of the Israelites consequent upon the sin of Acham, and exhorted the workers not to engags in this special Christian work while indulging in any known sin. , A special service for young people was held in the ' afternoon, when the children from the several Wesleyan Sunday schools in the town attended. Mr. Nix delivered a stirring addre-s upon the favourite children's anecdote of Samuel and Eli, maintaining the interest and keeping the attention of old and young from first to last. He exhorted the parents present to train up their children for God, and Ppoke upon the value of the daily use of the bible in the home. In appealing to the scholars for a consecrated life, he remind id them that God loved them, and desired to have them as His own, A large number responded to his appeal to enter the enquiry-room. Mr. Walter B rough conducted a united mission choir, which has greatly assisted in making the musical portion of the mission ser- vices a success. Sankey's hymns have been used, and the heartiness of the singing has been it feature of the mission. On ,Sunday evening the spacious chapel was filled, not a pew being empty. Mr. Nix delivered an earnest appeal from the words " Your own salvation," pointing out that they were sometimes preached to about this or that good man,or something which did not concern them in the least. Some men could talk for a week about the salvation of the world,or the salvation of our country from the curses of drunkenness, and gambling,and lust. The salvation of London was a great thing,and he could give startling facts which he had seen and known in thatcity,yet the greatest thing of all to them as men and women was their own salvation. He pleaded with them with all the powerful eloquence of an enthusiastic evangelist to consider this matter.</p>
        <p>The series of week night meetings opened on Monday evening in Bold-street Wesleyan Church, when the congregation was not so large as expected owing to the Spiritualiet discussion in Parr Hall. Oa the platform with Mr. Nix were the ministers of the circuit, the Revs. W, Potts (superintendent), W. G. Triggs, and G. Harbottle. There was a strong choir, conducted by Mr. W.Brough. After the open- ing devotional exercises Mr. Nix made allusion to one or two general matters, expressing hie surprise at intelligent men and women wasting their time at a Spiritualist meeting. A parrot could 'chatter but he wanted to see what men could do and had done. He requested the assistance of all, and especi- ally the women in the furtherance of his object, to make men Christians. If there was to be an election here—he did not think they would stand much chance,the town was too much under the beer-barrel he believed,—but men would begin to fight and stir about. But to do the same for the mission oh no ! He believed in the salvation of the man and not the sal- vation of the soul that people talked socmuch about; and they ought to look to the welfare of their bodies and had no right to put anything into that body which would dm; it down or injure it. Therefore they ought not to take intoxicating liquor and a good many thought they should not smoke. They must study the welfare of the body so that they would be strong to labour. Addressing himself more particularly to the younger portion of the congrega- tion, Mr. Nix declaimed forcibly against bad litera- ture. They must throw over their trashy novels, let them go I "Do not let your minds be a sink- hole for all these men write about," he interjected. There was plenty of good literature about, and they should ales read the paper to see what the devil was doing, and learn to fight him. The subject of Mr. Nix's address wis " The su I- denness, the simplicity, and the reality of cenver- sion," end as a basis he said they would always be right if they stood where Jerltl.3 Christ stood. Jesus of Nazareth had founded an empire, founded it on love, and it would never pass away. To every man, woman, and child present he said, if th.y had any desire to go to the Kingdom of Heaven they must be converted. It was not difficult. There were no forms, ceremony, or sacraments, and they would go to the Kingdom of Heaven,and lead godly lives. It was only by their being converted and by receiving from Jesus Christ a new heart, and being born from above that they could do so. They could never live a good life without. fhey heard a good deal about conversion Thera were some people who rejoiced in it, and others who made fen of it. Jesus Christ was the infallible teacher and the only one. Some people said the Pope of /tome was in- fallible. That was a great mistake. The Pope made a great many mistakes every time he opened his mouth. There he was wrapped up,caged, and kept well informed by a few, and he thought he knew ' everything, poor fellow t Jesus Christ, the one and ' only teacher said himself "ye must be born anew." Unless they submitted to him ae a little child they could not enter the kingdom of Heaven. Every in- telligent person in the congregation could settle for themselves in a minute whether they were go- ing to Heaven or not. Re was as sure he was going to the Kingdom of Heaven as he was sure that he was standing on that platform. It was</p>
      </div>
