      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 21, 1898.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF Mn. H. E. FALK.</p>
        <p>The death of Mr. Hermann Eugene Falk took place at his residence, Catsclough, near Winsford, on Wednes- day morning. By his removal there has passed away one of the most prominent men engaged in the salt trade during the past,half-ceatury. The firm of R. Falk, senior,and Co. commenced business in 1840, and the sub- ject of this notice started in his own name, in 1858, at Meadow Bank Works, Winsford, which were of an ex- tensive character. He continued to conduct them until they were assigned to the Salt Union, Limited, in 1888, and since then his son, Mr. H. J. Falk, has been a director of the syndicate. The late Mr. Falk was thoroughly acquainted with the manu- facture of salt, and had a considerable connection in the Baltic, to where in former days large quantities of English salt were shipped. He carried on his busi- ness in Liverpool, and was at one time one of the largest eonsignors from that port. Mr. Falk opened a mine at Winsford, from which more rock salt was des- patched than from any previous Cheshire mine. He was an extremely energetic and successful man of business; of strong will and determination. For years he was a member of the Salt Association, and had had more to do with the Salt Chamber of Commerce since its foundation 40 years ago to the present than any other individual. Mr. Falk was a trustee of the River Weaver, and took a very active part in the navigation. He was one of the Arst to introduce steam on the river, and had built a series of iron vessels which carried very large quantities of salt. Many of them are now in the possession of the Salt Union.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Indigestion</head>
        <p>Indigestion</p>
        <p>Caused me most intense misery for many years. I became so bad off I could not get upstairs without resting on every stair. Medicine did not avail un- til I saw an advertise- ment of Hood's Sar- saparilla and resolved to try this med- icine. I have taken three bottles of Hood's Sar- saparilla and can now get p stairs as well as I ever Lged man." DAVID MEAD, 62, Burton End, Haverhill, Suffolk. 17' ood's Sarsa- parilla All chemists. 23. 94.. 46.64. C. 1.11004 co. Ltd., 34, Snow lllll,London,E.C.</p>
        <p>Hood's Pills do not cause pain or gripe. All chemist. Is. lid.</p>
        <p>THE DECLINE OF MERSEY SALT SHIPMENTS.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. E. Falk, in his summary of the salt trade of the Mersey for the past year says :—The exports of salt from the Mersey in 1897 show a total of 591,202 tons,the lowest figure recorded during the last twenty years. The moment is perhaps opportune to institute a comparison extending over ten years,because 1887 was not exposed to the disturbances la trade caused by an anticipation of higher prices on the formation of the Salt Union in 1888. On this point, however, it is desirable to state that common salt is now being sold at a lower price than that which prevailed two years before the formation of he Salt Union. While shipments from Liverpool have thus heavily declined from 994,726 tons in 1887 to 591,202 In 1897, being handicapped by an unavoid- able river freight from the works in Cheshire by most burdensome tolls upon the river Weaver,arnount- ing to more than 16 per cent on the works' price of com- mon salt ; and by heavy dock dues in Liverpool three times as high as those imposed in the competing port of Middlesbrough ; it must not be supposed that this trade has all been lost to the country at large. The develop- ment ef saltfields in other parts of Great Britain, near ports where the authorities me more accommodating than in Liverpool, has been one important factOr in diverting the salt trade from the river MersAy. Of courge It laas pgt been the Only factor, hems, ealtfields have been extended In othef parts of the world, and England has to face in this respect competition of a severity which did not exist ten years ago. The one point upon which the British salt trade may fairly congratulate itself to-day is the very marked improvement in the quality of English salt, which has been brought about since the formation of the Salt Union. The importance of this feature upon many industries, and notably upon the fisheries of the world, can scarcely be exargerated, and it is sincerely to be looped that fishery authorities throughout the universe will draw the attention of those most closely interested to the necessity of using the pure, clean product of Great Britain, if they wish to maintain their place in the world's markets. In 1887 Liverpool sent to the United States of America 169,195 tons of salt. In the year just expired the total amounted only to 91,725—a terrible decline. It is at all times difficult for England to compete with the salt works of America on their own continent, and this difficulty is turned almost into an impossibility, by the fact that the Government of the United States, in the year just con- eluded, placed upon common salt a duty amounting to 150 per cent of its cost at the works in Cheshire. Ship- ments to India have been somewhat higher than in 1898, though they are 127,000 tons behind the shipments from the Mersey ten years ago. 82,000 tone of this deficit can be traced to the shipments from Middles- brough, but the balance has for the time been lost to British trade. Other distant markets do not call for any special observations at the present moment, but it has, unfortunately to be recorded that, in view of the com- parative failure of the Scotch fisheries in 1897, ship. manta from the Mersey to Scotch ports declined by nearly 22,000 tons. The growth of competition renders it imperative to seek for fresh outlets and to try for new markets. It is to be hoped that our Government, in its commercial negotiations with other Powers, will recog- nise the importance of leaving no stone unturned to break down barriers to trade, and to open up these markets which at present exclude us to a free and un- restricted exchange of conamoaities. The name of China is on everybody's lips at the present moment, and thoughts of what might be done there in salt are calcu- lated to make those lips water. Freights have had a somewhat firmer tendency throughout the year,and the constant disappearance of small sailing tonnage is not without an unfortunate influence upon the Liverpool salt trade.</p>
        <p>THE SATURDAY POPECLA.R CONCERTS IN PARR HALL. •An excellent programme was provided in the Parr Hall on Saturday evening, but the attendance was not so satisfactory as the " bill of fare " war- ranted. Mr. J. A. Muir was a host in himself, and fully maintained the reputation he has gained. He delighted the audience with his humorous musical sketches and songs, and no doubt, they will be pleased to renew his acquaintance at some future date. His first item was a chat about umbrellas, concluding with a fine rendering of the song "The Ole Umbrella." complying with the wishes of the audience for an encore he sang "Is yer mammy always wid yer." A humorous story on " Tbe Ball of the Season," in which Mr. Muir gave a clever whistling solo, was also a great success. Mr. Muir's other items included an original humorous musical sketch entitled " The Academy of Love," in which the author ventured to give advice on love affairs ; and also a humorous song entitled "The Gay Tom- tit." He was loudly applauded after each item. Mr. Fred M album made his lirst appearance as a novelty inetrumentalist,aud made a very favourable impres- sion. In addition to a number of concertino solos, he also gave performances on instruments which he had made himself and named the Malburn orehes- traphone, and the ventrilophone. On the latter in- strument he imitated the cries of a cow, dog, lamb, pig, duck, hen, rooster, and also of a German band clarionet. The performances upon the largest and araallest concertinas in the world were very cleverly executed, especially the march composed by the performer, introducing an imitation of he flute. The vocalists were ;—Miss Lizzie Skeet (soprano), Madame Edith Parker (contralto), and Mr. A. Marshall (baritone). Miss Skeet was indis- posed, and she was only fairly successful in her singing of "Eileen Alannah." She was, however, more successful in her second song, and was encored for her singing of "Dolly's Revenee." She com- plied with "On the Zuyder Zee." Madame Parker, Who is a favourite with Warrington audiences, was twice encored. She sang with her accustomed sweetness and clearness, and the loud applause and recalls of the audience were evidence that her efforts were appreciated. Mr. A. Marshall's initial effort was " Nazareth," but he was far more success- ful in his singing of "The Soldier of the Queen." The duties of accompanist were ably discharged by Mr. W. H. Payton.</p>
