      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <p>pen to all believers and he was one of the be- lievers. The flrA fruit of conversion was an interest in the conversion of somebody else. Only a fort- night ago a man in Darlington was converted and went up jut° the gallery for his wife and sister who also went into the enquiry room. Matthew got up and followed Christ. He had ten thousand reasons for not doing istl only one reason for doing so, and that was Christ's command " Follow me." He followed Christ and was converted, and the first thing he did was to seek the conversion of others. Mr. Nix added that he .knew lots of people who had been converted but their pockets had never been. It was a tender place with an Englishman, was his pocket. Matthew s pocket was converted and he never went back to his profession and never became a backslider, but wrote his gospel. His conversion was sudden, and simple and real. What Jesus Christ did that day he could do to-day. No man lived to himself, not one. There was some man or woman influenciog them and leading them in the right or wrong direction, and it was for then to decide that night. Jesus Christ wanted to lead them and said" Follow me." Follow Him and h would take them to the I e Cingdom of Heaven. They could cooperate with Him to sweep away drink, gambling, lust, selfishness, and worldliness. The world wauld be all the better for their existence if they would follow Jesus Christ. If they were faith- ful unto death they should enter the Kingdom of Ifeaven. lle prayed God to grant that they would all obey Christ and ' Follow Him." During the singing of the last hymn about a score of persons left their sesta at the preacher's invita- tion and entered the enquiry room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>A. LIVE MA.N MEASIIRKD FOR</head>
        <p>A. LIVE MA.N MEASIIRKD FOR</p>
        <p>HIS COFFiN, HE EEL/11'ES 1118 WEIRD EXPERIENcE. NEWLY has Ctieltenhain had a topic mare tverthy Of serious discussion and inquiry than the strange case of Mr.G. 13. B„te. What we have to record (says an article in the "Cheltenham Chronicle ") reads like some- thing beyond the bounds of possibility, but, probing more deeply, we find a scientiec cause for this miracle. The hero of this narrative,Mr. Boats, landlord of the 41 Bell," Bath-road, Cheltenhana, though fifty years of age, is, to quote Sheridan, "A fine figure of a man," with a beaming, honest, open face, tanned by Eastern suns ; it needs but a glance to see that he has served his Queen in foreign parts. " I joined the army," said the old soldier, " in 1856, and served thirty-three years, eleven moortie. / was in the Tenth Lincolnshire, of which I was colour and pay sergeant for eighteen years, retired on full pensionorame ' to Cheltenham, and went into the licensed victualling trade. " I remember as though it was but yesterday," Mr. Boats continued, "the Perak Expedition of 1874 from Singapore,to punish the natives for the assassination o Lieutenant-Gensral Bireh, our commissioner. We bad one or two warm brushes with the enenny.and I was un- lucky enough to receive an ugly wound on the left leg. However,it seemed to heal,and though I had to wear an elastic stocking, I was able to walk about and perform my duty as usual." " Did the old wound ever find you out again ?" Mr. Boats gave an involuntary shudder. "It did," he replied. " Three inn a half years ago ulcers began to break out on my leg and arms. My leg was lit, rally perforated ; it looked like raw liver, and the pain was excruciating. I bore it for month% but I goon had to take to rnv bed,as I was quite incapacitated from work. spent £BO in twelve months upon that leg. One doc- tor's bill alone was £lO. " One Jay, as was hobbling about in the street, I I. met my old officer in the street, and he said • Bsate, you , are in a fine mess ; You must go to the hospital 'and he gave me a ticket. There I went nett day in a ity, with some of toy belongings, and three electors caine and dreev long races over my leg. 'Are you the man that has come to have your leg off ?' said one to me. • For goodness sake, doctor, take it off as quickly as you can,' I replied, • and rid me of my pain. The doctor said • My Poor fellow, we cannot take the responsibility of cutting off your leg in the present state of your blood.' I left the Cheltenhem General Hospital a hopeless and miserable man. Then passed a weary time," continued Mr, Boats. " I hardly know bow I lived those eighteen months. I tried all sorts of remedies, some of which seemed to ease me, but not for long, and my leg swelled until it was almost the size of an elephant's. I was obliged to stay in bed, and I well remember awakening from a kind of stupor one afternoon to find the undertaker measuring me with the three-foot rule he carries with him. It gave me a horrid itart, I can tell you. Some days after—it must be two mouths ago now—I managed to go down- stairs, and there met Mr. Morris, a neighbour, who in conversation told me that he had had a leg like mine, but not so bad, and that he had cured it in no time. ,s For goodness sake, man, tell me bow you did it," said 1. • Simply by Dr. Williams' Fink Pills for PtL e People,' he replied. That night I sent and bougut a box. After the first few doses I noticed a change for the better in my leg ;I felt better in myself, too ; and I have been taking these pills regularly ever since, so that now, with the exception that I have to wear an elastic bandage, I am as well as ever I was In my I fe. Mr. Boate then undid the bandage in question, and exhibited his leg with some pride to the reporter. But for a few marks- and a slight discoleuration, it was as healthy a limb as one would wish to possess. " a ' miracle—that's what it is,". was Mr. Boate's emphatic comment. " Though," he entinued, " I am grateful to Provideece, yet I recognise that under Providence I owe my present condition to Dr. Williams, Pink Pills, and lam never tired of telling the fact to one and all for the benefit of other poor sufferers. True, Mr. Boate's recovery does seem like a miracle, but it is a miracle in which all can believe, even the most sceptical, because it has a scientific cause in Dr. , Williams' Pink Pille,which,by removing the impurities in the blood,went at once to the root of the mischief. They cure in the came way eczema and other skin disorders, ansenia or impoverished blood, rheumatism, sciitma, consumption, erysipelas, St. Vitid,' dance, rickets, and indigestion. They are ales a splendid nerve and spinal tonic, and thus have cured many cases of paralysie,loco- motor ataxy, neuralgia, and nervous headache: They are obtainable of all chemists, and from Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 48, diolborn-viaduct, London, at 2s. 93. a box, or six for 13s. 93., but are genuine only when hearing the fultname, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills Los- Pale People, printed in red on the pink wrapper.</p>
        <p>ONE MOMENT PLEASE! Sufferers from Gravel, Lumbago, Piles. Pains in the Bank, Dropsy, Wind and Water Complaint, Diseases of Kidneys, Bladder, Stone, Sciatica, Rheumatism, and Gout will find a positive Cure in Holdroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a small boa and If not satisfied your money will he returned. Prices is. lid., of all Chemists. or post free for 12 stamps, from HOLDROVed, Medical Hall. Cheekheaten. Yorks. Sold at 35, Bridge-street, Warrington. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them write to the Proprietor and a boxiwill, 1-wleent neXt post.—Advt. •</p>
