      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>DECREE GRANTED.</head>
        <p>DECREE GRANTED.</p>
        <p>In the Divorce Division on Tuesday, Mr. Justice Barnes and a special jury resumed the hearing of the petition of Mrs. Edith Marianne Walker (nos Noad) for a judicial separation by reason of the alleged cruelty of her husband, Mr. Andrew Barclay Walker (son of the late Sir A. B. Walker, of Liverpool), who has an income of £20,000 a year. He answered denying the charge. Mr. Lawson Walton, Q.C., Mr. Crump, Q.C., and Mr. Temple Franks appeared for the petitiener ; and Mr. Carson, %C., Mr. H. B. Deans, Q.C., Mr. C.F. Gill,and Mr. Willock for the respondent. Further evidence was given in eupport of the peti- tioner's case.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Prince,a yacht steward, said that he first became acquainted with Mr. Walker in hovember 1895, and in February of the following year be went with him In his yacht to the Mediterranean. Mr. Walker took more drink than was go d for him. In 1E95 Mrs. Walker left the yacht and went on shore. She stayed at the lams hotel that a gentleman did who had been on the yacht. When Mrs. Walker returned to the yacht her husband was very angry and convened with the crew. He told her it was an hnproper thing to do. In August 1897 Mr. Walker spoke to his wife about her conduct be- fore marriage, and he wanted her to answer a few ques- tione,which she refused to do. Witness received instruc- tions from Mr. Walker to accompany Mrs. Walker, and put her and her luggage on there. He left her at Ports- mouth at an hotel. On the yacht when he returned was the Duke of Manchester. Cross-examined : He never saw Mr. Walker use vio- lence to his wife,or ever heard bins use coarse or abusive Language towards her, whether drunk or sober. Mr. Walker asked her t 3 admit the truth of some stories he had heard, and said that he would forgive her. She made no reply. I This being the close of the petitioner's case, Mr. Car- son, Q.C., submitted that there was no case upon the evidence on the petition properly to his submitted to the I jury. The petitioner had sworn that before going to ,Cowes in August 1397 she had forgiven her husband, so Ithat up to that time there would be a complete condona- tion. After that he submitted that there was no evi- dence of legal cruelty, especially after tke tecent decision , of Russell v. Russell, in the House of Lords.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lswson Walton, Q C., submitted that there was ample evidence to go to the jury of distinct acts of ernelty. With regard to condonatioo,there was no such plea raised. Conduct on the part of the husband which constituted legal cruelty revived the previous cruelty, anal removed the bar of condonation.</p>
        <p>Hie Lordship: lam of opiuion that there is a case to go the jury, and the case must proceed. Mr. Carson.Q.C.,said that he did not intend to address the jury then' but to call his witnesses. Dr. ManlevTims, examined by Mr. Gill, said that on March Bth, 1895, he was sent for to see the respondent at 89, Piccadilly. The petitioner came in. He was in- formed that there was to be a wedding on that morning Mr. Walker was In a state of absolute intoxication, and in a condition bordering on delirium tremens. Witness said that the marriage must be put aft at once, and sent Mr. Walker to bed, after which respondent became wildly delirious. Did you speak to the lady ?—I told her that the mar- riage must be put off, and that Mr. Walker could not possibly go through the service. Did you say anything about the clergyman P—l said that no clergyman would marry him in the state he was in.</p>
        <p>Did you see the lady later on ?—Yes, and she was anxious to know how Mr. Walker Was going on. I told her that he was suffering from chronic alcoholiqm, aud that as far as I could judge I did not think be could be</p>
        <p>reclaimed in any way. As gently as I could, I told her ' I thought it a pity she had made up her mind to marry him, and that it would be a source of great anxiety and care for her, and she would be very unhappy. By Mr. Crump On the occasion of my fiat visit I was told that the lady intended to marry the respon. dent.</p>
        <p>Mr. Reid Walker, brother of the respondent, said that Mr. Andrew Walker unfortunately took to habits of intemperance, which had been his misfortune for about five years. In April 1835. pstitiouer called on him, and said that she was engaged to be married to his brother. Ac delicately as he could he pointed out his brother's failing, and begged her to consider tbat before she mar- ried him.</p>
        <p>John Collins, Mr. Walker'd valet, said he never saw his master strike the petitioner, threaten ber, or use violent language towards her. He heard she was advised to lock her door at night. Cross-examined :On one occasion the respondent had to be held down by two men. He knew Mn. Walker was nervous of her husband.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Eve Mary Aitchison. a widow, said that she 11118 formerly the wife of John Robert Aitchison, whom she divorced in 1888. Last may the respondent called upon her when she was living at Bt. Margaret's, Twickenham. Upon being qu-etioned as to a conversation which passed between them, Mr. Crump objected. His Lordship : Are lon going to call Mr. Walker Mr. Gill (examining counsel) said that be was not. He contended that the conversatien should be given in evi- denco,as it had a bearing on the case to show Mr. Walker had good reason for his allegation against his wife. His Lordship: You want me to try an issue of conduct before the marriage.</p>
        <p>Mr. Carson : With the view of the jury estimating Mr. Walker's conduct.</p>
        <p>We L-irdshlp : The difficultv is this: unless Mr. Walker is called we would not know he knew of this matter at the time of the marriage. Mr. Carson sudmitted that the jury should be ao. quainted with the whole facts of the case. His Lordship ruled that this evidence was not ad. missible, having regard to the absence from the widaess• box of Mr. Walker. The reports of detectives in the Aitchison divoroe case were also held to be inadmissibleafter Mr.Wood,a deter, tive,had tendered his evidence.i It arose in this way. Ha said he had watched Mr.Aitchison and Miss Edith lima Noad.</p>
        <p>Mr. Caz,ou Were you in court on Friday 2—Witness: yes. I MA w Mrs. Walker in court and aleo in the corri- dor of tlic court.</p>
        <p>Is Mrs. Walker the Edith limes Noad you were watch- ing at 14, Pelham-street ? The question was thereupon ruled to be inadmissible. Other evidence of a similar charaeter was also ten- dered and refugii, his Lordship stating that it had no. thing to do with the case, the question for him only be- ing one of cruelty. At the close of the evldetre counsel addressed the jury on behalf of their respective clients. His Lordship, in directing the jury, pointed out that a greatadeal of irrelevant matter had been introduced into the case, and as soon as he asoeirtained that the respondent would not go into the witness box, he thought be was justified in ruling that the matters sought to be brought forward should net be admitted in evidence. The question wan whether the conduct complained of was sufficient to Mlle danger, or a rea- sonable apprehension of danger. to health, lite.or limb. Drunkenness was no excuse for any . acts of violence. Having gone through the evidence at length, his Lord- ship left to the jury to gay whether there was legal cruelty on the part of the respondent. . The jury retired to consider their verdict at a quar- ter past three After an absence of ten minutes they returned, and found the respondent was guilty of cruelty. His Lordshir thereupon granted the wife a decree of judicial separation with costs.</p>
      </div>
