        <p>1 TOW LE'S CHLORODYNE EMBAS7,OB rri (1 Ina.uaNzA. 1 (MLR'S CHLORODYNE BRONCHITIS, coNsumprzox. TOWLE'S CHLORODYNE ASTHMA, TOWLE'S CHLORODYNE SPASMS. ac. TOLE'S kAI (1 LORODYNE 130. per Bottle. W A. P. Tons 114.xcarsrsa.</p>
        <p>The approach of the opening Political Speeches. of Parliament seems to have re-invigorated some of the leaders of both political parties, and from the speeches made within the last few days by Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, Mr. ASQUITH, and Sir MICHAEL lids-BEACH the country will be able to form some idea of the political programme of the com- ing session. It is quite evident that domestic questions will not take a leading place in that programme unless something unexpected happens. The political speeches of the present day are al- most entirely devoted to such themes as the army and navy, colonies and commerce, and thel deep designs of foreign powers. Mr.CHAMBERLA/N was, in his Radical days, intensely absorbed in matters pertaining to domestic reform, but he is now Colonial Secretary in a Tory administra- tion, and that makes a difference. It was, we be- , Hove, Sir Thum&amp; HAncounr, who said that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN talks about the colonies as though he had discovered them, and certainly they have never been so loudly exploited as since Mr. CHAMBERLAIN took his present office. A few months ago some people seemed to hold the opinion that the most important place on the face of the globe was South Africa, but somehow or other the Colonial SECRETARY now appears to be quite willing to allow that colony to fall into the back- ground. When he was speaking at Liverpool on Tuesday one of his hearers interjected the query "What has become of IcittoEx ?" but Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, apt as he is at retort when it suits his purpose, carefully ignored the inquiry. He has had a very lively bout with the stolid and sturdy old Boer President, and there is a general impression that, taking everything into account, 1 he came off only second best. He is now,therefore, quite prepared to leave South Africa and its rather intricate racial problems severely alone,and perhaps that is on the whole the best thing that could happen for South Africa. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN is now mainly interested in the fate of the West Indian islands. The staple product of these Crown Colonies is cane sugar, but the trade in that class of sugar has '-leen almost annihilated by the competition of beet sugar which is sent into this country from the Continent, and especially from Germany and France. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN now proposes that a large sum of money should be taken from the British Exchequer and handed over to the planters and sugar exporters of the West Indies to tide them over their period of dis- tress. Of this proposal the best that can be said is that a grant in aid is better than a protective tariff upon foreign sugar, but at the same time it is to be hoped that when Parliament is asked to take the substantial subsidy out of the pockets of the British taxpayers there will be a searching inquiry as to the real reasons for the proposal. We have before us a pamphlet entitled "Should sugar be shackled ? " written by Mr. GEORGE MATHIESON, who has a close acquaintance with the sugar trade in this country, and he is of opinion that much of the depression in the West Indies is due to the antiquated and unbusinesslike methods of the growers and factory-owners. Mr. MATHIESON de- scribes Barbadoes as perhaps naturally the finest island under the sun for the growth of sugar, but he says the planters persist in producing it "under the conditions of 200 or 300 years ago, whereby they lose fifty per cent, of the extractable sugar in the cane, and in addition produce a sugar worth £2 or RS per ton less than sugar produced by an improved system." Owing to defective machinery and methods in some parts of the West Indies it takes sixteen tons of cane to make one ton of sugar, while the same amount of the product can to had from ten tons of cane in Egypt, nine tons in</p>
        <p>Queenland, and eight tons in Hawaii, and the Egyptian and Queensland sugar is worth £3 a ton more than the West Indi&lt;in article. If this be tho case there are certainly two sides to this ques- tion of relief for the West Indies. British manu- facturers and traders will be asked to contribute through the taxes to this relief,which Mr.CusicsEa- LAIN says, is to be "substantial," yet there is not one of these manufacturers who does not know that if he allowed himself to fall hopelessly behind his competitors in his machinery and methods he would speedily lose his business, and would have no prospect of obtaining relief from public funds. It-is understood that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S latest ambition is to develop the backward parts of the E mpire, but it is well worth considering whether the best way of attaining this end is to give alms on a wholesale scale or to show people the way to help themselves. There can be no doubt that unless the West Indian sugar growers throw aside their indolent conservatism in business they will never be able to compete with the enterprising and scientific German, and imperial almsgiving I Will in the end prove to be a very doubtful boon. With regard to the other speeches referred to at the commencement of this article, that of Sir MICHAEL Hicits-Balcu shows very clearly that the chief efforts of Government will, as soon as Parliament meets, be directed towards strengthen- ing the army and navy. Sir 3elicitear. is of opinion that the British navy is at the present moment quite strong enough to sweep off the seas every hostile fleet, yet he is not content. In the coming Estimates provision will be made for more sailors, and, large as the fleet is, it is to be made larger by the building of more vessels.</p>
        <p>The Engineers' It may now, we suppose, be taken Dispute. for granted that the long stoppage of labour in the engineering in- dustry is coming to an end. The ostensable cause of the struggle was the demand for an eight-hours day in London, and this demand has now been withdrawn, but still the lock-out notices which affect far more workmen than did the original strike for the eight hours has not been declared at an end by the Employers' Federation. As a matter of fact, it has long been seen by those who have eyes to see that the demand for the eight- hours day was Lot the principal cause of the lock- out which has been gradually developed during the last seven or eight months, and therefore its withdrawal does not in itself settle the matter from the masters point of view. The Employers' Federation, or a dominating section of it, has de- termined to destroy, or, if not to destroy, to thoroughly cripple the Amalgamated Society of Engineers and the other trade unions concerned, and considering the immense resources of the Federation the only thing that could have frus- trated this object, was a universal rally of the trade union world to the side of the engineers. That rally did not take place to the extent that the crisis demanded, and the: result is that the employers have scored a very important victory. What the ultimate consequences of this great trade struggle will be it would be useless to attempt to predict. It is not at all unlikely that it may influence to a marked extent both the political and iadustrial future of the nation. When the working clasess of Australia were de- feated in the great strikes of 1890 they at once turned to politics for the accomplishment of their ends, and it must be said that they have been successful to a marked degree. As a rule they have worked with the Liberal or Radical parties and the labour men now control legislation in nearly all the Australian colonies. Whether any changes in that direction will result in this country from the engineers' abortive struggle for better conditions remains to be seen.</p>
        <p>If Mr. J. S. Haworth, the President of the Northern Society of Electrical Engineers, is a true prophet it would be advisable for Warrington Cor- poration to hurry up with its electrical supply scheme. Mr. Haworth predicts an immense deve- lopment of the use of electricity within the I immediate future, not merely in connection with lighting but for industrial purposes as well. He anticipates that electricity is about to work a 1 mighty revolution in the chemical trade whereby towns like St. Helens and Widnes will be trans- formed from the smoky and sombre places they are now into something a little more celestial. In that case perhaps even Sankey Brook will be rescued from pollution without any expensive litigation between the Corporations of Warrington and St 'Helens. Electricity is soon to play a prominent part in connection with other important industries. With- in two or three years, Mr. Haworth declares, it will be used in every engineering establishment in Great Britain for welding and other purposes, but he said nothing, perhaps because he had not heard of it in time, about Edison'a reputed discovery which is to completely revolutionise the iron trade. The Presi- dent of the Electrical Engineers entourages the makers of electrical machinery to extend their works, for he tells them that if they were to doable their premises forthwith they would not be in time to cope with the demand that is coming upon them. Another of his remarks is rather suggestive when taken in connection with the dispute in the engineering trade. It is sometimes said that British workmen are driving trade out of the country, but Mr. Rawoith states that he has learned from bitter experience that the demand for steam engines in this country altogether exceeds the supply. in other words,while trade does go out of the country it goes out because engines made by British engineers are in such great request that the demand exceeds the supply. That is a very different version from the one usually given.</p>
        <p>A few weeks ago we suggested that if the mere hers of the County Council were invited to War rington to see the need for a new bridge with their own eyes,it might perhaps be the best way to induce them to take a practical interest in the matteritwas not intended that this advice should be taken alto. gether eeriouely,but it appears that it was not so far wide of the mark after all. The deputation from the Corporation which has waited upon the Highways Committee of the County Council last week seems to have met with a rather cold reception, partly on the ground that while some members of the County Council have been taken to inspect the bridge they were not, with one exception, members of the all- important Highways Committee. There seems now to be little or no hope of enlisting the support of the County Council or. behalf of a new bridge, and no doubt the Corporation will hesitate some time before it embarks upon an expedition of £lO,OOO entirely from its own resources.</p>
        <p>It cannot be said that the London and North Wes- tern Railway Company have shown any regard for the convenience of the public who have to make use of the bridge over the canal at Sankey Bridges, by declining to comply with the request of the Great Sankey Parish Council for a more suitable bridge to be placed there. The reasons given for this dnision by Mr. Harrison, the general manager of the company, are not altogether satisfactory. To throw the blame for the blocks and inconveniences which occur on the drivers of vehicles, because they</p>
      </div>
