      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>';e:c17:1.</head>
        <p>';e:c17:1.</p>
        <p>TB,REE CASTLES" TOBACCO.</p>
        <p>446 'THREE CASTLES" TOBACCO.</p>
        <p>46 THREE CASTLES" TOBACCO.</p>
        <p>L</p>
        <p>Willnot make more frequent use of the relief bridge —which bye the bye is not always open to them— is hardly justifiable. No one who knows the place in question would suggest to a driver of a heavy vehicle that he should make use of the unsuitably placed relief bridge. But the chief objection to the existing road bridge seems to have been lost sight of, and that is the danger to which pedestrians are liable in consequence of the bridge hav- ing been made without a footpath. In a quiet rural district such a bridge as that provided by the company at Sankey Bridges may meet all the requirements of the case, but when it is connected with a main road, and especi- ally in the case of such a busy highway as that between Warrington, Widnes, and Liverpool, it is totally inadequate for its purpose. Children who are compelled to cross this bridge in order to get to school, and even grown up persons are liable to accidents when walking across. The Railway Com- pany, however, seem to think it good enough for the purpose, and the only hope for any improve- ment would seem to lie in the Parish Council obtaining a promise of a footpath when the present bridge comes to be replaced at some distant date.</p>
        <p>Here are a few figures well worth the notice of licensing magistrates. In Birmingham in 1896,3,312 persons were dealt with for drunkenness, but in 1891' the number had increased to 4,044. These figures should be of special interest to Warrington- ians, as the licensing magistrates of this town are now annually invited at the Brewster Sessions to "re-arrange "the licensed houses of the town ac- cording to the Birmingham pattern. This re-arrange- Iment consists of the giving up by brewers of one or more licenses in parts of the town where pablic- houses are numerous in exchange far a license in a district in which such houses are fewer and farther between. That is no doubt a smart business tran- saction for the brewer, but what about the public whose interest it is that order and sobriety should prevail ? We have always contended that these bargains between the bench and the brewers are bad in principle and not calculated to be of ad- vantage to the town, and these figures from Bir- mingham support with extraordinary force the ar- guments used in these columns. Birmingham's li- censing methods were held up as an example to Warrington, yet in a single year there ha 3 been an enormous increase of more than 20 per cent, in its cases of drunkenness. The evidence from Birming- ham is quite in harmony with what has been laid before the Royal Licensing Commission as to the evils of spreading liquor-shops over localities which were previously without them, and in which there -was no popular demand for them.</p>
        <p>OPIIIVING OF NEW PREMISES. MILLINERY MILLINERY</p>
        <p>_ . 2dRB. TONGUE ---=4l to announce to her friends and the public /7 -enernily that she has opened the Drew/aloes 41, MEEEEIf-STREBT, WARRING.TON. al a Millinery and Draper Establishment, and boPes to receive a share of their patronage.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNE S.</p>
        <p>A RUNCORN tiOAT AGROUND..</p>
        <p>The boat" Yoli;;" a Runcorn, took the ground in Chester River when going to Liverpool on the 17th inst. She lifted the following day and proceeded on her jour- ney. No danger was caused.</p>
        <p>SEA-GOING STEAMERS DELAYED IN THE CANAL AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Friday last several sea-going vessels were delayed in the Ship Canal at Runcorn, owing to the dense fog. They were anchored to the pier wall under Runcorn Bridge until the following morning when the fog had lifted. They were on their way to Manchester.</p>
        <p>DakIJOIITS MATCH._</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, a team or eight men, from the Seamen's Institute, Runcorn, journeyed to Widnes and played a draughts match with one of the Widnes clubs. Twenty- six games wcre played, Runcorn winning 13, Widnes 11, and 2 were drawn. The games were very even and much interest was taken in the match. THE PHILHLRMONIC CONCERT. The Runcorn Philharmonic Society give their second concert on Wednesday next, in the Public Hall, Runcorn The principles engaged are Mews. Charles Fry of London Mr. J. R. Robinson, Lubbis N. Burch and B. Salkeld and the chorus numbers 100 voices. The principals are com- ing with a good reputation and a large audience is antici- pated. POLICE CASES. _ -</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning, before Mr. F. J. Norman, at the Runcorn Police Court,Patrick O'Grady was fined ss. for being drunk in Bridge-street the previous day.—On Thursday merning,before Messrs. F. J. Norman and W. Timmins, Richard Lewis was fined Sc. and costs for dis- orderly conduct in Church-street the previous day.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER GIFT FROM SIR J. T. BRENNER. On Tuesday night a deputation from the Winsford Liberal Club waited upon Sir John Brunner at Winning- ton fiall,Northwich,with a view to receiving his support for a scheme which is on foot in Winsford for the erec- tion of a new Liberal Club. Sir John, who is about to erect and present to Winsferd a guildhall, generously promised £5OO towards the scheme. The present pre- mises are very unsuitable,and a new club would be much appreciated in the district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>P.S. A. AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>P.S. A. AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday at the P.S. A. meeting held in the Bethe- sada Church on Sunday afternoon,Mr.F. Beck presiding, Mr. Harvey of Widnes,delivered a suitable address. Mr. E Worrall sang" The dream of Paradise,"with violin and obligato by Messrs. O. Ditchfield and A- Dutton,in a very feeling manner. Mrs. Leathwood rendered "Rest in the Lord" in an effective manner. A number of hymns were sung in which the audience heartily joined. BREAKING INTO • CABIN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. W. Timmins and P. J. Norman, at the Bannon' Police Court on Thursday, Janes Ellis aged 15 years, a cripple, residing at 22, Wivern Place, Runcorn, was charged with stealing one tin of treacle, one tin of oats, llb of tea, Sibs sugar,six pennyworth of biscuits a handkerchief, and a mutter, valued in all at 433. 51. from the it Christal,lying at Wigg's lay-by,on the 18th January. Mr. Shingler, master of the flit, said there had been considerable damage done to the boat. The lad was sent to a reforraatory for four years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>RUNCORN _LAD MISSINe.</head>
        <p>A RUNCORN _LAD MISSINe.</p>
        <p>The police have received information of the sudden disappearance of Walter Harvey, aged 14 years, of 20, English Row, Runcorn. The lad lisd been on the narrow boat " Nellv" and when at Lymm on the 12th inst. the captain sent him for a pint of beer at 7 p.m. The lad never returned and nothing has been seen of him since, and it is feared that he must have been drowned. He is of medium height, dark brown hair,fair complexion and was attired in long dark coat, cord trousers, blue cotton striped shirt, two pairs of socks.and an old pair of laced. up clogs.</p>
        <p>- ENGLAND FIDEL CHRIST. ' Special services will be held in St. Paul's ard Halton- road Wesleyan Chapels on Sunday next, in aid of Wes- leyan some Missions. The services at bcth places of worship commence at 10-30 a.in and 6 p.m, The preachers at both chapels are the Rev. R. J. Clifton and the Rev. C. W. L. Christien. On Monday the annual meeting will be held in St. Paul's Chapel, when an ad- dress will be delivered by the Rev. S. H. Hallam, of Liverpool. The circuit ministers will also deliver ad- dresses. Collections will be taken at all the services.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE CENTENARIANS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Booth, who resides at Wilmslow, and who cele- brated her 100th birthday last October, continues to appear in good health and spirits. She walks about, converses freely, amd still writes her own letters. She is residing with her 800, the Rev. J. Nowell, a retired Wesleyan minister. Mr. A. Nowell, the distinguished artist, whese picture, "The Expulsion from Eden," was purchased by the Liverpool Corporation, is grandson of the old lady. Mrs. Booth was a few weeks ago in Run- corn. She stayed with one of her relatives in Union- street.—Mrs. Hudson,the Weston Point centenarian who celebrated her 101st birthday on Christmas DAy, also continues to appear in good health. In a local cycle shop window there appears a photc of Mrs. Hudson on a tandem. She is on the front seat and a Mr. L. Jackson, of Greenway-road, is on the second.</p>
        <p>WESTON NEW CEURCE.</p>
        <p>The following appears in the last nnmber of " The British Architect," with respect to the St. John's Church, Weston, viz :—" Many are the country places improved and adorned by little groups of church and school and cottage buildings by Messrs. Douglas and Fortnum and we now illustrate an interesting little group of church and schools and village cross erecting at Westin. Run. corn, in Cheshire. A unique interest attaches to this little church because of the effora of the village choir boys to help forward the project. They have been so far successful that subscriptions have been received from 3,000 choir boys from all over the world. The Newcastle choir boys were so generous that they sent all the money they had collected for fireworks towards the fund. This is surely heart-whole devotion to architecture. The site and £5OO were generously bestowed by the Lord of the Manor, Majdr J. Cavendish Orred." LOYAL Bastin:ma LODoN, No. 300, M.U.0.0.. RUNCORN. The balance sheet issued in connection with this I lodge, which has just been published, shows a very sat- isfactory state of things. The amount of funds in hand 'December, 1896, was £405 les. so. The members' con- tributions for the past year amounted to £9O 2s. 00.., which, together with contributions from widows, inter- est,and entrance fees, brought up the total receipts of the sick and funeral fund to £ll2 Bs. Bid. The amount spent in full sick pay was £44 is. Bd.; reduled sick pay £l5 les'; levies to dietrict funeral fund £22 1.05.; widows 131. 4d., leaving the value of the sick and funeral lucid I £515 2s. 11d. It will, therefore, be seen that there has been a substantial gain on the year. The total worth of , the society's funds is £535 21. 51. The accounts have been audited by Messrs. Joseph Ford and J.H. Newman. It is proposed to commence a juvenile branch in con- nection with the lodge. RUNCORN Fenn LIBRARY Contsurrea. On Tuesday evening the monthly meeting of the above committee, was held in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall, Mr. D. Bisbrown presiding. There were also present Mr. W. Handley, Mr. T. Riley, and Mr. J. D. Jones &lt;librarian and secretary). The librarian reported that he had received the following gifts :—From Sir J. Brunner, Coate's " Gems of Illustrations:" from Mr. Wesley Cooke; " Gordon's Journsls of Bartousn." Free Library report from Bradford, and also catalogue of music in the Warrington Free Library.—Accounts to the amount of £l9 18s. 6d. were passed for payment. The following books had been issued during the month of December :—Lending Department I—Theology 33, philo• sophy 14, science 77, history 101,postry 11, fictien 1324, miscellaneous 200, French literature I, music 11. total 1772, decrease on corresponding month of last year 237. Reference department :—Miscellaneous 40, total 40, de- crease on corresponding month of last year. 9.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS. At the Runcorn Petty Sessions on Monday,before Mr. A. Thomas and Mr. A. R. Norrnan.lames Cook, a sailor, was summoned for using obscene language in Suffolk-st. lon the 2ad January.—Defendant pleaded not guilty.— P.C. Gregory said that at 10.30 p.m. on the date named be was on duty in Suffolk-street when he saw defendant kicking at his father's door. He was making use of very bad language and witness told him that he would report him—Superintendent Okell said the case was ad- journed last Monday as defendant did not appear.—The I magistrates imposed a fine of ss. and costs or fourteen days. The fine amounting to 13s. 6. was paid.—John ' Bostock Forster pas granted permission to sell at the Cornwall and Devonshire Arms lately occupied by Wm. Wray.—Samuel Spann was also granted to sell at the Albion beerhouse in Netson-street.Runcorn.—Betsy Wor- rail was fined ss. and Ts. 4s. costs or 14 days for disor- derly conduct in High-street on the 16th January. r.c.,e Gregory and Upton stated the facts of the case.</p>
        <p>CHARITY CONCERT AT. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening in the Public Hall, Runcorn, a grand concert waz given in aid of Mr. James Harrison, of 4 Wivern Place, Runcorn, wh met with an accident six years ago,which has rendered him incapable ef earn- ing any means of livelihood. There was a large audi- ence and the following programme was gone through : Grand fantasia, "Joan of Arc," Widnes Subscription Prize Band ; song." Come Beloved" (with violin obli- gato), Miss Clara Taylor, R.A.M. ; song, Mr. G. pear- son ; song," Skipper," Mr. T. Johnson ; duet, " May. ing," Miss Simkin and Miss C. Taylor ; song, "The voice of one we love," MiSEI Maggie Hill ; euphonium solo, (Selected), Mr. T. Hynes ; comic song, " De- laney's arcken," Mr. W. Hough ; selection, 6. monea in Egypt" (Itossini's Sacred Opera). Ppecially arranged for the Brass Band Contest, held at Belle Vue, 1897, Widnes 'Subscription Prize Baud ; duet, "Gipsy Countess," Miss Davies and Mr. E. Johnson ; song. " Queen of the Earth," Mr. T. Johnson ; violin solo," Alice," Miss Tem- perley ; song (selected), fdr.A. Shingler ; trombone solo, " The Village Blacksmith," accompanied by the band ; CORIIe song, " Windy," Mr. W. Hough ; God Save the Queen." All of the items were well received and most of them were encored. Mr; W. Cooke. A.R.C.0., wag the accompanist, and Mr. J. H. Harper officiated as conductor,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 21, 1898:</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 21, 1898:</p>
        <p>A FORMER RUNCORN LADY FOUND DROWNED. On Wednesday morning the dead body ot a well- dressed woman, of ladylike appearance, was picked up in the Mersey opposite the Herculaneum Dock, and de- posited at the Prinee's Mortuary. The woman has been identified as the wife of Henry Lewis, of No. 1, Lilir- street, Rock Ferry,formerlv of Thomas-street, Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>, CAMDEN WESLEYAN CHURCH.</head>
        <p>, CAMDEN WESLEYAN CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Special sermons wvre preached at the Camden Wes- leyan Church, on Sunday, in aid of the trust funds. The Rev.C. W. L. Christien occupied the pulpit in the morn- ing, and in the evening the 11,w. R. KOHN F.R.A.S., preached. The choir sang two anthems Awake, Awake," and " Those that wait upon the Lord." Collec- tions were taken for the trust fund. On Monday even- ing the Rev. Mr. Killip delivered a lecture in Brunswick School, on the subject" Old SA and his family. or the solar system." Mr. E. 'raison, C.C., presided. The school was crowded and great attention was paid to the lecturer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S CHURCEI, VICTORIA-ROAD, RUNCOR,I.</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S CHURCEI, VICTORIA-ROAD, RUNCOR,I.</p>
        <p>The tea and entertainMent given by the married ladies connectel with the above &amp;ace of worship, on Wednes- day last week, reAlised £22 53. 81. The married mons' tea and enrertainmmt realised about 1.13 103., the single mens' proceedings £lO. Toe ladies are to be con4ratula- ted on their success, especially as the proceedings were similar to the other events, but of course Wednesday's performance was in capable hands. The following in a correct list of those who presided at the tables :—Mes- dames M4rris, Mostyn, Richardson, Branch, Hatton, J. Hewitt, Shaw, Warburton, Barnes, Baxter, T. D nason, W. Leach, Povey, Hopley, D. Jones, F. Hewitt, Davies, Soden, Johnson, Hatley, Robinson, Carter,E. Dobson, J. Leach, Hough and Inglesfield,Mrs. Morris and Mrs Burn were the accompanists at the entertainment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RIMCORN SAYINGS BANK. ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>RIMCORN SAYINGS BANK. ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the trustees and managers of Runcorn Savings Bank was held in the Board-room of the Gas Company, High-street, on Tuesday aft ,rnoon. Mr. E. Imison, C.C., was voted to the chair, and there were present Messrs. J. Handley, J.P., James Pritchard, T. Norman, R. Wright, J. R. Frith, G. Sumcerfield, W. Fisher, W. Hankinson, Joseph Pritchard, D. H. Jones, and W. Handley (actuary). THE ACTUARY'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Actuary's report read as follows :—ln presenting an epitome of the business of the bank, for the year en- ded November 20th last,ic was very encouraging to be able to state that the volume of business has been larger than during any year since its eseabliehmeat,now nearly 55 years agc,thus proving its usefulness to the general community and the confidence reposed in it as one of the oldest existing institutions in the town for the devel- opement of thrift and encouragement of habits of pro- vidence. The total number of transactions has been 3,707,an increase on the previous year of 329. The amount deposited has been £11.250 ss. Id., an increase over the previous year of £215 6s. 3d. The amount with- drawn has been £11,392 114.1d,an increase over the pre- vious year of £3,143 Ile. 6.1. This increase of with- drawals has been largely due to a demand for more remunerative investments, princi- pally on the part of friendly societies, a large number ol 'which deposit their funds with the bank,and may indicate a revival of that prosperity of the district which has been so long looked and hoped for. The sum of £92795. 01. as interest and dividends on stock has been paid to depositors, or added to their accounts during the year, and the total turnover has ex- ceeded the previous year be, £3,189 10s. 7d. The num- ber of accounts open is 1169 at against 1140, the previ- ous year, a net gain of 29. In the last annual report it was presaged that given a fair increase in the volume of business, the small additional cost of bank management caused byfadopting the suggestions of the Central Inspec- tion Committee and the extension of the bank hours -vonld be met .by an increased amount of business. This expectation has been realised and the than resultant sufficient o increased pay t h ryeev ter, s nu cost s thoaf s m a n beenagem a n t moreahnads also to add the sum of £8 19s Od. to the bank surplus. During the year to the regret of of all Mr. Simpson I felt compelled by his advancing years and the state of his health to sever his nfficial connection with the bank. In the long period of 49 years Mr. Simpson's active in- terest in its success has been unabated, he having dur- ing that period successively been manager, trustee and president, and for nearly 40 years he was its active trea- surer. His intended permanent removal from the town and his delicate state of health seem to render any hope of further active connection out of the question and his expression of regret at his severance after such a long and contino- ous official connection shows that the step is keenly felt by him. The bank has also during the year lost a valued supporter by the removal by death of Mr. W. F. Salkeld, for 30 years it actuary and for 12 years ene of Its trustees. His social position and the esteem and con- fidence in which he was so widely held did much to eetablish•the bank. And the position of the bank to-day is very largely due to his guidance and thoughtful care for its welfare. The auditors have during the year at short periodical periods thoroughly examined the accounts and at its close subjected the whole of the books and accounts to a searching audit. Their certifi- cate in statutory form is submitted. The amount owing to depositors on November 20th last was £38,120 Be. 2c1., and the assets of the bank, to meet this liability, were the amount lodged with the National Debt Commis- sioners, of £37,838 18s. 4d. and the amount lodged with Part's Banking Company, Limited, in the name of the treasurer, for current needs, of £382 12s. 2d., these sums being more than sufficient to pay every depositor in full by £92 2s. 4d. The Inspector from the Central Department has made his usual examination of the books and method of business, since which a letter has been received from the Department, stating that they are glad to note the way in which the number and amount of the deposit accounts have increased since the extension of the bank hours. THR ADOPTION OF THR REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the position of the bank was so admirably get forth in the report of the actuary that it was hardly necessary to take itp time by attempting in any way to analyse it. Last year it was predicted that if the amount with which the bank dealt could be in- creased to something like £40,000 they would get rid of what was really a difficulty with regard to meeting the expen;es of the bank. That prediciien had beau amply fulfilled, and last year the surplus had been increased by nearly £9. With regard to the references made to Mr. Simpson and Mr.Salkeld, he thought the bank had been singularly fortunate in the character of the men who had been associated with it,and he could only hope that those upon whom their mantles had fallen might wear them as worthily as they did, and in their day and generation make themselves as useful, both to the bank and the town at large. (Applause.) The Chairman dwelt upon the need for increased accommodation in the bank, and suggested that a committee should by ap- pointed so consider this matter and also a report from the inspector of the Central Department with regard to alteration of rules. Be moved the adoption of the actuarieg' report and the re-appointment of the auditors at the same remuneration as in the previous year. Mr. James Pritchard seconded the motion,which was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>MORE .ACCOMMODAVON WANTED.</head>
        <p>MORE .ACCOMMODAVON WANTED.</p>
        <p>The Actuary said the Inspector in his report sug- gested that u the income more than met the expendi- ture for management there might be a little more outlay in publishing the accounts. The present statement did not give quite sufficient information for advertising the bank. He further suggested that the managers should consider whether they ought not to dinpense with the charge for pass-books. As there were now always two officials in the bank during hours of business, the In- ' spector thought the time had come when the number of 'managers might be reduced to 11 and the number of trustees to LE. These numbers seemed to be the rule in all the banks in the country. Mr. James Flandlev moved that the Chairman, and Messrs. F. A. Lake, J. R. Frith, Joseph Pritchard and R. Wright be appointed a committee to consider the inspec- tor's report and also to consider the question of Is- creased accommodation for conducting the bank's busi- ness and report to a future meeting. Mr. James Pritchard seconded the motion. _ . . _</p>
        <p>Mr. Frith and others bore testimony to the inconveni- ence and discomfort that arose in the present small premises, of which the lease, the Actuary remarkedghad yet 11 months to run.</p>
        <p>The motion was carried. TIN NESIONLTION OF MIL J. SIMPSON.</p>
        <p>. . The resignation of Mr. J. Simpson was submitted and action thereupon deferred, until the report of the com- mittee as to rules had been received. Mr. J. Handley quoted figures to show with what remarkable accuracy the depositors were credited with their full interest notwithstanding the multiplicity of accounts.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said he did not suppose the bank would show the same growth of business if depositors did not get what they were entitled to in the way ot interest. The Actuary said over one-half of the accounts were amounts under £lO. The Chairman : That shows the hank is taken advan- tage of by working people to a large extent. A vote of thanks to the Chairman, whose character and abilities were eulogised by Messrs. Frith and J. Handley, concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>CLEAR COMPLEXION, LUXURIANT HAIR, spotless arms soft white hands, and shapely nails are produced to, &amp;TIM:Mk Seer, the purest, sweetest, and most effective skin purifier and beautifier of this or any age. It is so because it strikes at the cause of most minor cutaneous affections. viz.,the clogged, d, inflamed sluggish, or overworked pores. CuvicuizaSOlP derived its remarkable purifying and beautifying properties from Cl/vicuna, the great skin cure, but so delicately are they blended with the purest of toilet and nursery soap materials that the result is incomparably superior to all other skin and complexion soaps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>SEIOCKEVI STOLLY OF DRINK AND MISERY AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>SEIOCKEVI STOLLY OF DRINK AND MISERY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A morran SEND TO GAOL.</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Patty Session on Monday before Mr. A. l'hornas and Mr. A. R. Norman, Michael and Ann Corcoran, husband arid wife, living on Mill Brow, Run- corn, were summoned at the Instance of Superintendent Okell for negleeting ir children, Mary Ellen sod Ann, on the 13'h January and divers other days within the past six months. The male defendant pleaded not guilty and the female defendant guilty. Sergeant Lee ii posed that on Thursday afternoon about 150 p m. from complaints he received Ile, Vkited defendant's house in Ashworth's Row. Be th-re saw the second named defendant and told her he had had many complaints of the children being ill-treated and ha wanted to see them. The baby six months old who she now carried in her arms was lying in a cradle on an old straw bed, which was saturated with wet and smelled very bad. Witnees drew her attention to the condition of the cradle but she made no reply. Fie requ•sted the woman to undress the child. The only clothing on it was a frock and chemise. They were very dirty, smelt very badly, and were full of vermin. The little child's back was all covered with bites from the vermin. Witness called her attention to the condition of the child and she replied " I know it." Witness Said" You ought Is be ashamed to know it:" He examined the cnild's head and it was totally Mille with these insects. He asked to see the child's bed. Tee female defendant showed him an old bedstocks and bed where she said the child slept. The colour of the bed was as black as his (witness's) great coat. The small from it was dreadful. Witness came downstairs and the woman had her own bodice unbuttoned. Her breast was all black and she was filthy herself. Witness asked her where the other child was and she said at the Catholic school. He went to thel school and brought the child Mary Ellen seed seven years back to the house. When he got back to the house ' the woman had gone. Witness got two neighbours to undress the child. Her clothing consisted of a frock, petticoat and chemise. They were alive with vermin. Her head was completely covered. Across the chhcl'a shoulders were red marks as if the child had been rubbing against the wall. There were also blue marks on the back of the child which she said had been done by her mother. When at the school he told the schoolmistress about the condition of the child and she replied " I have noticed it." Witness spoke to the male defendant about the neglected state of his children and he said he kilo', ab nit it. Wit- ness had cautioned them several times before. He exiled on Friday niglet but thole) appeared to be no improve merit. He then said "I always give her my 'nanny and what she did with it ks didn't know unless she spent it ia drink." The female defendant was of very drunken habits and on Saturday n;ght they were both drunk. When at the house on Thursday the 13414 January the woman said her husband never gave her any money and said she only got Is. that week and 33. the previous week. The man told him that he gave her 18 i. the week witness called at the house and 164. the week before. Witness had received f a o. „Arcorans' wages for the last six weeks. Re worked at the docks, and on 3rd December he earned £2 43. fle. 10th D-cember, £1 84. 111 ; 17th December, 135. 14i1 ; 24th December 18a. 10i l.; 31st December, 10s. 10d.; 17th January, £1 2s, li. At present work at the docks Was very slack. The wages of a man who attended to his work averaged from 20s. to 30s. a week. Police Constable B )wyer said at 11 p.m. on the 12:h January he received a cnnplaint abut the woman ill- treating her children. Witness found Mary Ellen screaming in a neighbour's houes. He went into defen• dant's house which was in a wretched condition Tile woman was using most obscene language. Witnes spoke to the husband and he said he could not help it as she spent the money in drink. The male defendant was then under the influence of drink, and the woman Was drunk. He visited the house again. The husband wass out and the woman was sitting at the table with a few mashed potatoes before her. Ihe only furniture was a lamp. table, and a chair with no back to it. The man said there was no use getting furniture in as the female defendant Pawned it. This concluded the evidence of the police, and the male defendant then gave evidence on hie own behalf. He said he was a ganger employed at the docks. Ile produced a list of his weekly a ases showing that be and three other men received £9 103. a week for their work, and of course it had to be divided amongst them. The money he had given his wife on an average was 245. a week. Anything he bnuuht that was pawnabie it was always taken by her. Sae even to the clothing off her own back and pawned it. Re had done his best. Last Wednesday morning he cave her 3g. to pay the rent. When he came ho,s she wt.' out; and not a particle of food in the house. When he mice,/ the laudlord that morning whether his wit' pail turn paid any for nine weeks. He the rent he said she had not did not know what to do ;he had tried his best. He remembered being summoned in 1895 14t a On are(, of cruelly illtreating his children. It was thm withdrawn against him. Superintendent Okell: Why didn't you make fresh arrangements then Defendant : I have tried. Superintendent Okell : You are not a sober mars, but no doubt you have to put up with a lot. Ide not press the case against the man. He has certainly a lot to put up with. The female prisoner was then charged on a summons for using profane languaee in Windmill-et on thd 12th January.—P.C. Bowyer stated the facts of the case.— The magistrates committed the female defendant to prison for four months on the charge of neglecting the children, and 14 days for the istond offence. The Chairman (to Corcoran) : Tne charge against you Is dismissed. We think you did your best under the cir- cumstances. Sire is a drunken character, and you have' 1 no doubt some difficulty, but it is your detv to see that the children are kept clean and properly fed. We hope you will see after them iu the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>VICAR, OF RUNCORN. AND THE DUTIES OF CHURCH PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>THE VICAR, OF RUNCORN. AND THE DUTIES OF CHURCH PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>The Vicar, the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, speaking at the annual congregational tea party in connection with the Runcorn Pariah Church ,held on Monday night, referring to the Technical Institute, said that while differing from ..(,r „Tabu Brunner on many vital points— on that oc7,asion, h:a thought the man was a very poor patriot—he ire-ant in the sense -f a R incorn patriot— who did not feel deeply grateful to bir John Brunner fot his extreme and tnixohcited generosity—(applause)—in enabling the fecniiical Institute to be free from debt, and he thought those among them who were strong Con- servatives ought to be all the more ready to acknow- ledge such generosity pat because they did differ from him. (applause.) Since h. 9 had been Runcorn he had Lit that the Churchmen the town did not exercise the influence in ordidary tow, matters which they ought to do. (Real. bear.) 'mere were one or two people who certainly did exercise some influence, but they were only one or .two. Time after time he had been asked to go to public meetings on matters con- cerning the town's hu.iness, and when he had got there and looked round the raoin,he found himself surrounded by Nonconformists. All honour to them, but he should like to see Churchmen there. This stato of things was to be found in their representation on th.- Board of Guardians. Why should he (the Chairman) he the only Churchman on the Board? He did not like to see mem- bers of the Church of England keep themselves in the background.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CHA.RGE AGAINt.,r A FORMER, RITNCORNIA.N.</head>
        <p>THE CHA.RGE AGAINt.,r A FORMER, RITNCORNIA.N.</p>
        <p>I At the Stockport Borough Police Court, on Saturday. Thomas Cookshott, The HAMM, Queen's-terrace, Padi- baui,described as a schoolmaster and a bachelor of arts, was charged on remand with obtaining £6 by means of a cermio forged telegram at Stockport on November 27th. 1897 —Mr. Dobson, depute towntclerk,prrsecuted, and said Mr.Ssmusl Meggitt was the managing director I of a stesl comp toy at Sheffield, and in November last he went to Cork, and there gave instructions for his letters to be addres,•l to Holyhead. The prisoner saw a letter at the Holyhead Hotel,addressed to Mr.ldeggitt, and on November 21 the manager of the hotel re- ceived a telegram from Chester, signed Samuel Meggitt, asking for his letters to be ad- dressed to him, "Paste Restante," Stockport. The prisoner came to Stockport and went to the Manchester Arms Hotel.He sent a boy to the Post Office to adr for a letter for Mr. Meggitt. The officials of the Post Office re- fused to give up any letters as the Request sq as not signed. Then the prisoner went himself,and signed the name Samuel Meggitt. The prisoner received a letter which was from Mr. Price, of Sheffield. The prisoner wired to Mr. Pric that he was short of money, and ask- ing for a remittance by telegraph. A telegraph money 1 order was received for LB, which the prisoner cashed.— After other evidence had been given the prisoner on be- ing charged, said "I reserve my defence." He was then committed to take his trial at the next Assizes at Liver- pool.</p>
