      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Council, was held on Tuesday afternoon, at Dutton Workhouse, Mr. J. mebirvre j.p.. presiding. There were also present, Messrs. H. 'Falb J.P., T. Baxter, C.C., J. Knowles, T, Wright, G. Fang, J. Spruce W. Pickering, J. Darby. shire, E. dat,lll, S. Woodward, S. llooton,T. Darlington. J.B. Chadwick, T. leris. J. Parr, J. M. Williams, J. Stokes, with Mr. J. Ashton (clerk) Mr. G. F. Ashton (deputy clerk) Mr. Swinton (surveyor) and Mr. J. Far- rington, inspector of nuisances.</p>
        <p>THE- COONCII. INVESTED _WITEI _CERTAIN URBAN POWERS.</p>
        <p>A circular was read from Mr. S. B. Provis assistant secretary to the L-,e41 Government Board enclosing two C pie. of the order which they have issued investing the Rural District Council of Runcorn with certais Urban powers as regards certain portions of their district, and declaring the e cpea-es incurred by the Council in the execution ot thes.: piwers to be special expenses. He plintwt out that the Order would come into force on the 14!h Februarv,and that Article ".required that it should be published in some local newspaper before that data.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>ACIBItS Lkl4l.</head>
        <p>ACIBItS Lkl4l.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Grundy, Kershaw, and Co., solicitors, Man- chester, wrote reterrmg to a resolution passed by the District Council on the 31st August, consenting to the stopoin .i, up of a p)rtion of Ackers-lane. The writer also adled that the Latchford Without Parish Council's re- solution was not properly confirmed until after the pas- sing of the District Council resolut:on. Mr. Greeaall, the magistrate's clerk, wrote them that he could not advise the magirrates to give the usual certificate unless the D.strict Council's resolution came after the Parish Council'a confirming resolution. They should be glad if the Council woull pass another resolution in the terms of the last one and send them a copy signed by the chairman of the meeting.—On the motion of Mr. Pilling the resolution of the Parish Council was confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RELSBY W&amp;TEEL CO.</head>
        <p>THE RELSBY W&amp;TEEL CO.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Woods, secretary of the He!shy and District IVatt.r CI., Limited, wrote stating that a.; their Board meeting on the sth inst., he wee instructed to say that Why a.q.reed to the rates proposed in the letter of toe Council dated 22ad ult.,and also to the draft agreement which he now retired. Mr. U. C.Taylor was preparing the plsns, and same would he sent in due course.</p>
        <p>NORTON AND RALTON ROADS.</p>
        <p>letter was read trona Messes. Potts, Potts and Gar- dener, solicitors, Chester, enclosing a copy of the Bill in reference to; the Norton and Halton Roads : the object of which is to relieve the owner of Norton Priory kstate from the liability to repair certain roads and so provide for the future maintenance and repair thereof.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>TAR LIGHTING OR LATCHFORD LOCKS.</head>
        <p>TAR LIGHTING OR LATCHFORD LOCKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Henry Hunter, chief engineer to the Ship Canal Company, Manchester,again wrote drawing atten- tion, to the fact that there was no statutory obligation newt th. 4 cornuany.to provide for the lighting of Latch- ford Locks, and, therefore he could not recommend his direotora to take upon themselves unnecessary obliga- tion 4 far which they received no consideration. Mr. Pilling said a committee had considered this matter and they were of opinion that they had no power. The matter wi ultimately referred to the Parish Council. The Clerk was instructed to send the Parish Council a copy of Mr. Hunter's letter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>CONTEMPLLTED IMPROVEMENTS IN TRODSMLM.</head>
        <p>CONTEMPLLTED IMPROVEMENTS IN TRODSMLM.</p>
        <p>Mr. Re,giaald Potts, clerk to the Chezhire County Council, writing under date 13th January,stated that he was informed by the county Surveyor that he was aware that this entrance (the approach from Main Road Into Ship-it., FrodAham) was bad and to put the same right would necessitate at present considerable expense. The corner had wool in its present state for many years and,thecouuty Surveyor considered it would take no irnaldir a littler longer particularly as their committee ht 1 in nontemplttion conpiderable improvements to the footpaths throughout the whole of the village.</p>
        <p>THE CALLS: TUE COUNCIL AND DEFAULTING TOWNSNITE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward reported that the balance in hand after the previous meeting was £1,324 ss. lid. The receipts am.unted to £144 94. 61., and the cheqaes drawn that day were clas 3s. 6d., leaving a balance in hand of £1,303 118.11 d. In rPoly to Mr. Aaron, Mr. Woodward said the town- ships of Grappenhall, Stockton Heath, and WhiLl-y Superior were defaulters in respect,of their calls. . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Aaron raid this Bart of thing had been going on for nearly 30 years, and certain townships did not pay one call until another was due. The Chairman said he bad asked the clerk to take prompt steps in the matter. CANAL soave acre' 1877. AND 1684. Mr. Farrington. inspector of nuisances. reported that during the year which had just closed he had inspected 89 boat.. The condition of the boats and their occupants continues to improve, more especially the boats which have no females on board. (A). One boat was met with which was not duly registered. (B). No notification, of change of master received. (C). Two mate were met with which were without certificates. (E). Overcrowd- ing—none. (F). Separation of sexes—no complaint. (G). Cleanliness and ventilation aatisfactory. (H). Re- quiring painting—none. Defects in marking—one. (I). Without water vessels—none. (K). Removal of bilge water—no complaint. (L). No refusals of admit- tance to the inspector. No legal proceedings had been taken during the past year. Two official notices had beed issued. One ~fficial notice complied with no cases or infectious diseases had been met with. There had been no boats detained for cleansing. Nunaber of boats on the regiaterone. Number of boats registered in 1897 number of women with boats inspected,49. Number of boats re-inspected during the year, 5, which reduce i the number of women to 46. Number of children with boats between 5 and 12, years of age, 20. Number of childreu with boats 5 years and under 30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNOORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNOORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the above Board of Guar- dians was held on Tuesday afternoon, at Dutton Work- house. Mr. J. Knowles presiding. There were also p:e. sent, the Rev. Canon Wood, Mr. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., Mr. A. R. Norman, J.P., Mr. S. Yarwood, the members of the Rural District Council,with Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), and Mr. G F. Ashton (deputy clerk). 'MR LADIES VISITING COMMITTEE. The minutes of the Ladies Visiting Committee were read. The chief item was in reference to the tea and entertainment given by the ladies, to the inmates of the house on the Wednesday previous. Mr. Norman pro. posed that a very hearty vote of thanks be accorded to the ladies for the excallent treat they gave to the in- mates and Guardians.—Sfr, Stokes seconded and said he was one of those few Guardians who were present. He WAS very sorry to see such a poor attend- ance of 'Guardians, Runcorn was very pawls, represeutel—Mr. Derbyshire supported the resolution. He learned on that occasion that the ladies were doing an excellent and most useful work in catering for the in- mates.—r. T. Wright bore out all that had been said by the previous speakers and the resolution was carried unanimously. THE AR unt. DAuirsaraz wANTs MY AND NAVY ABOLISZBD 0 TO MORROW." Mr. A. Wellesley BreintleT, ptain and superintendent of the Liverpool Training Ship "Indefatigable," wrote stating that his committee having obtained the Local Government Board'rcertifriate were now in a position to accept boys from Poor Law Sohools. His committee hoped the Guardians wenld he able to send them some boys of good physique to train for the Royal and Mer- chant navies ; there was a fine opening for them in both services and at present a brisk demand.— The Master, in reply to a question, said they had two boys already on the ship.—Mr. Taylor : How long are they supposed to be there ?—The Master : Three years —Mr. Taylor : And have we Ts. 8.1. to pay—The Mister: I don't think we paid so much for the others.—Mr. Taylor : And then we have to pay RS for their clothing at the end of their term.—Mr. Derbyshire proposed that the letter lie on the table. He wanted the army and to-morrow if it was the(lapurgohtetcetri on of this country to a navy es sib baen d atboo ei sahveed higher power if it was necessary.—This proposition was not seconded and the matter dropped. TIRE BOARD ROOli.</p>
        <p>_ The Local Government Board forwarded copy of an M mOrdkourn to be enlarged ation to be provided order they had issued authorising the Guardians to accommodation ac an ud sela tvhaet oßroya ar dnrdoom other a t he Wm at a coat net exceeding £.500, and consenting to the proposal of the guardians to re-pay the loan of £5OO within a period of 20 years.—ifter some discussion Mr. Joynson, of Runcorn, wag appointed clerk of the works, the remuneration to be £ls.—Tbe motion was carried by the Chairman's casting vote. TIES NURSING STAFF AT TES Noun. Mr. Wright reported that the committee which was appointed to consider the question of the nursing staff at the Workhouse recommended that an additional nurse he appointed at a salary of £25 per annum. He moved that the recommendation be adopted.—The motion was carried. THIS HEROICAL OFFICERS AND VACCINATION WITH CALF LIMPS.</p>
        <p>, With reference to the use of calf lymph as recom- mended by the Guardians, Dr. Burton of East Bank, Frodsharn, wrote stating that he thought ed. as a pro,. posed allowance was inadequate seeing that one tube of calf lympb costs lc, case Id., postage Id., making Is. 21. in all. If be vaccinatcd one child at a station he lest 81,, and this, again, was mere unlikely to be successful than if taken from another arm. One shilling per ease would about cover the extra expel:loc.—Dr. Barry S.Bower,of Stretton,near vvarrington.wrote in similar terms.—Dr. Carruthers, of flalton,also wrote stating that he was willing to provide and make use of calf lymph for the rum of one shilling in addition to the usual fee. He c-mld not undertake it for a less sum.—Dr. Selby, of the Rock. Frodrisam, inti- ' mated that the new regulations re-vaccination with calf lymph met with his approval.—Me. Woodward reported 1 that the Vaccination Committee had had before them at their meeting that morning several letters from medical officers of health of the district in reference to vaccine. tion with calf lymph. They stated that the sum of ed. as the suggested payment fee vaccination with calf lymph was insufficient as the actual cost was Is. They asked the Guardians to reconsider th-ir former decision. The comnaittee decided to recommend the Guardians to</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>acceed to the doctors' request.</p>
        <p>rineNCE comltrrnss.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward, chaliWan-o—t the Finance Committee, reported that the amounts granted in out-relief were : Runcorn district £95 ; Frodsham £2l ; Budworth £2l. The cheques drawn that day amounted to £3268 83 10d, of which £2935 133 6d was a payment to the County Council on account of county and bridge rate. The balance in hand was £76 23 11d. The balance was very w, but when they handed their cheque to the County Council they would receive a cheque from the County Council, which wonld renew the balance. Whitley Superior had not paid its call. The report was adopted. .</p>
        <p>.. Mr. Woodward said._ the county call was less by £482 4s lid than last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
