      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD ENQUIRY AT RUNCORN. THE ALTERATIONS AT THE TOWN HALL</head>
        <p>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD ENQUIRY AT RUNCORN. THE ALTERATIONS AT THE TOWN HALL</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning at the Runcorn Town Hall, Mr. Robert H. Bicknell, an inspector of the Local Govern ment Board held an inquiry respecting au application of the Runcorn Urban District Council for power to borrow £3OO for the purpose of providing additional office ac- commodation at the Town Hall. There were present Messrs. Imison, C.C. W. Handley, F. A. Lake (law clerk), E. Marshall (s,cretary), and J. Wilding (sur- veyor),</p>
        <p>The. Law Clerk stated that the population at the last census was 20,000. The assessable value of the town- ship was £47,251, ann the outstanding loans amounted to £24,735. The loans for waterworks, cemetery, &amp;c., were under special Acts. The Town Hall was briginally a private house, and was purchased from Mr. Charles Hazlehurst in 1873, for use as a hospital under the Cholera Regulations but it was never used for this pur- pose.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Ilandley said it was used for a hospital for a very short time. There were two small pm( cases treated there. The building however, was found unsuitable for a hospital, and it was again occupied as a dwelling by Mr. Beasley. Subsequently the Local Government Board cave permission for the building to be used as a town hall.-</p>
        <p>Mr. Like said it was now found that the office acco- modation was inadequate, in consequence of offices hay- ing to be found for tne secretary or the Cemetery Com- mittee, and for the water works officals. Hence the proposed extension.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilding produced plans and explained in detail how it was intended to provide the requlred accomoda- tion on the E;erton-street side of tne building, with an entrance from that street into the Cemetery, and Water Offices.</p>
        <p>This concluded the inquiry, and the Inspector ma.de an examination of the buildidg.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>RUNCORN FOOTBALL NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FOOTBALL NOTES.</p>
        <p>The downtrodden Runcornians had a " social on Thursday evening last, at itilay's Cafe, to enjoy them- selves and also to talk over several important matters. Not one of the guests had any look of dismay on his face but all appeared in good spirite, aud of course departed in good spirits. Their countenanzes gave the comsnittee fresh life, as it were,and everybody present thought that there was yet something good in store for the 15 who formed the Runcorn Football tosin.</p>
        <p>The teams were in separate compartments, the reason why I canxot conceive, unless it was that the " first teamers " did not want their younger brothers to know of the dodges that were to be introduced forthwith on the field of play. The eatables which 'were of a teetotal kind were soon consumed, and the men commenced to put their heads together to devise some means of break. leg the spell of ill-luck which Itbey have sorely exper- ienced within the last eight weeks.</p>
        <p>Whether anything was decided upon I am not aware, but if anything was it certainly did not come off on Saturday. The change in the A team, viz.. Jolley at back and Houghton as three-quarter,did work as antic'. 'dated. Hoskins and Williams were tho shining lights on the Salford side, whilst Butterworth. Houghton, and Faulkner wore the champions on the side of the greens. The game in the first half erss very keenly contested. Houghton helpd the back division of the visitor's to m very considerably by his weight. Runcorn were at fault in their combination on several occasions. In the second half the home men held the upper hand and soon ran up a score of 20 poinze to 5.</p>
        <p>The "A" team match was the best which has been witnessed on the Irwell-lane ground for some time. Although Salford team included a county man and three first tearners, the boys in green not only kept them from crossing the border, but on several occasions held them penned to their own territory. W. E, Hough a cetera,' on an association field donned the green jersey for the the third time in his life, and played a splendid game. His running powers are great and he could easily out- diatauce any player on the field.</p>
        <p>The 11e4 of utilising the Di ill Rail twice a week for practising combination, &amp;c., is every good one, and it ths players will only stick to it, Runcorn will be again as popular as it was a few years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>SONS OF nTAIPERANCE.</head>
        <p>SONS OF nTAIPERANCE.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE GRAND DIVISION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting was held on Saturday afternoon in the Schoolroom, Greenway-road, Runcorn, Mr, E. fiallsworth, most worthy patriarch of the order,took the chair. A good number of officers and representatives attended from different parts of the county—Crewe, Northwich, Winsford, Lostock, Weston and Runcorn.— The officers gave their reports, bearing on the financial and numerical condition of the order, which, after full discussion, were accepted. There are over 500 divisions in Great Britain, wsth some 38,00 0 members, and a juvenile at cadet organisation of 370 sections and 17,000 members, it was decided to consolidate all the funds in the various divisions in Cheshire for sick and funeral purposes. After a full discussion rules were adopted for the cadets, and it is now antici- pated that there will be a large increase in the number. —The officers for the Grand Division were then elected : Mr.?. Leicester (Northwich), G.W.P. ; Mr. Thos. Cooke (Runcorn). G.W.A. • Mr. William Dunbabin (Weston), G. W.C. ; Mr. Edward Kay (Roncern),G.W.S.; Mr.Thes. Heesom (Runcorn), G. W.T.; Mr. A. Price (Crewe),G.W. Chaplain; Mr. A. Noden (Winsford), G.W. Sentinel ; Messrs.W. Heath (Crewe) and James Johnson (Runcorn) auditors. The three trustees appointed were Messrs J. Bowering (Crewe). Thomas Whyte (Runcorn),and Thos. J. Holt (Runcorn),—A. letter was read from Mr. John Jones. resigning the position of treasurer which post he has he'd for the last 25 yeare.Mr Daniel Bisbrown thought that they ought to place on record their appreciation of his services. He also referred to his consistency as a temperance worker. He moved a resolution in these terms, which was seconded by Mr. Thomas Cooke sup- ported by Messrs. J. Bowering, Thomas fiefdom and E. 13allsworth, and carried with applause. —Mr. J. Corm, (Crewe), was appointed G.P. of cadets, and Mr. Samuel Timperley was:selected as representative to the National Division meeting, to be held at Hanley in Whit-week.— It was decided to hold the next Grand Division meeting at Weston.—An eloquent address was given by the Chairman, Mr. E.Hallsworth,who urged the members to renewed efforts in extending their noble order.—The meeting than closed.</p>
        <p>R UNCO RN'S WATER SUPPLY. LECTURE BY DR. ANNETT. In the Runcorn Technical Institute on Tuesday, Dr. g. E. Anima delivered a lecture on " Micro-organisms (bacteria) in relation to the health of Runcorn.' ThFre was a large attendance. The lecture was illustrated by slides and prepared plates, thrown upon a screen by a !entire maisipulated by Mr. C. Handley. Dr. Annett showed his audience representations of the various kinds of bacillus at present known to the bacteriologist and explained their distinctive features, and the manner in which they multiplied and produced disease. Speaking of RUISCOTLI water, the Lecturer exhibited some plates, upon each of which had been introduced one quarter of a cubic ceatimeter of Runcorn water,and explained that 1 it was only those micro-organisms that grew at the tem- perature of the blood that could grow in man. In tap water drawn at 22 centigrade there were 500 micro-or- gaairms, and at 37 centigrade, or the heat of the blood, there were only IC • sample taken at the well-side con- tained 600 and at blood beat 4, bottom of the well 1900 and ,4, and reservoir 2400 and 450 respectively, at the twotemperatures named. The organisms on the plate containing water taken from the side of the well and the tap were not typhoid baccili. which would indicate sewage contamination • they wei, the nr‘httary organ- isms that grew in the atmorphere. The w•ater taken from the bottom of the wall chewed a larger number of micro organisms, because the water was disturbed by pumping, operations. Organising were even got from the rock in which water lay. Tim organisms in this sastiple were not such as represented sewage contamination. These samples were within the range of what might be called good water. A copy of Professor Bnyce'd report was then shown en the screen. It stated that the num- ber of micro organisms growing in a cubic centimeter of tap water at the ordinary temperature was 320, and at blood heat nil. The organisms growitg were the or- dinary organisms found in most waters. Professor Boyce concluded his report by saying " no evidence of sewage contamination," because, Dr. Annett, said there were no orgsnizients that would grow at the temperature of blood heat. Within the past few years Runcorn water had been analysed. The total solids were high and the chlorides were high. As compared with the average water Runcorn water was exceedingly good. He con- sidered Runcorn had little to fear in regard to its water supply.</p>
        <p>SALE—'W proceeding. Extraordinary Reductions in mILLINEET. JACKETS. CAPES, CLOAKS. DRESS MAMMALS. FURS, &amp;C., &amp;C., to clear Winter Stock. w. T. Wir..trann, SANYET-BTRILFCT, LEGH-lITREET, Warrington.</p>
        <p>- •2</p>
        <p>--`"`"`",-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>PIANOFORTE RECITAL AT PA RR</head>
        <p>PIANOFORTE RECITAL AT PA RR</p>
        <p>HALL.</p>
        <p>Mons. Arthur De Greef, a distinguished pianist who is rapidly rising in public favour, paid a visit to Warrington on Wednesday evening, and gave a recital in the Parr Hall before a large audience. The following is the programme :</p>
        <p>VARIATIONS in C minor Beethoven ALLEGRO AGITITO BERCEUSE Cho pin VA.teac, A flat Somenzo. B flat minor BEELPSODrE, B minor INTERSIEZZO-BERCEUSE Brahms NHAPSODIE, G minor Nocromrs NORWEGIAN BRIDAL PROCESSION Greig To THE SPRING MARCH OF THE DWARFS ALCESTE'S CAPRICE and FOGA Saint-Sai,ns VA-LSE-CAPRICE De Creel TARANTELLA The descriptive pieces by Edward Grieg—more es- pecially the Norw,egian bridal procession, were masterly examples of De Greef's ability as a piano- forte executant, and it is not surprising that the Norwegian composer declared him to be the finest interpreter of his works that he had ever met. It is a matter of course to say that the pianist's brilliant execution of the whole programme aroused the en- thusiasm of the audience, and on several occasions in response to repeated applause,he was constrained to bow his acknowledgments. The Valse-Caprice, one of the pianist's own compositions, showed both a breadth of treatment and charm of subject,and its performance called forth loud applause. The grace- ful Berceuse by Chopin, received careful and deli- cate treatment, and may be regarded as one of the triumphs of the evening, 3lent;_on should also be made of the pianist's fine performance of Lifzts' " Tarantella ' which brought to a fitting close a most enjoyable evening's entertainment. Mons De Greef created a most favourable impres- sion, and no doubt many who beard him on Wednes- day will be desirous of renewing his acquaintance at no distant date. His performance on Wednesday evening entitles him to take high rank amongst the virtuosi of the day and it should be placed on record that his reception by the Warrington musical pub- lic was warmly enthusiastic. One of Messrs. John Brinsmead and Son's grand pianofortes was used, and the rich quality of its tone and the perfection of its mechanism were subjects of remark amongst the audience. The local arrangements for the reci- tal were in the hands of Mr. R. H. Pearson, of Bold-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON T. WIDNES.--LIBERAL CLUBS. The return match between the Liberal Clubs of War- rington and Widnes, was played at Widnes on Thursday evening, the result being rather singular. At Warring- ton the home team won by 100 points, and on Thursday Widnes reversed this by 101 points, victory resting with them on the home and home matches by 1 point. Two of the games played by reserve men on the Warrington team were left unfinished. At the close Councillor Tlnnion prnposed a vote of thanks to the Widnes men for the hospitable way in which they had treated them, and Mr. Sadler cordially responded. The scores were as follows</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. WIDNES. 11 Woods 97 P Quinn G Strange B J Mitchell S J Beaman Halliwell l5O Whelan 94 G Beard A J Jones D Minion 0 Toole l5O H Deakin 124 J Rigby A Greenhalgh 5O Smith lOB J Bennlon</p>
        <p>Majority for Widnes on home and home games, 1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>PENiETH.</head>
        <p>PENiETH.</p>
        <p>El</p>
        <p>MISSIONARY Lecrune.—On Friday night last week, a lecture was given in the St. Mary's Mission Room me "Church missionary work in Africa." The lecturer was the Rev, J. R. Jones, and the various incidents and places of interest were shown by the aid of the lantern. There ores a large attendance,the collection in aid of the Missionary Society Funds amounting to £3 2s.loid.</p>
        <p>THR MANCHESTER IND LITERPOOL DISTRICT BANKING COMPANY, LIMITED. The half-yearly meeting of the shareholders WAS held on Wednesday a', the head offices, Spring Gardens, Manchester. Mr. Abel Buckley, chairman ef the board of directors, presided. The meeting adopted the report of the directors, which stated that the net profit for the six months amounted to £134,249 ss. lid., and recommended that it be dis- posed of as follows :—Dividend of 15s. a share and bonus of ss. a share, £lOO,OOO ; added to reserve fund,.£ls,ooo;carried to next acc0unt,.£19,249 51.11 d. The retiring directors were re-elected, namely, Messrs. Richard Smith, R. Fryer Smith, William Tipping, and George Gordon Warren.</p>
        <p>TRH PASSION PLAT.—LEcrcraz AT ErrNeoax.</p>
        <p>The first of a series if lectures Whict-iis-tO-be held in connection with St. Edward'. Catholic Church,Runcorns was given OR Monday evening in the school, Mill Brow. The lecture was entitled " Impressions of Oher-tunsei - gau and its passion play in 1890." The lecturer was the Rev. W. Dallow who has delivered many lectures on this interesting subject in almost every large town in the British Isles. The room was crowded to exces.. The manipulation of the lantern was in the hands of Mr. T. Bowyer. Before concluding his extremely interesting lecture, Father Dallow said that a splendid new organ had been recently presented to the church of S.S. Peter and Paul. Ober-Ammergau, by Mr. Evans of the firm of Messrs. Evans and Co., coal proprietors, Havdock, who collected tor it from the English people. The total cola was £650, and it was opened by a grand service in august 1594. Recently Mr, Evans died in the Bavarian village and his funeral was attended by all the linhabit. ants of the village.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
