        <p>• BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES. A WARRINGTON TOWN COUNCILLOR PROSECUTID BY THE R.S.P.C.A.-TAN CAS DISMISSED.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning at the Warrington Borough ?slice Court, John Sumner, carter, was charged with cruelty to a horse, by working it in an unfit state ; and Mr. James Grace, a Councillor for the Borough, was charged with causing it to be worked.—Mr. A. Browne defended and denied the offence.—lnspector Greatroz. R.S.P.C.A., stated that about 11 a.m., on January 10th he was on duty in Winwick-road, wher he saw Sumner in charge of a horse and cart. Witness saw that it was weak, examined it, and found it to be an aged brown mare in a weak, emaciated condition. Both fetlocks be- hind were overshot from weakness. Under the collar he found four old wounds, all inflamed and painful. Sum- ner said he knew the horse was unfit for work and that Mr. Grace had told him to work it. A month ago he bad told his master it was unfit for work. The Inspector added that at 1 o'clock the same day he met Mr. James Grace who said he knew it was in a bad state and that Sumner had told him a month ago. He said he did not know about the sores. The horse was sent to the knacker's and shot.—Detective Inspector Monaghan de- posed to seeing the horse,in company with the Inspector and Detective Barlow. There were three old wounds on the shoulders and one appeared raw. Sumner said Mr. Grace bad told him to work it.ln his opinion there would only be pain from one wound.—Deteetive Barlow cur- reborated.—Mr. Browne said this showed the value of official evidence. The Inspector swore that all four wounds were bad, and the police told a different tale. On the day in question Mr. James Grace never saw the animal or ordered it out to work. It was Mr. Thomas Grace, his client's uncle, who suggested to Sumner that be should take the horse out with some light tip. That was its only load. Mr. Grace destroyed the horse because he understood the Lospeetor to Bay if he did so be would hear nothing more about it.—Mr. Thomas Grass said the mare had set worked the whole of December, and on the day in question Mr. James Grace had gone to Liver- pool and did not know the horse was being worked. Witness sent it out and had never seen the sores.—Mr. Jannis Grace corroborated. He admitted that Sumner told him a month ago that the horse was unfit for work,and it had been resting the whole of December.— Sumner said the wounds were healed up before the horse went out on the day in question, and the top of one was knocked off in the journey. It was a horse that never put en flesh.—The magistrates dismissed both cases.</p>
