        <p>Farna.Y.—tlef ore the Mayor (chairman). Councillor Roberts, Councillor Smethurst. Mr. J. Hepherd UNJUST SOALMS.—John Hughes. 37, Howard-street, was *Merged with having in his possession on the 6th January for use a pair of scales which were unjust and also un- stamped. --Mr. Place prosecuted and said that his assistants visited the defendant's shop on January 6th and found his scales unjust and unstamped. They were condemned on June 14th and he had ne-er had them repaired or done away with. The scales were against Mlle.:etc.—Mr. W. H. Wallington, assistant Inspector. coreoborated.—Mr. Place asked for a nominal fine and confiscation of the scales.—A penalty of 51. including costs was imposed, and the scales to be forfeited. A similar offence was charged against John Patrick Grady, 255, Liverpool-road. On DePeraber 16th.— Inspector Plase said the scales were seized on December 16th, and had been condemned on June 11th. They were ntsjJet against the seller as in the previous case —A similar penalty was Imposed. WOIIKING A LAMS HORSE.—lifehael Doe!. 24,Silver street. and John Gregg. 35, Winwick-road. were charged, the former with working a horse in an unfit state on Janoary Bth, and the latter with causing it to be worked. Gregg did not appear—lnspector Gresatrex said that at 11 o'clock on Janu- ary Bth he was in Queen-street. and saw Doyle driving a brown horse attached to a spring cart. He examined it and found It aged, and in poor condition, and taffering from lameness of the off hind leg due to a decesred joint. Both defendants admitted knowing it was lame to witness, and Gregg maid he sent it out.— Mrs. Gregg said the horse had beenshot since.—A fine of ss. and costs was imposed on each defendant. A WOMAN'S QUARREL.—CesiIia Joseph no Rtiane.l6,King- street, was charged with assaulting Frederica Nixdorf. 6, Bing-street, on January Bth. There was a cross-summons ' against Mrs Nixdorf for assaulting Mrs. Ruene.—Mr. Davies appeared for Mrs. Nixdorf and said that at 10-33 on tne night In question complainant was coming home from the market when defendant ran at her, and with- out provocation struck her twice on the head, knocking her to the ground, and also tearing her dress and a costiy cape. Mr. Davies said that it was a eve of mistaken identity as defendant had quarrelled with some other women in the Market, and perhape mistook complainant for One of them. Complainant had never given her any proyoution.—Mrs. Nixdorf corroborated. —Mr. Davies predueed a letter which defendant had sent to the Chief Constable defaming com- plainant's character and which the htter Indignantly denied.—Mr. C. H. Unsworth appeared for nes. Ruane and agreed to pay the costs of the summer's. and the advocates' ee together with an apology. The statements in the letter were also withdrawn. MONDAY.—Before Mr. F. Monks (chairman). Alderman Francon:lb. Mr. T. EirChall, Mr. J. W. Hayward, Hr, S Wright. and Councillor J. Shaw Green Lincoln/cr.—Julia Ann Shields. Dial street, a wonan oil bad diameter, pleaded guilty to a charge of indecency In Wharf-street, at 11,10 on Saturday niyht. —P.C. Corbett proved the case. the Chief Constable rnenti nine that it was her 40th appearance —She was committed for a month. Dacrxxxamme.—Joseph GUI for being dr ink and disorderly In Arpley-road et 1.45 on Sunday morning was d'scharged with a caution.—William Tomlinson. drunk and disorderly In Sedge-street at 10.40 on Sunday evening.--Defendant said Ise had no money and he did net know it was any good send ing a poor fellow to gaol.—He was discharged era nromising to leave the town; James 'Nam, oh.wo, drunkenness in Legh-etreet, on January 9th. fined 55. %MI eilitSs BEOGING.—Two people of no abode. John Mitchell and Catherine Mitchell pleaded guilty to a charge ef begging in Priestley-street at 3.30 on Sunday internam' --Detective Mescock deposed to watching the de'endanta bpeezing from door to door. They were each in peseessim of bread a•yd meat, and coppers.—Defendants said they had walked from Widnes and were forced to beg for somethlog.—The magis- trates sent them to gaol for? days. DANOEROus DISIvING.—James Rylev, 40, Howley-lane, was eharged with driving a horse and lorry to the danger of the public on January 10th.—P.C. Baxendale said he saw de- fendant at 6.33 on the eveningln qaestion driving a horse and empty lurry down Buttermarket-street at ten miles an hone in his judgment. Witness shouted and defendant took no notice but went rapidly down learn-street. and Church- street, There were a lot of people about and witness thought the horse's speed was dangerous.—Thomas Wally, carter, said be saw defendant and he was driving at five miles an hour. The noise of defendant's tarry startled witness's horse.--A fine Of 29. 63. was imposed.</p>
        <p>TIIESDAY.—Before Mr. T. Birchall (In the chair), Conantile J. Shaw Green, and Mr. R. Garnett. Tan Ilismotor or A Rl:M.—William Barker, agerll3. Thal- wall-road. Latchford, was charged with stealing from the dwelling-house of Joseph Cook, 78 Mersey-street, on Satur- day. January 15th, a ladies' gold ring value Is—Re pleaded 111t1.—Th,. Chief Constable (Mr. L. Talbot) said that Mr. Cook took the hoy into his ensp'oyainnt as errand boy. At 9 o'clock oe the morning of January sth Mr. Conk saw his •vito's ring hAnging on n hook to th•• kitr:hen, noel it was out missed until Sunday afternooti A, the hoy did not tarn no to work on Monday 11, i.rn ing suspicion rota:lllrd</p>
