        <p>to him and the police were !Wormed. Detective Meaeock made enquiries which led hint to arrest the boy In Mersey-street and take him to the Police Station. He then called at Mr. Cook's shop where he found Barber's mother, who was In conversation with Mr. Cook. He asked for the ring and she took off her glove and removed the ring from her finger.—Mr. Browne appeared for the boy and said that he had given the ring to his aunt telling her be had found it. Then Mrs. Barber heard of it and got it back from the aunt, and went to Mr. Cook's to inquire about it. having heard a rumour that he had lost one —Mr. Cook cor- roborated the Chief Constable's statement, but added that Mrs. Barber never offered the ring until Meaeock asked for it. —The boy was ordered to receive 9 strokes of the birch, and the Chairman remarked that they thought the mother hal acted Injudiciously. B•KTH-DWELLERS.—TWI disrepu'able-looking young men answering to the names of Joseph Boyde and Matthias rye, were charged with sleeping out at one o'clock that morning without visible means of support.—Detective Barlow said that in comeanjt iiith Detective Roper he visited the neigh- benrhood behind Aiken-street on Monday night. They found the two prisoners sleeping in a hole in the ground with a small lire near them. Witness believed they had beea sleep- ng in the same hole for six months, off and on. A number f suppers, Ao.. hat recently been missed from Whitecross Works.—The Chief Constable said ens of the defendants ad- mitted having done 113 regular work for five years.—They were committed to gaol tot a month with hard labour. W ILDNESDLY. —Before Mr. John Fairclouqh (in the chnr),lslr. John Geddes, and Mr. T,Birchall.</p>
