        <p>PARR'S BANE, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The report of the directors of Parr's Bank, Limited, for the past year states that the position and prospects of the business continue to be in every way satisfactory. Including £66,365 brought forward at 30th June, 1897, the net balance of pro- fits, after providing for all bad and doubtful debts and rebating the current bills at 5 per cent, is £04,081. From this sum the directors recommend the payment of a dividend at the rate of 19 per cent. per annum,free of income tax, for the quarter ended 31st December, 1897, to be payable on the Ist Feb- ruary, £62,700 ; dividend at the same rate for the quarter ending 31st March, 1898, to be payable on 2nd May, 1898, to the shareholders whose names shall be on the register on 14th April, £2,700 £124,400: to bank premises account, £20,000- 1115,400 ; leaving balance to be carried to next ac- count, £58,681. The directors much regret that owing to ill-health their colleague, Mr. James A. Bannerman, has been obliged to resign his seat at the Board. The directors who retire at this time by rotation are the Right Hon. Lord Playfair, G.C.8., Mr. H, B. Muir, Mr. H. C. Ross, Sir Thomas G. Frost, Mr. Peter Stubs, Sir David Gamble, Bart., C. 8., Mr. John Dun, and Mr. E. W. Nix, all of whom are eligible, and are recommended for re-elected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BALA.NCE OF ARGUMENT.</head>
        <p>THE BALA.NCE OF ARGUMENT.</p>
        <p>There are people who say it is upon the whole a good thing for the world that there is plenty of sorrow and suffering in it. How else,they say, could kindness, pa. tience, pity,and the spirit of helpfulness be manifested? If property were equally divided, as some theorists want it to be,and no one consequently felt the sting of pi). verty,how could there be such things as the sentiment and the act of charity ? Should we not all be at once cold and selfish ? And if there were no sickness whet e we,uld be the tender ministrations which it elicite, the noble and costly public institutions which grow out of it, and that cloeenees of union between human hearts which results from the exercise of sympathy and the work of rescue.</p>
        <p>In short, is it not true that the conditons and experi- ences among 119 which seem most unfortunate and r grettable are in some strange way the soil wherefrom springs up all that is best in human character ? I con. fees myself unable to understand the nature of the orthodox " Heaven," for this reason. It used to bother me when I was a toy and went to church and heard sermons; and it bothers me now that I have grown older and become tired of hearing sermons. Just think of a place where everybody is perfectly happy, " where the inhabitant never saith I am sick," and where there is nothing to be done to put the people ia any better form and fettle than they are in all the blessed while ! The entire civilised human race is working hard ts get rid of its disabilities and its discomforts—whether with really all-round success or not is mighty hard to say. We abolish some of our old troubles, and out of that very fact a tough and hardy lot of new ones spring up. It may, however, be said that VIA treat disease better than we used to. There is less suffering, and the average length of human life increases. When for ex- ample, we can take that hydra-headed old ailment, inr digestion or dyspepsia, and relieve it or cure it altoeethe' we have won a distinct victory over perhaps the worst enemy that makes our flesh a prison-house of torment We have mule life sweeter, happier, and more efficient without in any dangerous degree injuring the chances of doing good in other directions. It is something to be sure, when a man can stand up and te:l a truthful story like this :</p>
        <p>" For many years I suffered from indigestion and ' conitipation. I felt weak, weary, and heavy. I had a bad taste in the mouth, and was constantly spitting up a sour uid. After meals I had pains about the chest, and could only take the plainest of food, and this did not agree with mo. Sometimes better and then worse, but never properly well,l kept on like this for yearsoisid although able to get about,l was in a great deal of pain. I saw a doctor and took medicines, but only got ease tor a short time, and then was bad o 3 ever. In Febru- ary, 1895, I read in a small book about Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and got a bottle of this medicine from Mr. Ferris. After taking it a few days I got much bene- Rt. I could eat well and enjoy my food, which agreed with me. I continued its use and soon got strong and ' well, being free f rem all pain. By taking a dose now and then I keep well. You can publish this statement as you like. (Signed) John McKee, 78, Thorndyke Street, Belfast, August 24th, 1897." Yi ithout worrying ourselves over the insoluble problems associated with our lives In th's world we can settle down on one thinv, and stick to it—f r it'd so. I mean that nobody will submit to poverty or to pain save when he cannot help it. flit were a Tieition with so whether to undergo the torture of the damned from chronic d7sPI.P.SIa for the sake of j istifying the exist- ence of doctors and hospitals, or to get rid of it by an obvious means without applying to either of them, I Should take the latter course. •</p>
        <p>_ Let us abelish all the suffering we can, even at the risk of putting mankind on th,Lt particalsr dead level Of happiness which cornea of good health. And, for its 8/se:titled uses,and within its limitatione,this is the office and mission of the now famous rnodicine called Mother Seigel's Syrup. For the plat twenty rears or Co its pro- gress through the nations has been followed by the bene- dictions of those who were ready to perish. It has con- ferred renewed health, with the potencies and blessings attached thereto, upon uncounted numbers that had vainly sougot relief from other agen.ties. Mon have earned more money, and women sung more songs, out of what this remedy ha.; done for them, than from the effects of any brand of charity ar philanthropy whatso- ever. And that is about the point I wanted tai make.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>?IfHB appearance of that unique little " Diary and Corn panion," issued by GRO. G. HUSSEY &amp; CO., for the use of cricketers (which may be obtained from Sports Desiers Booksellers, or Railway Bookstalls for 63.). reminds us of the owning season, In anticipation of which Geo, a- Bussey Co.. have prepared a " record " stock of Bats. We are told that the timber they hold for Bat making alone exceeds 1,000 tons. . . . . . _ . .</p>
        <p>-GloriCos Cricket follows the Brltlih Flag everywhere." (1)</p>
        <p>V Secretarial' of Football Clubs are requested to note tha reports of matehee played on Saturday must be eent to th Head Office. Bewsey-street, Warrington net later than 1 o'clockon Thursday morning or they cannot be inserted.</p>
        <p>SALFORD DEFEAT RUNCORN k SECOND TIME. Runcorn journeyed to Salford on Saturday with pleasant anticipations that the result of the day's en- counter would be anything but favourable to Salford. The committee had ordered a change in the team and placed Jolley at fullback and Houghton was made a threequarter for the day. Salford had a strong team, which included R. Thomma Welshmsn, who has played for Castleford, West Hartlepool, and the Durham County Club. Tunney kicked off for Salford. and Jolley made a good return to the centre of the fi Id. After scrim- maging and an exchange of kicke between Smith and Jolley the play was taken to the hams quarter flag, where Griffiths was penalised, but Eloughton's place kick at goal just went wide, a dead ball reaulting. The drop-out kick was well returned, and J.Faulkeer ran to the Salford line, where he passed to Lightfoot who lost the ball over the line, and T. Williams kicked it dead. le Upon resuming at thequarter flag the ball was rushed to midfield, where Woodhead received a ad in- jury to his leg. He was compelled to retire,and took no further part in the game. Fla for la considerable time was confined tn the Salford q carter, hot the home players slefended well. Thocsas at length made a gocd run, but he was collared on the Runcorn goal line, where several packs were fought out. The ball wee carried back to the Salford end of the field, and Srnith was compelled to touch down. Two other dead balls fell to Runcorn and a touchdown, the reeult of a place kick at goal taken by Houghton. The visitors continued to have the best of the play, until J.Fatilkner passed the ball wildly to Lightfoot, Find T. Williams, securing psi. session, scored a try for Salford after a grand race with Houghton.Griffiths placing a geed. This was quickly fol- lowed by Walker picking up the ball, after s mull by the Salford centre, and scoring a try for Runcorn. and Houghton placed a goal from an acute ;wale. }Nit- time score :—Salford, one goal and one try (five points); Runcorn, one goal and one try (five points.) Farmer re- started the game, and the visitors quickly became the aggressors, but Pearson with a splendid run carried play to the other end of the field, where Runcorn were penal- ised, and Griffiths placed a goal for Salford. After play had for a time been confined to riddfield Grey, (rem a neck, passed to T. Williams, who threw the ball to Hos- kins, and that player ran over at the corner and scored a try for Salford, which was not improved Isy Griffiths. This was followed by the vidtorte forward. rushing and passing the ball, hut T. Williams Ravel. Griffiths broke away after a scrum:nage, and smart passing between T. Wiiliams and Hoekins ended in the lasl.-nangBd Player scoring another try for Salford and Griffi.lis placing a goal. Then the visit re had a turn, and Hall weed. Walker, Faulkner, and Butterworth were pre• rninent• Thomas, however, raised the teige with a good kick and,following up, collarel Jars, with the ball in his posseieion. After scrummaging, Brosra,Tan• nev,and Griffiths made a good passing run, and T. Wil- liams securing the hall scored a splendid try behind the posts for Salford, Odffithe placing a goal. The visitors tried hard to scare,but they were unable to do so. Final score :--lalford. four goals and four tries (20 points) Runcorn,otie goal and one try (5 points). The following were the teams :—Stlford : D. Smith, full back ; J. Hoskins, T. Williams (caotain), R. Thomas and A. Pear- s-M. thrwe.roarter banks ;I. Grey and B. Grifii'he, half hacks; J. lihapps,J. William , W. Brown, R. Moe, H. Woodhesd,W. Llewellyn, P. ronney,and H. Davis', for, wards. Runcorn : J. Jolley,full hack ;A. E.Hailwood. C. S. Hetiehton.W. Butterworth (captain) and .1. Light- foot.three-qoarter hacks ; Faulkner arid W. Robinson, half haeks ; J. Walker.J. Farmer,W. Lightfoot,J. Lane- iey,T. Moores, R. E. Antrobue, S. Betley,and R. E. Dart lair, forwards.</p>
        <p>LEIGFI v. WIDNES..</p>
        <p>At I.idgh. In grans:is—sr:eh—sr," -b—e-f Or-e" 3,000 fip t at n r s. Both teams were stronzly represented. Coop started for Leigh, and *ter a lot of evelllnTe kickine between the respective backs play settled down in the netresl ground.</p>
        <p>, Then Molynenx charged down Rifpan's kick and Mlles- ters driblied past the visitors' backs, but Nevitt rtashed up and saved. Leigh had now the best of matters, but Batey kicked strongly, and following up tackled Coop, who thereby got his shoulder damaged and retired ,Leigh playing fourteen men. Widnes then attacked, but wer repelled by Molyneux and Dunbavin, jun., and by quick passing Leigh reached the Widnes line. M'Masters almost scored, Batey stopping him grandly. Though short- handed Leigh were playing up strongly, and eventually frera off-side play Widnes were penalized. and Laderton taking the bail at top aced seored vessullY, MelYneux f Ailing. Leigh continued to press severely, and Widnes had their hands full in keeping then' out. Batey even- tually relieved, but the Leigh forwards, who were play- ing brilliantly.dribbled back again.Olivar Dunbavin,who had taken his place at full back, was charged over, and Widnes seamed to have an opening, but Pearson rushed up and saved. After grand pasaing by all the Widnes banks Brennan was almost over, Pearson touching down. Leigh continued to play up finely,but eventually Widnes rushed the ball to the Leigh line. Molvneux cleared, and by splendid passing Leigh ran down the field, but Hodg- son Oventwilly missed the ball, Play became rough, the tackling being levers. Tnen, whilst part of the Leigh players were stopped attending to an injured comrade, Widnes rushed up and Hunt scored, Johnson improving. Widnes further improved their tonsition, the final result being—Widnes 2-2-0, Leigh 0.1-3. RII:(CORN EflitgErs v.Rtrwcomer Som. Woßis.—Playing against the wind, the final score ended as follows : Hornets 10 points, Works nil.</p>
        <p>" A " TIAN COIII2IFFITION. WIDMIS "A" •.Larox "A."—On Saturday these teams met on the ground of the former, bernro a fair attend- ance of spectators. Final score—Widnes "A," 21 points, Leigh "A" nil. RuNcoan A V. SALFORD A.—Grand weather prevailed on Saturday,when the above encounter was decided on the Irwell-lane enclosure. The ground was in good con- dition, it having Nen relied previous tn the match. There was a large gate when the teams entered the field. ' Salford team included Miles an old county man, Doran, :Waiter, and Pearson, once shining lights in the first ' team. The visitors opened play, Woods returning well. Harter caught and sent the leather to centre. several scrimmages were ordered and Wilkinson kicked into Salford's 25, Miles being tackled by Hough before he could return. Doran cleared from a scrimmage and Hignett and Smith made good headway. Butterwnrth and Wilkinson were safe and carried operations hack to the visitors' quarters Saflon handed to Williams whe in turn passel to G. Wools who dashed brilliantly for the lino, liii ririwross was stayed by Pearson who grounded him juat Slq he was ahout to score. The game was very fast, cad the tackling of the hacks being equally son. Eventually Dignett broke avv•V and trenefarr"t to Smith who had loft to him a clear field and it was thniight that be woilld certainly score, but Gough who was the right wind threequarter 14W across the field and smartly tackled Smith seven yards from the line. Par this grand piece of work he was warmly applauded. Play from now up to half-time was very even, nothing being scorial when the whistle sounded. After the change of ends Salford began in earnest and made every effirt scor . and consequently the gs me hoo.amo very fast. Wilkinson wee very con- spicious with his aprints. Doran got possession from a scrimmwe and Reuthtim and Miles travelled within a yard or two of the home goal. The Greens thon made a siege on the quarters of S ilfnrd and sucrievided in main- taining this pontion for a short time. Williams slipped nicely away hi q Peas was mulled and a certain try was lost. A thlford forward vras vemsynd from the scene for foully charging Langley. The game ended with a clean shoet,no score having hson made. Runcorn had the host of matters throughout and it is the boat A team matoh that has bsen held on the ground during the whole of the present season.</p>
        <p>WARIITNOTON V OLDHAM (.. A " Taaars).—Yhis 'own match was Wird at Oldham, aad resulted in the War- rine,nis ns eanfirmin: th• I,•ilt of their firet mestheq, the Vigi•,,,q winninff hr t I (three points) to nil. The Few hv no rn pace re so presents the neriority of the War- rinctoaiane,for dorin,z the firt half they were attackine: almn4l-, r.nron7hour, awl only the able defenze eet up by the lona's prevented a larze score. Carey was almost a 3rorrr twine in q ic6l,lc!,llion doriny, the early por- tion of the Vervid half, hot Scott did the nredfol, aftor a CApital run. Jones f Ailed to convert, so that Warrinv too won by a try to ail.</p>
        <p>THE CITIES rION OF PROFESSIONALBM. MEETING AT HUDDERSFIELD. The vlio.arnert meeting of the committee of the N,rthern Uniem to cons;der tha question of profession- alism ,eas held at Huddersfield on Tocaday night. At its Conclirlion the representatives of the Press were re- fused any information. It is understood, however, that tn.inhers separated withou coming! to any decision, and it is regarded as orohthie that the matter is left over for diecoasion by the rnipnr!tiVilcountr committees. As far aq can he gathere4, the Lancashire Northern Union clubs are for the most part in favour of the adoption of pr fessiooallam, whilst the Yorkshire clubs are loth to talm the step. Immo being i fsvnur of a re- turn to pure amateurism.</p>
