      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Private Alfred Shaw,</head>
        <p>Private Alfred Shaw,</p>
        <p>110 W HE WAS DISCHARGED FROM THE AKMY, IN-</p>
        <p>CURABLE, AND MADE WELL AND STRONG BY</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>DR. WILLIAMS' PINK PILLS.</head>
        <p>DR. WILLIAMS' PINK PILLS.</p>
        <p>Lord Wolseley's laments on the scarcity of Army Re- cruits have aroused widespread attention which is not to be wondered at, having in view the great National Interests at stake ; but what has escaped comment is the serious leakage annually recorded from the ranks of men, as the official phrase goes</p>
        <p>"MEDICALLY UNFIT EOM FIIITIEIt Sxanci." A reporter of the "South London Press "recently paid a visit to 59, Paradise-street, Lambeth Walk, London,S.E. and sought out one of those interesting, not to say un- fortunate, young men—Alfred Shaw by name—who, having chosen a military career, experienced the re- agorae of being compelled to abandon it from causes over which he had no control. Mr. Shaw stands five feet nine inches in big bo)ts, end is in appearance a fine specimen of the hardy, robust young Britisher. "You invalided from the Army !" exclaimed the in- terviewer, in astonishment. "By your appearance,yon seem as fit to take a hand in the Soudan or any other campaign as any man who donned a red coat." " So I am—now," replied youag Shaw, larcenically. "But it's only during the put two months that I have</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>BECOME MYSELF AGAIN."</head>
        <p>BECOME MYSELF AGAIN."</p>
        <p>'And then he told the story of how Her Majesty lost a young and promising soldier. He said.—"On Decem- ber 9, 1895, at the age of 18, I enlisted in the Norfolk Regiment, passed the medical examinations with flying colours, and was sent to the regimental depot at Nor- wich.' was settling down quite comfortably, when I was overtaken by illness, and on January 8, 1898—barely a month after joining—was sent into the regimental hos- pital. I</p>
        <p>HAD CAUGHT A CHILL</p>
        <p>a week before. All of us recruits had been sent out on a route march of some 15 or Itt miles, with full kit. I had sweated pretty freely,and after we got back to bar- racks I felt a bit stiffish, which gradually drew worse and ROWE In the hospital they said it was RESIIIIATISIL AND HEART DISEASE.</p>
        <p>"I remained in hospital until April 3 without im- provement. I went home until May 2,but was no bet- ter. Into the hospital I was sent again, and here I re- mained without mending until June 1, when I was sent before the Medical Board at Colchester,and my discharge wee decided upon.</p>
        <p>" Things began to look blacker still. r got worse in- stead of batter after my discharge. My mother was worried a great deal over my health, but luckily in July she read something in the newspapers about Dr.Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and the result was that she bought a box. After taking that I felt ever so much better. In the course of the following month I took several more boxes, and wonderful to say, I felt as strong and as robust as I ever did in my life. "You say I look a fine, strong chap," Mr. Shill? re- marked In conclusion. "So I am—now, but it is thanks to Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and you are quite at Warty to print my experiences for the sake of others."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PUBLIC DEBATE IN PARR</head>
        <p>PUBLIC DEBATE IN PARR</p>
        <p>HALL.</p>
        <p>"CHRISTIANITY OR SPIRITUALISM-WHICH IS THE BETTER SYSTEM FOR MANKIND ?"</p>
        <p>On Monday and Tuesday evenings large audi- ences assembled in Parr Mall to listen to a discus- sion on " Christianity or Spiritualism—which is the better system for mankind." The debaters were Mr.:G. 11. Bibbings 8.A., of Norreanton (trance and inspirational speaker), and Mr. A. .I'. Waldron, of London, (Christian Evidence Society Lecturer). The proceedings on Monday evening were presided over by the Rev. C. Finch, and he was supported on the platform by the two lecturers named, and also by the Rev. S. J. Fleming, Mr. S. S. Chiswell, of Liverpool, Mr. William E. Stout, and others. The rules of the debate were that each speaker should occupy half-an-hour, and then each be allowed to speak again twice, a quarter of an boar each time. In opening the proceedings the Chairman said he hoped his duties would be extremely light, but that would depend largely, if net absolutely, upon the audience. Re would ask all present to listen qaietly and attentively to the speakers, and to re- frain from interruption. Let them weigh the argu- ments the speakers would adduce, and "having proved all things hold fast that which is good." (Hear, hear, and applause.) Mr. BIBRINIS en rising to make his firsh speech was cordially reeeived. He said he was the representative of a somewhat unpopular cause, arid vet he had no fear, and did not want any. He held to his own opinions; he had the courage of his convintiens, end h- was prepared to defend them to the best of his ability. If he faileri lie should do so knowine that he had done his heat. He knew he had an honourable opponent. He had Mr. Waldron's record before him, and in other 'ebates with Important people he had been termed " a gentleman." (applause.) And he believed be had every right to that title. He should net spare Mr. Waldron, and he did not ask Mr. WAldron to spars him, but what they had to say ceuld be said without brutality. (Applause.) If Modern Spiritualism was false, impure, immoral, and irrational, the people of Warrington would want very little of It. He held, however, that if it werrethe oppo- site, thee, in common with milliens of people throughout the world, would want plenty of it. fl did not forget the terms that had been hurled about ; the names that had been called, bat calling ether peoole names would not interfere with farts. (Hear, hear). Pact e were too stubborn We laid it down, to commence with, that the fact that Spiritualism from the standpoint of organisation was in a minority did not tell again3t the, system he humbly championed. All truth had had to pass through the baptism of persecution to enter this world at all, and If Modern Spiritualism was true—and he held it was—it was passing through some of the stages of opposition in cemmou with other truths that had corne as unwelcome guests into the world. Christianity was popular to-day, and it was thoueht to be very nice to be on the orthodox side. It required no special merit to stand on the popu- lar side,where the nos populi became almat like the vor dei. The test of merit was when men were prepared to stand in the right with two or three. He was open to believe that even his opponent would not take exception to the statement that the most fiendish crimes that had stained the history of the world, the thodsand, al- meet million devildoino of the Inquisition, had all been invented in the name of Christianity. The story VIA 9 this—that although Spiritualism was unpepelar,its unpopularity dare not belittle it in the eyes si those who baying eyes to see saw and having ears to hear heard. What were the claims of Christiaeity ? and what were the claims of Spiritualism? As a matter I of fact their little systems had their day, and Chris- I , tianity had dons 'much—they would give to it its full 1 mead of praise. They were blond to admit the sincerity of these men and women who were virtunos,true,noble, and charitable ; but they did not fail to observe that the main bulk of the progress the world had known, the greatest of her benefactors, the most marvellens of her achievemente had been won not by orthodox thinkers but by hetercdox heretics. A number of names were mentioned in support of the contention, and the speaker remarked that the 19th century was the cen- tury of revolutinn—blondless,fortunately. Materialistic science had been at work, and the faLles of the past, where they had been fableselad been well-nigh exploded and all over the world that day there swept the great tidal wave nf ratlD:laligtir, thought, and the system known as theological Christianity was unable to provide the spiritual food that the world required. Man was</p>
        <p>pre-eminently and primarily a spiritual being. He had his animal nature and his moral nature ; and he had what was infinitely more value to him—his spiritual na- ture ; and because of that spiritual nature muet find certain appetites, certain needs, certain wants, and the question was "What can supply those wants ? " Did Christianity supply those wants to-day to the full ? Materialism abounded on all hands. It was admitted in the Church, and the Church had had to be changed and to take into its services methods that 25 years ago would have been regarded as the special work of the devil. (Hear, hear, and applause.) That was a rational protest against the theology that existed, and modern spiritualism, more than any other system, , whilst it would not belittle the work done by other auxiliaries, helped to supply the great need. Spiritualism was superior to Christianity, that was to Christian theology ; and modern Spiritualism was superior to theological Christanity in that it started right with man and gave him some possessions worth possessing. From the standpoint of theology man was purely corrupt—if he might use the word purely in eon- neotion with the corrupt—and whilst he knew that many of the forward men in the Church had get away from the libel upon the character of the Infinite Good- ness, and whilst those who clung to it were afraid to say so, there never had up to now been given a virtual denial to it, and they as Spiritualists would labour on till the Church said " We have made a mistake and we own up to it." (Applause.) Spiritualism said that every man had some goodness in him, and that he started with goodness in him. Spiritualism said that man never had fallen, and that ever since order and humanity had been in the world man had been in the ascending scale of progress. Spiritualism was superior to Christian theology inasmuch as it never made belief a necessary of salvation. The superiority of Spiritualism was shown in the fact that they did not make salvation depend upon any one book or upon any system of belief. There was a wide point of differ( nee between the two. (Inplauee. Mr. WarmitoN met with a hearty reception on rising to reply. He said he could re-echo the sentiments uttered by Mr. Bibbings at the commencement of his speech. Mr. Bibbings and he went to the same school, played in the same playground together, and he was glad of the privilege of entering into a debate where all personal enmity was buried—(hear, hear) and where principles could be discussed without personalities. (Applause.) With reference to Mr. Bibbings' remark that Spiritualism taught purity, charity, and justice, he quoted a state- ment of Mr. Harris, an ex-medium, who stated that Spiritualism sought professedly to pull down and destroy all existing institutions ; that already hun- dreds of homes once happy had been turned into earthly hells, filled with untold horrors, fathers and husbands wandering in the mists of Spiritua- lism in search of some Jezebel who calleth herself prophetess, and whose wives and children were left to poverty, shame, and disgrace. (Applanse.) He also wished to direct Mr. Bihbings' attention to the fact that in America there were hundreds of Spiritualists who denied absolutely the existence of God. He be- lieved with the Rev. Mr. Haweis that Spiritualism would fit in with all the teachings of the thole, for the Bible was such a grand book, standing by itself in the litera- ture of the world, that there was not one crime in the whole calendar of human sins which was net condemned by that book. (Applause.) What good deed was there within the imagination of the best men and women who lived to-day which was forbidden by the teaching of the bible? (Applause.) Mr. Bib binge had quoted the text, " The wicked man turneth away from his wickedness," and tried to show that ethics were grounded apart from belief. Why was it a man turned away from his wickedness ? Because he believed that wickedness did not pay (Ap- plause). Carlyle had said that "there can be no work whatever to benefit humanity which is not grounded in faith." Why did Mr. Bibbings and other men go about preaching Spiritualism against rationalism, because everyone needed salvation, and if Spiritualism had any right of being propagated it had only a right on the line that it was better than any previous system, and that if they accepted it it would save them. (Applause.) They. had to believe that to be saved, so that Spiritualism de- pended for salvation on belief. (Loud applause.) Mr. Bibbiugs had said that salvation did not depend upon any one book or belief. Bet it did. It did not matter what they believed if they were against the truth. The truth was the only thing worth fighting for. (Applause.) The questions to be answered were what was truth, what harmonized with the primal facts of human consciousness, and what was best adapted for the needs and natures of mankind. Mr. Bibbings had said that the story of modern Spiritual ism was against the story of the fall, but he entitled to know whether that was only in certain circles in Eng- land'. The weakness of modern Spiritualism was that its doctrines in England were not universal. In Roman Catholic countries Spiritualism taught the doctrine of the fall. Mr, Fitzeeratd, the editor of the"Elwrician,' who was a sincere Spiritualist, told him personally the other day, in the company of medical men and several literary men of London, that Spiritualism taught all the creeds. Then, if so, how did he know which was cor- rect ? If some spiritualists by their communications be- lieved in the fall, what right could Mr. Bibbings have to say that his version war correct and all °there wrong? He (Mr. Waldron) accepted the doctrine of the fall, but not the findings of men and whet were called books of theology. The same thing might be described differ- ently by several persons,but it was there all the SAW. It needed no theological doctrine to tell them that men had fallen. Ask any mother,and she would tell them that almost in the youngest child she could see the signs of depravity. The doctrine of heredity was accepted by every biologist in the world. If Mr. Ribbings did not deny it then we inherited inclination teein.and that doc- trine was the basis of the dogma of the fall. But did Christianity teach that there was not one grain of good- nese in humanity ? IT so,why was it Christ said "Suf- fer the little chileren to come unto Me, and forbid them not, for of such is the kingdom of heaven." (Loud and continued applause.) Spiritual- ism, Mr. Bibbings said, recognized man's needs, and claimed manes a spiritual being.So did Christianity. He did not know one school of rationalism that accepted the interpretation given to the phenomena of Spiritual- ism as Mr. Bibbings interpreted it. The rationalism of Germany, Femme, England, and America was dead against Spiritualism. (Applause.y Referring to the re- mark that the greatest men of history had been those who had not been orthodox, M. Waldron contended that those who were styled heterodox, had always been In the minority. Jesus Christ started as one man against the world, and the greatest power in the world to day was the religion which sprang from that small band of Christian apostles. In answering the question submitted by Mr. Bibbings as to whether he aocepted the dogma of eternal punishment, he wished to ask Mr. Ribbings whether he denied that character tended to eternal per- ireatience,and did he believe that they would change their personality after the, change called death ? If so they would retain their memory, and would they not be sorry in eternity for the mis-spent opportunities and wasted faculties in the present life ? (Applause.) In answer to Mr. Bibbiag's question, "is it not a fact that materialism is en the increase ? If so in what way does the Church propose dealing there- with ?" he said she proposed by philosophical inquiry to raise a stria:dui...et truth which should laugh at criticism. He would like to ask whether the Spiri- tualista allowed scientific inquiry into their phenomena. When the " electric: girl " was brought before ecientific men in France she broke down before the tests of the scientific senate. Therm was not a medical man in War- rington who believed is the interpretatioa given by his friend of the phenomena of Spiritualism. Spiritualists did not give the same opportunities for the investigation of three phenomena that any chemist in the world would do on the subject of chernietry. With regard to the next question—" What has oceurred in the world's ex- perience to Metify silence tram beyond ?" he answered " Nothing." Christians did not believe that there was silence from beyond. They believed that the best man who ever lived on the page of history came back—Jesus Christ. (Applause.) In conclusion,he asked what higher spiritual doctriao was there to teach than that man was a supremely spiritual being and keeping to the histori- cal meaning of the word " religion," as lately sunported by Grant Allen's new book " The Evolution of the Idea of God," that religion was the union between the soli of MAO and the infinite spirit of the universe, what grander heights could Mr. Bibbings lift them to, what greater spirit of regeneration had he to offer them than this of God in the world, saving them ana uniting them to fiimeelf ? (Loud applause.) Mr. RIBBINGS said no importance could be attached tat the statements of Mr. Harris or Mr. Firzeerald. He rather objected to that sort of argument because as a Spiritualist he claimed he would not bring any argu- ment against Christianity because of its delinquents. If there were any delinquents in the cause of Spiritualism there were many in the cause of Chrietianity. Peebles, a leading Spiritualist of America, told them that he had a priuted list of 2 000 preachers and Sunday school .uperintendente, who in serving the world, flesh, and the Devil had been sent to penitentiaries.. Be should think himself a very poor exponent of Spiritul- ism if he were to hurl that at the moral law-abiding citizens; of a town as an argument in his favour. He asked for proofs that rationalism thoughnut the continent was against Spiritualism. As to Mr Waldron's state- ment about inherited depravity,could it be true that be. calve reason bad not struegled to the front fathers and mothers would sae that the.r little cradled darlings had within them the hopelessness of that miserable system</p>
        <p>of total depravity ? He urged that Spiritualists had in- vited scientific men to investigate the phenomena, and mentioned the names of Sir William Crook, Professor Barrett, and others. Some of the leading scientists of the day had tested it, coming to the subject in many cases opposed to it and with the idea that imposture was to be found. These had been obliged to confess that the evi- dence of Modern Spiritualism was correct, (Applause.) Mr. Wetorrox said scientists as a whole refused to ad- mit the interpretation given of the phenomena by Spiritualists, although there might be one or two excep- tions. Referring to the question of heredity, he remarked that emotion was not argument. That children were born with an inherited taste for intc sleeting liquor was a doctrine agreed upon by almost every medical author- ity. He urged that because a thing could flat be ex- plained was no reason why they should believe in Spiritualism. A large number of people were ruseeptible to hypnotic and magnetic influences. He should like Mr. Bibbings to show them that the spirit commit:tenni- cations were not enemies to facts, and he wanted facts weighed, examined, and testified to by . competent au- thorities on the open platform—not with the flighte turned out. (Applause.) Then he should want to know if the facts so brought to light were an advantage to humanity, and if the revelations were superior to the revelations of Christianity. (Applause.) He also wanted to know if the needs of 'mankind were better met in the doctrines of spiritualism than they were in Christiaeity. (Hear, hear.) Hew was it pos- sible far them to know the truth with reference to the spirit world when so many different statements had been made and views held by Spiritualists in different parts of the world ? (Applause.) Mr. BIBEINGS contended that Spiritualistic peenornetta werelwell attested facts. He preceeded to read from the book of Ezekiel as an evidence of spirit communion in the past. Tee names of scientific men which he had read out were testimony to the phenomena being facts, and be quoted a statement from the " Rock " and ens made by Canon Fawcett, of York referring to the increasing power of Spiritualism, and to the necessity of the arguments of Spirituallite being faced. He met the challenge to allow the phenoineua of Spiritualism to b) investigated openly by asking whether Christianity paraded its spirits in leant of a public audience. When Professor Tyndall offered a cheque of £l,OOO for an evidence of a direct an- swer to prayer, the Christian church said it was an in- stilt to the most sacred emotion. It was a matter affec- ting the spiritual coodition of man, and he agreed with the position taken up by the Christian church when challenged to prove it. He now claimed the same privi- lege for Spiritualism. (Hear, hear.) Modern theology had no message for them. When the crave robbed them of their dear ones they longed to know where they were. and modern Spiritualism gave that revelation which had corns down to them from all Gime. (App:ause.) Mr. WALDitote said his opponent's areurneet amounted to this, that if they could get tangible evidence and thoroughly believed in a future life—if they were thoroughly convinced of the existence of spirit forms— mankind would be the better for it. And yet the history of humanity was all against it. If they went further back into history they would find that the human family believed that the world was full of human spirits, and yet their condition was worse in very many respects to that of ours to-day, who were more enlightened on the subject. Personally he believed there was some truth in Spiritualism —(applause)—and further than that, there was some truth in every human system, for he was con- vinced that that which came from man could not be all bad but must contain some germs or truth., and some germs of goodness. But admitting that, Spiritualists gave us scant testimony of a future life. What had Spiritualism added to the knowledge of the world by a testimony, when the Church presented it in the fellow- ship of saints right back through the ages to the mighty Christ and the Cross? He believed in the existence of objective spirit forms because it was testified to by men's consciences. That was why we believed in Christianity. It was not a creed, nor a phenomena, but a life. (Applause.) The question he had asked of Mr. Bibbings. as to what Spiritualism had given us other than what we possesied in Christianity had not been answered. (Applause.) He refused to be governed by any man, be he Luther or Wesley. fie believed in Christ besause he believed Christ had taught the highest moral truth the world had ever listened to ; the most profound spiritual truths the world lead ever read ; and those men who had followed most closely to Christian principles,had bean the greatest benefactors to the race (applause.) With reference to the persecution by the Church, he admitted that had taken place,but he denied that there waa one bit of teaching in the New Testament Which bad supported that blot on the page of humanity. The quotation from Ezekiel which had been read, referred to a vision, and not to any spirit communion, fie would ask his hearers before accepting phenomena without adequate testimony to wait,measurep and investivate. Let nothing claim their attention simply because it vr.a supported by great Dames. Let them remember that the anly thing which wastlivine was truth, and the only thing worthy of their service.. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>A. THIN STREAK-ALONE DIVIDES DYSPEPSIA. AND CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>The Diet in Consunaption always On of the most important features.</p>
        <p>R. ANDREW WILSON.</p>
