      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>DEATIi OF AIR. V ILLIE RS.</head>
        <p>DEATIi OF AIR. V ILLIE RS.</p>
        <p>"THE FATHER OF FREE TRADE:' Mr. Charles Pelham Villiers who died on Sunday night had long survived all the men with whom he entered public life, He was the oldest member of the Privy Coun- cil, and had been far many years the oldest member of the Howe of Commons. He was older than Mr. Spencer Walpole and much older than Mr. Gladstone. At Cam- bridge he was temporary o ith Macaulay and Freed, and with that luminous comet Of a season, Charles Austin. His brother, Lord Clarendoe. died Foreign Stcretary,full of yeere and tionore, nearly 30 years ay. He himself was elected for Wolverhampton in 1835, and member of Wolverhampton he has ever sines remained. This very month he celebrated his ninety-sixth birthday In the en- joyment of fairly geed health and in the full possession of his mental faculties. He was in Parliament lone before Bright or Cobden, and he was the earliest Parliamentary champion of Free Trade. Charles Greville, writing on 7th January, 1844, says that "Charles Villiers . . . hae no love tor Cobden, who has taken the wind out of his sails arid got all the glory of a case of which Charles Villiers worked the beginning. ter. Villiers was a cynical as well as a briliiant talker. He eschewed sentiment and abhored gush. But he was a faithfully disinterestei servant of his country, and he cordially joined in no jealous spirit with all enemies of that odious system which John Bright celled " famine made by law." If there was any rivalry between him acd Cobden it was a struggle not for personal preced- ence or personal fame, but for priority in a righteous cause. The methods, like the characters, of the two men were totally different. Mr.Villiers was by tempera- ment as well as by birth an aristocrat. Cobden was in every fibre of his being a democrat. Cobden was a prac- tical man of business—though got, it is believed, a very successful one—who attacked the Corn Laws because they starved the people. Mr. Villiers was a highly trained and cultivated economist, thoroughly imbued with the doctrines of Adam Smith and Ricardo, who undemined and gradually,destroyed Protection by the weapons of lo !lc and reason, tie was a Free Trader in the Aria, rigid, mcientilic sense of the term, which has now its meet prominent representative in Lord Farrar,and which has lately been revived to justify the Government for deneuncine the commerciai treaties with France and Belgiuin. IT' have °aped( heard him say that he did not agree with Cobden in resting his case upon the assumption that foreign couLtries would follow the example of England. "1 always said," he added,in his quaint, dry manner, " if Free Trade is a good thieve for God's sake let's keep it to ourselves." I need hardly explain that this was an intentionally para- doxical way of puttirse it. What Mr. Villiers meant was that in the competition of neutral markets Free trade gave a diminct advantage to the country which adopted it. On the same occasion he made a remark which seemed to be full of interest and importance. Ile told us that Cobden negotiated the Commercial Treaty with France in 1860 on political and not on economic; grounds, not as a Free-trader, but as a lover of peace and a zealous promoter of friendly relations with the French Govern- ment. As Mr. Villiers was opposed to Home Rule, or at least to Mr. Gladstone'e Herne Rule Bills' Irish readers should know that he spoke with unusual warmth and earnestness of O'Connell's assistance in passing Free Trade. "It could not," he declared, " have been car- ried without him."</p>
        <p>Mr. Villier's conversational powers were of a very high order indeed. I have never, says Mr. Herbert Paul in the "Star," heard better talk than his, and I have never heard anything quite like it. Most great talkers, or, at all evente, most calkers who fel that they have a repu- tation to keep up, sound a trumpet before them. Mr. Villiers was singularly unemphatic. He would otter the most mordant epigrams in a low,quiet tone, as if he was making an observation on the weather. It may seem an odd comparison, but his colloquial style reminded one of Signora Dime's acting. There was absolutely no- thing in it to attract attention except its inimitable ex- cellence. When, at the beginning of "La Cavalleria Rusticana," an insignificant little woman slinks on to the stage, one can hardly notice that one is face to face with the greatest actress in Europe. Old men who have spent their lives in the must intellectual society of Lon- don agree that there was no equal to Mr. Villiers at his best in the delicate are of conversation. Yet be never raised lals voice ; he never tried to make people listen, he never seemed to be saying, anything in particular. His appearance was distinguished, even when he was bent almost double with age. His high forehead, his keen eyes, his sharply-cut, clearly-chiselled features,sug- gem e a man with a history, and a man of great met.. eat p,,„, r. In his old age he was gifted with almost univer:aelhleference, and the silence when he spoke was BO complete that his lowest tones were distinctly audible. He was not an enthusiast, and was certainly no believer in a political millennium. When asked what he thought would be the result of extending the county franchise he mentioned—l can think of no better word—that the cottagers' wives would get more blankets, and the landlords would get more votes. Of an eminent statesman's relations with the peers in his Cabinet he said " always down on his knees before %hem "—a pause—" except when he's kicking them about." This was not really an ill-natured remark, or I would not have quoted it. It simply referred in Mr. Villiers's caustic style to the union in the same indivi- dual of Radical politics with old-fashioned acknowledg- ment of rank and respect, for constitutional usage. Mr. Villiers was no Radical himself, though in early life he would have been sat down as an advanced Liberal.He was not very keenly interetted in any reform after 1846, except the usefel changes he made in the poor law as President of the Poor Law Board under Lord Palmerston. He voted pretty steadily for Liberal measures in the House of Commons up to 1886. But be WA.NRINGTON BRI D iE was generally believed to have resisted all constitutional changes in Lord Palmerston's Cabinet, and Mr. Glad- DEPUTATION TO THE COUNTY COUNCIL. stone did not include him in the Cabinet of 1868. The A CHILLING RECr:P NON. ostensible mason is said to have been that Lord Charon- A deputation from the Warrington Town Council, don was Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs, and that accompanie one Villiers was enough. tie), waited upon by the ' • J. Town the Highway, Clerk (Mr. J Lyon Whit- The fact is that Mr. Gladstone and Mr. Villiers were and Bridges Corn- at opposite poles of political thought and character, mittee of the Lancashire Cou Council at their re- Mr. Gladstone was all fire, energy, and zeal ; Mr. quest last week, with a Vies, ot supporting the hfares converged upon the Villiers was all criticism, scepticism, and contempt. It application made for a new bridge across the :der; must, however, be remembered that as a Free Trader sey at Warrington. The deputation pointed out Mr. Villiers stood at first almost alone in his class ; that emonptmroo.asi.neTrothheeenTioowl schemac le roxf tht, :in 1835 the Whigs were more than half Protectionists, •bridge on the South Side, and three the north,and and that he fought with undaunted com age for his own that in coneequence of the increasingtraffic, intellectual convictions. present structure was inadequate to meet the re- He had none of Mr. Cobden'e dislike for office. He explained corportahtieognre fora t took what Palmerston offered him,and was a thoroughly . capable head of his Department, His real recompense, i widening the principal teoroughfares to 60ft at a however,was the unabated and,in the circumstances,un- I wide exampled fidelity of his constituents. He appeared, I cost of about a quarter of a million of money. The think, three times in the Division Lobby of the late deputation, however, saw from the very beginning House of Commons. He certainly voted for retaining 1 that the Chairman of the County Council Highways Lord Salisbury's Government in power after the General ' Committee (sir. II uli on) haul made up his Election of 1892, and against the second reading of the ' mind adversely to the r, guest of the Corpora- Home Rule Bill in 1893. But he had practically ceased tion. From a remark eele„h was mad- for almost a generation to attend Parliament or to visit vertently made he appeared to be annoyed, his constituency. Yet for 12 years the electors of South because the Corporation had invit 1 h deputation Wolverhampton returned their absent and inactive f t c Council • " o he mintywhich visited Warrington a member without a contest. As he said himself, in his , • few weeks ago, to inspect, the bridge. Only one of dignified refusal of a pecuniary testimonial, The re- these gentlemen—Mr. Jackson—it was pointed out ward of public service is public confidence." was a member of the Ilighways and Bridges Com- mittee of the County Council. Mr. flultoo, it seems, visited Warrington and inspected the bridge on his own account. In answer to his enquiries, a police- man informed him ; that he had no difficulty in regulating the traffic over the bridge, the policeman no doubt thinking that the inquiry referred to the traffic on the bridge at the time. Mr. Hulton also stepped soros+ the bridge, antl came to the conclu- sion that the present structure is able to meet all nst .tu'raierteettm,} the demands upon it for County purposes, The result of the interview eves that the com- mittee of the County Council ah.,olutely refused to take into consideration any application fur is bridge 60 feet wide on the ground that no existing bridge anywhere was of the same width as the streets con- verging upon it. The deputation was politely in- formed that when the Corporation had completed their improvements for widening the streets, the County Council Committee might poeeibly favour- ably consider a proposal to extend the width of the present hedge from 20 feet to 30 feet. The mem- bere of the deputation who had been accommodated with a back seat in the commit te. room then with- drew, no doubt more convinerd ten ever that the only way oat of the increasing difficulties with the County Council is the setting on u,f Warrington as a County borough on its own nce•lunt.</p>
