      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <p>letterS to tbe Obitor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>TWO FAMOUS ROADS.</head>
        <p>TWO FAMOUS ROADS.</p>
        <p>Sr,—Your correspondent last week prodded the lion very effectually, but whether be will rise is yet doubt- ful. I would suggest, before it is too late, that the Cor- poration enclose the Latchford and Wilderspool-roads and keep them as samples of what may be done to- wards representing hill and dale on a small scale by the judicious sprinkling of loose macadam on odd occasions. Both thoroughfares are famous throughout Lancashire and Cheshire, and it would be a pity were their un- common and striking features altered permanently. Hoping that this little scheme may meet with the approval accorded less sensible ones which have passed the Co aneil.—l am. Yours truly, MUD.</p>
        <p>THE CONDITION OF ENITTSFORD-ROAD.</p>
        <p>Sir—Yeur correspondent' Max" complains that the Knutsford-road is sadly in need of repair. I beg to sub- scribe to that complaint. I have used the roadway both as pedestrian, cyclist and 'bus p monger and in each capacity I have found my journeys most unpleasant. There are hills in the pavement which need to be brought low and valleys to be laid straight. May I ask the Committee to perfoim these acts, if not cf duty, of kindness, and so enable the inhabitants of Latchford to peso in and out of Warrington without discomfort. The Moutsford-road is not a bror lway. Make it a level way and we shall be content.—Yours faithfully, LATCHFORDIAN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>IRISH DISTRESS FUND.-WHAT IS THE</head>
        <p>THE IRISH DISTRESS FUND.-WHAT IS THE</p>
        <p>COMMITTEE DOING?</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 am particularly anxious to know what steps are to be taken ') make the Warrington fund worthy of the lien. Father Whittle made a suggestion at the men ng in the Town Ball, that the various religious boiiss in the town should have a collection in their churches and chapels for the poor starving people in Ire- land. As yet, I have not beard of this suggestion being taken up. We had collections on behalf of the Indians; and why not do the like for Christian people in Ireland? Hoping that our good Mayor will call a meeting of the committee, and that they will make some practical arrangements which will have the effect of adding con- siderably to the fund.—l am, sir, yours,&amp;c., " SURSUM GORDA."</p>
        <p>THE CONDITION OF WILDERSPOOL ROAD. S4,—lt's all very well for your correspondent to com- plain about Latchford-road. I suppose he uses it and only thinks of himself, but I hope the Mayor and his friends on the Paving Committee will not devote all their money and time to one place only. We on Wilderspool Causeway deserve a little attention anda procession from Bridge Foot to the Saracen's Head' and back—not stop- ping too lone for refreshments—would not be a bad idea. I don't think Latchford-road is any worse or better than our road. Why cannot these roads be made as good as the Cheshire main roads, and why doesn't the Corpora- tion use its read roller a bit more ? Is it correct that they rmnot let the roller go out when they want to make ms.cadam because the engine is required to drive the machine that breaks the atones ? If it is correct it is about the most "one horse" arrangemert I ever beard of. When the roller is out no stones can be broke and when it is in driving the stone breaker the poor horses must do the work ot the roller. This looks like a penny wise and pound foolish policy with a vengeance. What are our three (I was going to say intelligent, but I don't want to hurt their feelings)LatMford Councillors doing. Let us try and wake them up.—Yours. &amp;c., WILDERSPOOL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SUNDAY OBSERVANCE.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY OBSERVANCE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of the Bth inst. I asked" boa" for reference to a record which he asserted was true with reference to the fourth Commandment. He neither withdraws the statement nor attempts to substantiate it—the latter, obviously, to bible students,because there is no such record in the New Testament. It would not leave required any further correspondence on my part if your correspondent had not so palpably drawn infer- ences from the texts mentioning the first day of the week,which a reference to the context shows to be quite unwarrantable and in which there is about the same amount of logic as there would be if I told him I wor- shipped the stars,and quoted as my reason for doing so. " Which maketh Arcturus, Orion and Pleiades, and the Chambers of the South." I must also take exception to " lona's" statement that" the fourth Commandment is part of a law en-' acted for the government of the world." The law of Moses was delivered to the Israelites and not to the Gentiles, who were therefore " without the law.' "What things saever the law &amp;title, it says to them who are under the law " and consequently the gentiles (un- less they became faithful Jews by adoption) were outside •both the blessing and curses of the law. On the one band we have the general tenor of bible history beating out the foregoing position, and such a definite position asia to be found in Amos 3 and 2, where the Lord speaks this word against the children of Israel " You only have I known of all the families of the earth, therefore I will punish you for all your iniquities," and on the other hand conjecture and sophisticated reasoning of such a character as will I venture to think be not satisfactory even to" lona " if he will only put prejudice aside and etudy the Bottk which in this correspondence he has more than once misquoted—as above mentioned and in his first letter. If" lona " will read my previous letters he will find his question re the law answered to some ex- tent, and further references are numerous, direct ana in- direct—showing that Christ was the ead of the law,even to those who had been under the law.ln Ephesians, 2e.15 We read " Raving abolished in his flesh the enmity,' even the law of commandments contained in ordin- ances," and in Romans 10.2 to 4 thus" For I bear them record that they have a zeal of God, but not according to knowledge. For they being ignorant of God's right- eousness, and going about to establish their own righteousness, have not submitted themselves unto the righteousness of God. For Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to everyone that believeth." The Eng- .lish law is binding on those who are under it,the Mosaic was obligatory on the Jewish nation,and so also the law of Christ is binding on those who are under that law hence I admit that nine of the commandments are obli- gatory on those who are under the law of Christ. I think you,lifr. Editor, for the space given for this correspondence, and in conclusion would ask your cor- 'respondent " lona "to make sure that his premises are correct when next he considers a biblical subj Pct or otherwise he may get u far from the truth as he has done in this correspondence.—Yours truly, R.G.L</p>
        <p>HALF-YEARLY SALE.</p>
        <p>- GEORGE HENRY LE S' &amp; CO., LIMITED. BASNIETT-STREET, LIVERPOOL,</p>
        <p>—ViilloEfer_on</p>
