      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting on Sunday afternoon was presided over by Mr. James Smith. Mr. Thomas Wheatley was the principal speaker, and the voca- list was Miss Ruth Uren. Mr. C. Drew gave a reci- tal, and Miss F. Holliday presided at the piano.</p>
        <p>OLD FOLKS' TREAT. A meeting of the committee for carrying out the arrangements for an Old Folks treat was held on Thursday night. The arrangements were carried forward a stage, and the date fixed for holding the treat was February 4th. Those eligible to partici- pate are persons over 60 years of age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>1.0.G.T. 1107 R OF EARLESTOWN LODGE.</head>
        <p>1.0.G.T. 1107 R OF EARLESTOWN LODGE.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly session was held on Monday night, presided over by Bro. T. Cutler C.T. One candidate was admitted. Bro. B. Newman reported on the annual Council meeting at Liverpool and also on the three-fold bazaar to be held at Mount Pleasant Wesleyan Chapel, Liverpool. on the 15th and 16th of February.Arrangetnents were made for a vifit to Leigh on Tuesday. The programme for the evening was of a social character. There was a large gathering present. MUSICAL FESTITAL AT EAXLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>In another column will be found an announce- ment of a musical festival to be given in aid of the fund for cleaning and renovating the organ of St. John's Church, on Thursday evening next. It will be seen that a good company of soloists has been engaged, and a chorus of O voices has during the past few weeks been undergoing special training by Mr. H. W. Radford, organist and choirmaster. Selections from the "Messiah" will be given, and it is hoped Mr. Radford'a efforts will be adequately supported. ST. JOHN'S CHURCH SOCIAL GATHRRING. On Monday evening the choir of St. John's Church, with the church officials to the number of about 80, were entertained by the Vicar at the Mission Hall, Earlestown, the occasion being the cutting up and distribution of Mr. Molyneux's annual Christmas cake. An excellent tea was served to which full justice was done. After the tables had been cleared, the Vicar, Rev. T. R. Matthews addressed a few words of encouragement to those present, after which the evening was spent in a social manner, and was of an enjoyable character.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>CONGREGATIONAL SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>CONGREGATIONAL SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a tea party and concert was held in the above schools. About 150 sat down to an excellent tea, at which nearly all the Sunday school teachers attended. After tea the room was cleared and a very interesting programme was gone through. Mr. Allatt, in opening, said that now that the new schoolroom was completed, he hoped that the children would attend more regularly than they had done in the past. Mr. Graham afterwards showed a large number of pictures with a magic lantern, which greatly amused the children. A vote of thanks was then accorded to Mr. Graham for his kindness in bringing his lantern. DEATH or MR. Joins AXON.</p>
        <p>It is with extreme regret that we have to record ithis week the death of Mr. John Axon, bootmaker lof Queen-street, Earlestown, which took place on luesday afternoon at his residence. Mr. Axon was I seized with an illness about a month ago, and erysipelas supervened. Dr. Mouncey gave him every attention, but his efforts at affecting a cure were 'without avail, and Mr. Axon died as stated above.He was 62 years of age, and leaves a widow and grown up son and daughter. Mr. Axon was a well-known figure, and during his 14 years residence in Earles- town, he has gathered about him a large circle of friends and acquaintances. He came to Earlestown from Geecross near Hyde, and will be buried there on Saturday next. THE BURNING FATALITY AT BARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>The inquest on the body of the child Mary Whit- ley who died from burns received on Tuesdaf last week was held at the Viaduct Hotel by Mr. S.Brig- house on Friday.—Mrs. Annie Whitley,mother, said she lived at 21, Bank-street, Earlestown. About 230 on Tuesday afternoon she went into a neigh- bour's house and took the child with her. She took some clothes to Mrs. Dickens, where there were some pigeons,which often amused the child. Whilst there witness heard a cry,and said to Mrs. Dickens, "just see what's up with that young puss now," and Mrs. Dickens said she was all over fire. The child was out of witness's sight about five minutes. As soon as witness saw the fire she sent for Dr. Metincey, but first got bold of her child and put clothes round her to put out the ffames,whilst some one fetched a macintosh. After that he remembered very little more. The child died at 5.25 next morn- ing. She had no idea bow the child gst on fire,and could get no signs. The fender was half a yard away from the fire and there was also a protector. Mrs. Julia Dickens gave corroborative evidence,and the jury returned a verdict of "Accidental death.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>_ _ -•___ NEWTON CPICKET, BOWLING AND TENNIS CLUB. --- ANNUAL GENERAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>_ _ -•___ NEWTON CPICKET, BOWLING AND TENNIS CLUB. --- ANNUAL GENERAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the members of the Newton-le-Willows Cricket, Bowling and Tennis Club was held at the Pied Bull Hotel, Newton, on Thursday evening. There was a large number of members present including, Mr. W. El. Hindley, Mr. J. Liptrott, Coua. oilier Simpson,C. H. Bodmer, kc. Mr. Hindley announced that the President, Mr. It. Stone had called upon him and expressed hii regret that he could not preside that evening, ache thought, owing to the great interest his late brother took In the club, if he did so it would recall remembrances which would be distressing, and he asked that his excuse be tendered the members. Mr. J. Simpson, the vice president, was then voted to this chair, and he said that as the secretary, Mr. F. Brierley, could not be present, it would be necessary for them to appoint a secretary for that evening. Mr. W. Gee was accordingly appointed,and read the minutes of the last annual meeting and of a committee meeting held on January 13th, which were confirmed on the motion of Mr. W. Speakman. The minutes of the com- mittee meeting held on 13th January contained a re- commendation that a paid secretary be appointed at a salary of fire guineas per annum, the selection of the secretary to be left in the hands of the General Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Fairclough movcd and Mr. T. Wilkinson seconded that therecotnmendations named ba approved,</p>
        <p>and the motion was unanimously carried. The treasurer (Mr. W. H. Hindley) submitted his fin- ancial statement of the season's working. Including a balance of £l5 15s. 11d, from the previous season, the receipts from all sources were £lB6 HO. and the expenditure £lBB Ts. 2d., leaving a balance to carry f )r- -ward of £lB lie. 03. This was the second best balance he bad had the honour of laying before them. He thought a club like their's should have £5O to lay their hands upon. During the past year they wanted a new pavi- lion, but they bad not sufficient funds in the -Tub to build one, During the past season they had had 38 n.w members, and 24 members had left, the total member. shiP being now 284. One of the members who had left was an honorary member,and he thought this was caused by some little misunderstanding as the ease was not put before him, by both sides al it ought to have been. He thought more firms in the district should subscribe to the club, as there was no doubt the club found healthy recreation for its employees. He was please to inform tuhnepmaidth,utbosuctriopftinaoll their members there was only one and although he had made a special application he was unable to collect it.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS. Patrons.—Lord Newton, Messrs. T.W.Legh, M.P..T.J. Gillespie, A. C. McCorquodale, and G. F. McCo7quodale. In moving the re-election of Mr. Robert S:one as presi- dent, he expressed on behalf of that gentleman his regret at not being able to attend the annual meetina, and announced his intention of giving annually a gold Medal for competition. The motion was unanirnou•ly carried. _ .</p>
        <p>The following were elected the Vice Presidents: Messrs. J. Faircloi igh,R. Brierley,J. Simpson, W. Glover, Dr. H. Watkins, H. J. Longton, W. Longton, and R. Stuart. Mr. Sankey proposed the re-appointment of Mr. W. H. Hindley as treasurer, for another 12 months. He did not think he would have very much to do, but he had no doubt be would keep his eye on those they elected to do the work. He had done yeoman work, and deserved all the praise they could possible give him. Mr, R. Chandley seconded,and the motion was carried with acclamation. I The Chairman read a communication from Mr. F. , Brierley (secretary) in which he regretted his inability to attend the annual meeting of the Cricket Club,and it was also with regret that he informed them that he could not again act as secretary and must ask them to appoint another in his place. Mr. Speakman moved that they accept the resignation and that they tender the thanks of the members for his services in connection with the club as secretary. Mr. E. Fairclough seconded, and the motion was adopted. The following were appointed cricket captains : Messrs. W. H. Hindley,R. Chandley, W. Wilkinson. and J. Pendry. Bowling captains : Messrs. T. Fairclough,J. W. Crouchley, J. Aitken, and W. H. Bodmer. Tennis captains : Messrs. G. Houghton and D.Berry. Auditors: Mr. T. Mayor and A. Wagge.</p>
        <p>Ktrix.—• New white inorganic Cement for Pottery POI ce' lain &amp;c.,6d and Is. postage Id. Of Chemist s.—Adrt. LINITY CATHASTICUM PILLS digestive, corrective, at d agreeably aperient, Sid., 130., Ofall Chemiste.—ildve</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL</p>
        <p>DISTRIBUTION OF PRIZES.</p>
        <p>•On Sunday afternoon the prizes gained by the child- ren attending the Bulestown Wesleyan Sunday School were distributed in the chapel by the Rev. P. R. Pass. more who was supported by Mr. .1. Thurley of St. Helens Mr. W. Clare (superintendent), and Mr. I. E. Barr (Secretary). After a formal opening, the Rev. P. R. Passmore called upon Mr. Barr (secretary) to read the 43rd annual report for the year, which stated that the school is divided into two sections. viz., senior and junior; these are further divided into 31 classes. Num- ber of teachers and ofßeers on the books 76, of which 35 are required in the morning and 41 ia the afternoon. Average attendance of teachers morcing 19, afternoon 26, number of scholars, including two senior classes, 460, average attendance, morning 135. after- noon 276. Forty-sine of the teachers are members of society • 67 scholars including juniors, meet in society claues. The Juvenile Missionary Association is a source of great encouragement, and speaks well for the energy and tact of its secretaries. The collectors have gathered together th 3 noble sum of upwards of £44, an increase upon last year. The moneys there collected were divided in the following manner— me third to Foreign Missions, -e third to General Hi Missiorm,and a third to their</p>
        <p>000 .- i 01706 own circuit mission work. The library which contains about 600 volumes, has been closed during the year swing to there not being a suitable place to keep the books in. They very much regretted that their Band of Hope, which is part of the Sunday School, had not met during the last 12 months, but they hoped that the young ladies and young men interested in this great cause would see that it was restarted before another year passed away. The prizes to be presented that afternoon, showed the interest that was still taken In the round 0 class. The recipients of a first division prize, value three shillings' scholars over nine years of age, had gained 100 marks for early attendance and 120 marks far tablet work, and those having a second divi- sion prize, valued two shillings, bad gained 94 marks for early attendance and 90 marks for tablet work. Scholars under nine years of age, having made 100 early attendances, received a first division prize, value two shillings, and for 94 early attendances a second division prize,value Is. 61. prizes were also given for early atten- dance and tablet work, value is 61 and is. The ne't income for the year was £59 us. na. and the expendi- ture £5353. CU, which balance from 198, £7 18s. fit left a balance on the years accounts of £l4- 4s. 101. In December a Christmas tree and sale of work were held, the object of which was to raise funds towards the pur- chasing of a new banner to head the school on Walking days, and other school reqeisites. The total receipts from the sale were £53 7s. 11d., and the expenditure about £7 43, 31. leaving a balance of £46 31. 81. An address was afterwards given by Mr. J. Thurley of St. Helens, who directed his remarks to the children and congratulatel them upon the privei they had gained. Be referred to the fact that Mr. Clare, superintendent, had been compelled to give up his position in the school. He had been with thorn a long time and had worked hard in the cause of the sch•nal,and they were very sorry to lota him.</p>
        <p>The prizes. which consisted of handsomely bound books, were distributed by Mr. Passmore. During the afternoon Miss D. Roberts sang " The Children's Home," and "The Children of the King," and Miss E. Goodwin sang " Jesus bids us shine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>(BY AN OCCASIONAL CORRESPONDENT.)</p>
        <p>The Local Government enquiry which was held at the Town Hall last weak was not the happy affair which, I fancy, was anticipated. The Inspec- tor showed himself a man fully acquainted with his business, and his queries proved at times most awkward to those concerned. At the outset, the Inspector drew in a pointed manner the Medical Officer's attention to a public msnure tip in the midst of a populous neighbourhood. Another matter which drew forth some forcible obser- vations from the inspector was the question of sewage. It was admitted that we had two sewage works with 6i acres of land available, but an admission which was not publicly made was the length of time the works had fallen into disuse. However the Inspector was bent upon satisfying himself on the point, and paid a visit to the works. I think he would be highly 1 edified by what he saw, and I think his report on , the enquiry will be capital reading for the general public. His observation that if they wanted new streets and more sewering they would have to make more sewage accommodation was quite in or- der and to the point. People talk about the expendi- ture of the Council, but if the regulations of the Local Government Board are to be complied with, the outlay will have to be considerably increased. We have 6i acres of sewage land, and we shoud have 15 acres, and this we must have before sanc- tion can be given for the loan.</p>
        <p>For want of funds the beautiful organ at St. John's Church. is being allowed to assume a shabby appearance. No effort haa hitherto been made to improve this condition of things, but Mr. Radford the new organist has an eye to the beautiful, and although he has only been here a very short time he has set about restoring the instrument to a con- dition creditable to himself and the church. For several weeks he has been working with this object in view, and his efforts will culminate on Thursday evening next in a musical festival, when selections from the"MeFaiak"will be given by a powerful chorus assisted by some first-class artistes from Manchester and Chester. The object is a worthy one and deser- ving of every support.</p>
        <p>-- The meeting of the Earlestown Fanciers A=socia- tinn last week was of a lively character, but, as was pointed out by the Chairman, the opening jDroceed- Ingsviere distinctly irregular. The initiators of theirregularity were speedily and rightly com- pelled to bite the dust. It is not necessary to enter into the causes which led up to the affair, but it is evident that rule 21, which governed the whole matter,had not been complied with, hence the con- fusion itoendwhich arose. It is a serious matter to expel w membersaeeo r according gto from arnu. any society, but when such be- comes necessary, the proceedings should be con-</p>
        <p>- THE ENGINEERS' DISPUTE. --- LATEST DEVELOPMENTS. The serve:try of the Jcint Committee of the Allied Trade Unions in London bag forwarded a copy of the letter drawn up by the Employers' Federation and addressed to the Committee to the secretary of each of the trade unions engaged in the engineering trades dis- puts, "for their consideration'and deeision." fie has also informed the secretaries of the Employers' Federation that an that course was necessary "it will be imPrAsible to repl by noon nn Friday," and the decision of the e allied trades will be communicated to the Federation Y at the earliest possible moment." The Esscutiye Com- mittee of th Amalgamated Society of ngineers on Thursday had the question of the acceptance of the terms of the emollyers under consideration. It was re- ported by the officials of the Federation at BOMA of the Solon, district .ffices that a number of additional look-out E notices were owterl on Thursday. Sir Bmjamin Dobson of eated that Sir C. Farness's Itt•er urgin on the em o overs a conciliatory attitude was " laughed at" .g 1 by the FedorAtiol, and was considered as being " ustft trifla too thin."</p>
      </div>
