      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>ffili EA AIIINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 2l, 1898</head>
        <p>ffili EA AIIINER, FRIDAY, JANUARY 2l, 1898</p>
        <p>company from the said James Nicholson at a price to be attreed upon or failing agreement as shall be settled by arbitration in the manner provided by the Lands Clauses Acts and the said James Nicholson his family and servants shall at all times be entitled to use and pass across or along the ferry and footpath afore- said free of all dues and charges whatsoever from the user thereof.' You will see from this that we are only to charge the same toll as was charged for crossing the Thelwall Manor Ferry. We have found the old Ferry Board, which as respects passengers gives the charge at lii. each person between 6 a.m. and 9 p.m., but toll only being charged one way. We have so pro- vided in the schedule of charges recently put into opera- tion, and I enclose you copy of this schedule herewith, from which you will see that we are not levying a charge of 2d. each person for crossing and re-crossing Thelwall Ferry, but id. ,as previously charged for use of the old Manor Ferry. I trust that the information herein given will be satisfactory to your Coun- cil.—Yours truly, W. H. COLLIER, manager. Mr. W. B. Cook, clerk to the Thelwall Parish Council." —Mr. Houghton raid that if anyone crossed the ferry with horses,to eet on the boat they had to go up four or five feet and down four or five feet, and if any acci- dent occurred they would not be responsible. They crossed the ferry at the risk.—Mr. Blackly said the Ship Canal Company would be responsible if they did not pro- vide convenient means of access. They had nothing to do but to give them notice that they were not comply- ing with the Act of Parliament by not giving the means of proper access, and if any accident happened they were responsible, and they had nothing to do but to sue them for it. If anyone in that parish complained to the Clerk it was their business to compel the Ship Canal Company to provide the necessary access. Why should people risk their property in crawling the canal ? He understood that the old ferry could not take carts, flocks of sheep, or cattle of any kind—all they had a right to do was to charge according to the Act of Parliament. A gentleman told him it used to be the custom to carry passengers for after nine o'clock p.m. until six in the morning.—The Clerk : That was according to the notice boards.—Mr. Houghton said the tenants of the farmers could go over with the horses and carts at any time of the night, if they gave notice, and the public from six a.m. until 11' Bleckly thought they ought to consult the Act of Parliament and see what they were compelled to do. Be moved that the Clerk write to the Ship Canal Company stating that the Council noticed there was no mention of any crossing being allowed after nine p.m., and that they wished to know whether any passengers who presented themselves later than that time would be taken across.—Mr. Houghton seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>OLDHAM'S STRUGGLE FOR THE CHAMPIONSHIP A CLOSE FIGHT AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM'S STRUGGLE FOR THE CHAMPIONSHIP A CLOSE FIGHT AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>It was Derby Day at Warrington on Saturday when ttTe all conquering Oldham fifteen appeared on the Wilderspool Ground to play off their return engagement. Strangely enough there is almost al- ways a fine day for Oldham's visit to Warrington, and as the match has for years been one of the finest of each successive season, the crowd is always of large dimensions. This is the day when "enthus- iasts" who are content with one match a year,ven- ture to spend their 61., and reserve all their shout- ing power. Up to date Oldham have only bowed the knee once, and then to their powerful rivals at Swinton, and among their victories was one of' 10 points to nil against Warrington. Nothwithstand- ing that the odds were so greatly against them War- rington entered the field with a determination to win if possible and the gallant fight they made proves once more that Oldham have no more dangerous opponents away irom home. The teams turned out as selected, Barber being still absent from the home ranks,and at the commence- ment of hostilities there would be close on 10,000 spectators present, Three train loads came from Oldham. A pretty strong breeze blew across the ground, but otherwise the conditions were emin- en.ly favourable for the match. Mr. Priestley, of Broughton Rangers acted as referee, and the teams were composed as follows Oldham: Thomas,full back; J. Hurst, I,P.Taylor, S. Lees, and J. Martin,threequarters ; A. Lees and J. Lawton, half backs; Broome, Frater,Bonsor,Davies, Ellis Edwards, J. Lees, and Tailor, forwards. Warrington: Boscow, full back; Moores, Uallam, Cross.and Isherwood,threequarters; Bate and Duck- worth, half backs; Taylor, Deakin, Hill, Swift, Bar- rett, Fairhurst, Robey, and Mason, forwards. " Smack " Sates re-appearance was hailed with cheers, the satisfaction being further increased when he won the toss and elected to play with the wind in the first half.</p>
        <p>Sam Lees started and Hill mullingthe return,Warring- ton were the first to defend against a rapid onslaught from the Oldham front rank. Boacow checked the movement in a workmanlike manner. Duckworth and Bate relieved cleverly and Thomas being at fault allowed Warrington to reach the centre. • better complexion was pus on the game by Cross and Hallam who made a determined entry into the Oldham hall, Martin being forced to kick in touch at the IS Bag. &amp;scow centred as ell and a scrimmage was formed in front of the goal, when Bate got away only to meet his match in Lawton. The latter retaliated by tricking Duckworth and putting in a lengthy run before he kicked to the line where Cross saved. The next minute Lawton was again almost over, welcome relief corning from Bate. Oldham were soon on the attack again and their superior finish told. Lawton got the ball out and handed to Taylor who slipped_ Cross and Moores before hand• log to Hurst, for the latter to score • grand try. S. Lees easily kicked a goal, and put the finishing touch on a beautiful piece of combination. The effect of the score was to stir up Warrington to greater endeavours. Irre- sistible forward play changed tho venue to the opposite end, where Thomas was conspicuous with some sterling defensive play. Taylor ( warrington) made a dash for the lino but the watchful Newporter met Min in at the corner. The homesters would not be denied and gave the Oldham men no rest. A smart passing movement saw Isherwood testing he defence on the other side,and being cornered he cleverly kicked across the goal,Deakin grounding amid tremendous cheering. Istierwood'e ac. tempt at goal was a complete failure. Still two points in the rear of Warrington, played foe all they were worth add mainly through the cleverness of the forwards aided by resolute tacking behind„ maintained 1 their hold. Taylor again secured and kicked across, Mason electrifying everybody by racing up, and scoring the second try. Taylor also was unable to negotiate a , goal. At this stage of the game Oldham were overplayed in every department, and owed much to Thomas's grand work in the rear. Ike Taylor and Hurst attempted a relief but were promptly upset, and Moores responded I with a speedy cross-run which gained ground though Bate did riot take the ball well. A series of mishaps caused Warrington to lose the ball in touch and Oldham worked their way U. Several openings were made for Hurst but Moores was on him every time. Hallam made good use ot is penalty, Taylor and Martin both kicking in touch at critical moments. This confession of weak- ness was followed by Thomas touching down to avoid disaster from the home vanguard. Ike Taylor broke through only to he grassed by Duckworth vrho was playing a hard game. Oldham got away and Bate gave them a further lift through over-eagerness, a penalty accruing. S. Lees dropped at goal,but failed iu his obj:ict. A short spell o; play on the home line was euded by Hallam punting strongly to the centre,but the effort was nullified by Ike Taylor being denied room to kick. Oldham resorted to passing but could make little headway against the home backs,who led an incursion to the visitors' hue. Moores nearly got in, Hurst tackling him at the very corner. Another penalty against Warrington cleared tbe Oldham line, and at hill time Warrington held the lead by two tries (6 points) to Oldham I goal 1 try (5P01048)• Taylor Set tue ball in motion, and the visitors soon ehowed themselves to be still pretty fresh,causing Cross to step in on the line. Horst only failed by inches at repeating his further performance, Boscow effecting a fine tackle at the corner irreg. Then one of those lightnini changes came over '.ha game. Play was proceeding warmly La the corner. when Hallam kicked out, and Warrington had hardly formed a sigh of relief, when Thomas caught the ball and coolly dropped a goal from centre. This was a staggerer tor the nomesters who rallied fulely,lsloores and Bate having hard lines at the other end. Agaiu the game reversed and Lawtsn receiving from A. Lees ran in between the plats, Lawless kicking another. With a clear lead of six points Oldham appeared to have the game at their feet, but Warrington were not quite done with. A lot of the dash seemed to have been taken out of them, but Bate Duckworth. and Isherwood effected a promising move- ment. A. Lees stole round a scrimmage and after run- ning half the field was kneed to punt, and Moores returned the ball strongly. Lawton followed suit a moment later but Moores gave him no chance of pass- leg. Hallam kicked to the line and Martha attempted to return, reckoning without Mason who charged the ball down and scored again near the posts. Once more the goal-kick failed. Now Warrington recovered their dash and made things lively for their opponents. Hal- lam nearly got in at the corner. A. Lees punted out, Moores returned and Warrington were at it hammer and tongs. Duckworth and Bate also got through some fine work, but the Oldham defence was equal to the strain. Time had barely elapsed when the whistle sounded, and Warrington had to accept defeat by 3 goals 2 tries (12 points) to 3 tries (9 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST YEAR'S TRAFFIC. During 1897 it is proved by the returns now available that the total weight of traffic on the Manchester Ship Canal was as follows :—lnward (tons) 1,053,837., out- ward 648,962, barge traffic 365,338, total 2,065,935. In 1898 the total was 1,828,237 tons, so that last year shows an increase of traffic. uf 239,698 tons. The in- crease during 1898, was 474,888 as compared with 1895, Sc that progress has not been maintained to the degtri that was anticipated. Dried fruits show a falling off in the imparts ; Limner has gone up from 179,859 in 1808 to 223,147 list year, and wood pulp paper, and paper. making materials have advanced trona 95,471 to 113,400 toms.</p>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL FERRY TOLLS AT THELVVALL. This subject was raised at the quarterly meeting of the The!wall Parish Council on Monday night. The Rev. T. P. Williamson presided, and there were also present: Rear-Admiral Jones-Parry, Messrs. U. Roebuck, N. Tay- lor, a Lambert, W. Houghton, W. H. Bleckly, and W. U. Cook (clerk). The following cnrrespondence between tke Council and the Scio Canal Company was read : Thelsvall, 19:h October, 1897. Sir.—At a meeting of the Thelwall Parish Council held last night, I read your ack- knowledgment of the receipt of:my letter, dated 29th July, 1197, re tolls at the Thelwall Nerry. The Council were unanimously of opinion that you had treated them most discourteously in not having replied to that letter. arid I was requested to draw your attention to the fact that my letter still remains unanswered, and to ask for a speedy reply. There an be no doubt that the Ship Canal Company were contravening tke Act, and the Council have no official information as to the Ship Canal Company's procedure with the matter of tolls at the ferry. I, therefore,have to rest lest thst you will kindly answer me queries addressed to you in my last letter, and furnish me with a reply at your earliest possible , convenience.—l am, sir, yours faithfully, W. B. Coos, cli.rk. Mr. W. H. Collier, manager of the Manchester Slop Canal Company, Dock Office, Manchester." The Clerk received tho following reply :—" Manager s Office, Dock Office, Manchester, October 20:h, 1897, Dar Sir,—Upon receipt of your letter of July 20th last (whin WAS duly acknowledged), we have several points before us upon the question you have railed,but further inquiries had to be made. Upon completion of our in- quiries we unfortunately overlooked your communica- tion, for which please accept apology. I beg to say that hy the Ship Canal Act nt 1885 the right of user of this ferry is given to certain pasons, but outside those pets ins we claim to be entitled to charge for: the use thereof. I give you below copy of section 100 sub-section 3, clause 2, of our 1885 Ac, viz :—` A ferry across the canal suitable for the conveyance of foot passengers at or near the Ipoint where the canal will intersect the said road num- ber 379 with convenient means ef access along the northerly side of the canal between the said road num- ber 379 and the point where the canal will intersect the footpath aforessid and the company shall at all times hereafter provide the necessary labour to work and shall work the said ferry whenever rr quired in the same man- ner and for the same toll as the said Thelvrall Manor Ferry across the River Mersey is now worked. And the said 'thelwall Manor Ferry shall be purchased by the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>INTERESTING WEDDING AT</head>
        <p>INTERESTING WEDDING AT</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>HICLIARDS-BRUNDRETT,</p>
        <p>The wedding was solemnised at the Parish Church, Warrington, on Tuesday, of Mr. S. R. Richards, Liver- pool, and Edith, eldest daughter of Mrs. Brundrett, Church-st. A large amount of int( rest wae taken locally in the event, the family of the bride being closely associated with the town. There was conse- quently a large congregation of relatives and friends in the sacred edifice, while a crowd of no small dimensions surrounded the entrance gates. The ceremony was per- formed by the Rev. G. B. Thurston, the newly- appointed rector of Lymm,and the customary marriage music was played on the organ by Mr. W. A. Wood (the organist of Grappenhall). Inside the church there 1115 some tasteful decoration from the font to the altar, arum lilies, primulas, and palms being freely used. The bride, who it is almost needless to re- mark, looked charming, was given away by her brother, Mr. Gilbert Brundrett. She was prettily attired in fawn, trimmed with ivory white and turquoise blue, and carried a shower bouquet composed of lilies of the valley. Her travelling dress was purple cloth with white revers and hat to match. As bridesmaids she had her sisters, Miss Kate and Miss Mary Brundrett, who wore plum-coloured dresses with pink and black ; trimmings, and carried bouquets of violets and pink ; hyacinths, and wore bangles, the gift of the bridegroom. ; Mr. L. B. Matthews, of Birmingham, acted as best elan. ;At the conclusion of the ceremony Mendelssohn's ; "Wedding March "was played,and as the happy couple emerged from the church they were subjected to a deluge of rice and confetti from their friends, and the bells also rang out a merry peal. The wedding breakfast was held at the Lion Hotel. and was handsomely served by Mrs. Gilbert, the man- ageress. The guests included Councillor and Mrs. Alfred Woods, Mr. John Richards, Mr. W. Richards, Miss Richards (Liverpool), Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Browne, the Rev. G. B. Thurston, and Mr. W. A. Woods. Mr. and Mrs. Richards left by the 3-20 train for Leamington, where the honeymoon will be spent. In the evening at eight o'clock, at the invitation of Mrs. Brundret, 138 guests, including the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Wright), assembled at the Lion Hotel for dancing, the music being supplied by Mr. R. H, Pearson's band, Mr. Arthur Browne acting as M.C. Supper was provided from ten to twelve. Councillor Woods superintended the whole of the arrangements for the wedding..</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the presents : Bridegroom to bride, silver card case and gem ring ; bride to bridegroom, pearl and diamond scarf pin ; Mrs Brundrett, drawing room suite and linen; Mr G T Brundrett, silver mounted Doulton jug and cups ; Misses K and Mary Brtm- drett, silver tea service ; Mr and Mrs T Harding, china clock; Mr and Mrs T Nash, tea urn ; Mr and Mrs W Hewitt, egg stand and spoons; Mr and Mrs Shaw, jam dish; Mr and Mrs R Byes, hot water jug ; Mr and Mrs Alfred Woods, silver mounted Doulton salad bowl and server ; men-servants and maidservants, eiderdown quilt ; Mrs Monks. silver cake saw ; Mrs Nicholson, pair of vases ; ssventeen tenants, Chippendale chair " Kitchener's," baromoter ; Mr, and Mrs Heatheote, silver card basket ; Mr and Mrs Morton, hearthrug ; Mrs Hatton, dinner cruet, fish slice, half-dozen silver spoons ; Mr and Mrs S Barlow, hot water jug and salver; Miss Rochester live o'clock tea cloth ; Mr and Mrs Cox, breakfast cruet ; " Parlour," silver cake basket ; Mr W A Wood, toast and butter stand ; Mr L Mathews, pair Wonder vases ; Mrs and Misses Craik. pair fens pots ; Mrs Whittle, flower stand; Mr T L Woods, silver inkstand ; Misses Woods, silver bread fork • Mr and Mrs J Young, jam dish; Mr. , and Mrs H Moult, linen bag; Mr and Mrs William Gill (Winmarleigh-street), pair fern pots; Miss L Pete, dinner gong ; Miss M Smethurst,table cloth; Mns Burton crumb tray and brush; Mr and Mrs Harrison, marmalade jar; Mr G H I Greenough, pair cut glass scent bottles ; Mr and Mrs J II Smethurst, sugar bowl and sifter; Miss A Wood, antima• cassar ; Mr and Mrs Jackson Lee, lone dozen !serviettes ; Mr and Mrs Chalmers, case of scent bottles ; Miss B Rem, brass inkstand ; six College chums, pair brass vases ; Miss Locker. pair silver salts; Mr Houghton, kitchen fender ; Mr and Mrs Greenhouse, Cnippendale chairs ; Miss A Dew- burst. wrought iron and glass vases ; Mr. Parkinson. fancy clothes basket; Miss B Twigs, fruit bowl ;Messrs R and J Finney, handbag; Miss B Whittle, wool hearthrug ; Mr kilgginson, brass stool ; Mr Tilley, clothes line; Mr and Mrs Spinks„ silver-mounted scent bottle ; Mr and Mrs P Gorst Witter dish and knife; Mr and Mrs J Allison, des- sert service ; Mr and , Mrs P Brans, antima- cassar ; Mr and Mrs G Whitehouse, fruit dish ; Mr F Aukland, spirit kettle and lamp; Miss J Jones, hairpin box ; Miss E Bennett. three Copeland jam dishes ; Miss Cooper. pair copper and brass candlesticks ; Mr ansl Mrs Matthews (Dublin), drawing room Clock; Mr H Dooley, shaving glass ; Mr and Mrs J Evans, drawing room lamp; Miss 1, Green, crumb tray and brush; Mr J Richards, card table ;Mr A Roberts (Iloylake), afternoon tea tray ; Miss M Brown, embroidered clan ; Mr P J Whittle, bread knife and fork ; Mr and Mrs R Johnson, lounge ch..ir Miss L Wenham, Swiss net mats; Miss A Cash,dra,wa thread cloth; Mr and Mrs Mainwaring and family, cala of four silver fruit spoons ; Mr and Mrs A Browne. case of silver salts and spoons ; Mrs Mottrarn, Dresden fruit dish ; Mr W and Mrs Owen, marble timepiece; Mr and Mns P Warbur ton, pickle Jar; Mr H Gandy, sugar basin and sifter; Mr- Richardson, breaksast Cruet; Master W and Miss Robinson. Cheese scoop ; Mrs Bothwell, fruit dish ; "Aeon," pair of framed views ; " Anon," atternoon tea servicz.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WA RRINGTON. During the week ending Saturday, January Bth,, 1898, the tollowing births and deaths were registered :-</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WA RRINGTON. During the week ending Saturday, January Bth,, 1898, the tollowing births and deaths were regis- tered :-</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths. Warriagton Registration District se 23</p>
        <p>The excels; of bias over deaths was 3 .37 Birth Rate 463 per 1,000. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 19 per 1400., against 16 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 227 the corresponding period of last year. The death.. rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to Nil per 1,000, against 3.3 the average rate of the previous four weeks, and against 4.2 the corresponding period ot 1897. The ages of mortality were- 0-1. 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 7 1 3 1 4, 7 23. The deaths included-2 from otithisis, 3 fiom bron. chitis, pneumonia. &amp; 2 from heart disease, 2. from injuries, all other causes 14. _ .</p>
        <p>71'he following eases of infectious disease have been reported during the week. Erysipelas, 1 case, enteric fever, .1 case,</p>
        <p>Total number of patients in hospital,on January 8-3. METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 43 Fah. and theretore 3 degrees lower than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 54 F. was recorded on the fith inst., and the minimum temperature 31. F., on the B.th inst. The rainfall measured 1.22 inch. The general direction was S.W., the reading of the anemometer denoting 600 nailes. JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>"E XAMINER" OFFICE, BRWSRY•STRERT, WARRINGTON. SPECIALITY</p>
