      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RrrGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Once again, the Warrington football enthusiasts have been treated to an exposition of the Rugby game, equal to that of any previous season. Oldham always play scientifically, and their superior tactics seem, peculiarly enough, to reflect upon their opponents at Warrington, whe in turn perform prodigies of valour and skill they were not believed capable of. Nobody will have forgot- ten the fact that last year, Oldham, in the height of their glory came a severe cropper at Warrington. After winning ten successive matches they visited Warrington so full of confidence that their jerseys were strained to bursting—and they lost. it was fondly hoped in the town that history would be repeated. As a coincidence people thought that the result ought to be again the same, biat the coincidence did not come off, and without beating about the bush I must admit that Warrington were beaten. The scoring was rather prolific, no WI than 21. points being notched during the game, and of which 12 fell to Oldhana and Sto Warrington. Every- thing considered the homesters did remarkably well, and but for the want ofa goal kicker would have won once more. It is rather astonishing to think that we crossed the Oldbara line &amp;times,while they only got past our de- fence on two occasions.</p>
        <p>There was a" bumper " gate,not an inch of standing , room being visible round the enclosure, and before the commencement of hostilities there was much discussion of the potand cons,. The Warrington case stood thus. Barber's enforced absence robbed the three-qmarter liner of halt its strength,but the rest of the team could not be improved upon. Add the determination of Warrington and it was felt that they would at least make a good show,though few were sanguine enough to expect a tory. It is rather strange that faulty goal kicking shout 1, have lost the match, This was a serious fault last season but Barber seems to have added the accomplishment to his many others and a Le:ter show has ,been made ' since.</p>
        <p>L imagine that their downfall twelve months ago, and keener recollections of the hard game at Oldham before Christmas, made the 5 from Spindledom less "cocky," and more inclined to strain every nerve. Their confid- ence was not too apparent, and the way in which they worked from beginning to end showed that nothing was intended to be lett to chance. Luck did not desert them, and there was a decided element of it in Thomas's dropped goal. Warrington were however by no means unfortunate in two of their tries. They just happened to get there first and that was all. Oldham deserve praise for their smart victory, and as there cannot be any questiun about their teing a better team than War- rington, we won't grudge them the points. I, for one would like to see them capture the championship, they have been rotibed "on the post" during the last couple of seasons. Swinton have an equal chance however, and it lies betweerctleem.</p>
        <p>Less than ten minutes after the start of the " grate ftte," Jack Hurst got his weekly try. There seems to be no preventing that man from scampering over the line during some portion of the game. He did it at Oldham and he repeated it on Saturday, despite Moores' watch- fulness. The feat was accomplished in a really beauti- ful fashion, and shpuld give Warrington a lesson. A quick pass from the half back to the centre, a short run past Cross and Moores by the latter, and then a light- ning transfer to Hurst finished a line concerted move- ment. Sam Lees kicked a goal, and Warrington found themselves five points behind in a twinkling.</p>
        <p>Instead of being downcast by this, Warrington re- doubled their efforts,and by sheer rush and energy pre- vented ()Wham even looking like winners up to half time. The forwards bent down the field like wolves and made things bum round the Oldham goal. Isherwood's head—without suggesting that the other players are witheut—again stood him in good stead. After a smart run he found the way blocked at the corner and with great coolness he punted judiciously over the Oldham backs and Deakin grounded the ball in an easy place. A. burst of frantic cheering was nipped in the bud when Isherwood's attempt at goal proved a fiasco. A feverish time followed. The honaesters meant to reduce that two points lead, and soon did it. Taylor secured, and either dropped at goal or kicked across purposely, but the fact remains that the speedy Mason raced up and dropped on the oval before a cherry and white could get near it. Another cheer was followed by a groan as the goal kick failed for the second time. At half time War- rington were a point ahead of Oldham.</p>
        <p>In the first quarter of the second half Warrington made a huge mistake. They started as if content to rest on their laurels,and all the dash was on the Oldham side. The natural consequences of course ensued. Hurst looked all over a scorer when &amp;scow brought him down. The ball was punted out and to the surprise of everyone, 'Thomas dropped a grand goal for Oldham, and the position was reversed.. Before Warrington could re- cover, Lawton took a pass when a yard in front of A. Lees and ran in between the posts,S. Lees again majoris- log. Seven points in five minutes. This settled the matter, but before the close Mason distinguished him- self, by scoring another fine try, but alas ! no goal re- sulted. From now to the end a frantic effort was made to pull the game out or the fire, but all to no purpose, though Oldham were defending continually.</p>
        <p>The bast man on the home side was "little" Moores. He bottled Hurst up very effectively, and moreover put in some really fine runs and punts. Isherwood was very useful on the far wing, but Hallam and Cross only showed with promineece on the defence. The forwards,, to a man, played a,great game, and behind them Bate and Duckworth were as active as bees. Boscow'S knee detracted from his display. In the Oldham,ranks Law- ton and the brothers Lees were continually on the move,. and simply made the play for the wings. Thomas was not in as good form as at Oldham, though remarkably cool. Hurst, as usual, could do nothing but run, and Martin seemed off, altogether. The combination of the whole team was as good as of yore, and contrasted strongly with Warrington's ragged moYements..</p>
        <p>An equally brilliant game may be expected to-day when the Reds of Salford enter the Wilderspool enclo-• sure. Their captain,Tom Wlliams,is about the cleverest Centre in the Northern Union—in fact, the whale of the back division play a beautiful passing game. They are a marvellously improved fifteen since last seasou,and will give Warrington a warm time.</p>
        <p>The following table shows the position of the clubs in he Lancashire Competition :</p>
        <p>For, Agst. P. W. L. D. 4. T. PI G. T. P. p Oldham 18...19... 1... 1...21 51-195...12 12— 60...33, Swinton l6 .14. 1... 1...18 66-204...11 7 43-29 Widnes 18...13... 4... 1...26 51-205... 8 19-- 'l3-27 Broughton Egre 17.-11... 4... 2...26 35-15 r... 9,16- 66,-24 Sal ford 19 .11. 7... 1-22 54-206-20 33-139_23, Warrington 20... 9...10... 1...13 30-116...21 .$l-135...19 Runcorn lB.. 8...10... 0...17 28-112-19 27-119.16 Wigan lB.. 8...10... 0...14 18— 82...14 31-121.-16, Tyldesley ....... 18... 7-11... 0...11 IS,- 824..21 62-188-14. *Lei gh 17... 7... 9... 1...23 28-130...15 28-114...13. St Helene 17... 8.-11... 0...10 21— 83...24 30-141-.12 B:ockport 17... 5...12... 0...16 20— 92...22 43-173...10 Rochdale H'rnetal9... 4.. 15... 0...19 20— 98...24 49-195... 8 Morecambe . 18... 2_16._ 0...11 9 49...27 6)-234... 4. 'Leigh hare had two points dedueted for playing an in- eligible man.</p>
        <p>Among the other games on Saturday Swinton ac- counted for tor Morecambe by only two tries to nil. Widnes gained a c^ttple of points at the expense of Leigh,, but the latter were distinctly unfortunate in having a couple. or men badly injured, and more meritorious was the de- cisive win of Broughton Rangers at Stockport. It is quite on the cards that last year's champions. may after all finish third,and there is no getting away from their splendid form during the last two months. Roehdale Hornets seem to be going from bad to worse, and col- lapsed completely at Wigan. Runcorn sustained a crushing defeat at Salford, notwithstanding thein re- arranged team. St. Helena also have done badly of late, though they were singularly unlucky in going under at Tyldesley.</p>
