      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-01-21_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>PADUATE,</head>
        <p>PADUATE,</p>
        <p>Ft:Nicest, SsamoN.—The funeral sermon of the late Mr. Eliza' Harris was preached on Sunday morning by the Rev. William Potts, of Warrington. There wan a good attendance, a large number of the deceased'S rela- tives being pres Aft. 'The minister in a few remarks at the close of the sermon, said Mr. Harris had been a man of very strongly marked individuality, sad even in the last year of his life no one cpuld ho brought into contact with him without realising that his yea was yea and his nay was nay. He chose his own. pathway through life, and whatever he did he did it with all his heart, with all his mind, with all his soul and with all his strength. How diligent a man be was in business the members of the congregation would know better than he (the minister) did,but it was a blessing to know his diligence did not exhaust itself in a secular form. He was glad to hear a deal of his thoughts were given to the old Sanday school where for many years be was superin- tendent. Mr. Garland presided at the organ. SUNDAT SCHOOL TRA PAIITT.—The chil- dren's tealparty took place at the above Sunday school on Friday evening last week. Tea was provided for the scholars in the school-room in the afternoon, end in the evening ti concert was held presict-d over by Mr, James Oaxaly. Thera ws‘a crowded attendance or parents and friends. The Chaim sn was supported en the pat- term by Mesdames Thornton, Adamson, Hutchings, Misses Edith. Betty, and Flora Bannett, 11. Eaglesfield, Maud London and Edith Richardson. The cnairman de- livered a short address, after which a varied programme of songs, recit itions, dialogues, etc , was crone through by the children in a manner rellacting credit upon the teachers who had prepared the little ones. The prizes for early and reguiar attendance were delivered by Mr. Gandy to a large number of scholars.</p>
